WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.010
- Welcome everybody to the
Public Board of NHS England,

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.690
and welcome also to members of the public

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.813
who are with us today,
physically in the room.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.320
Not as many as I think we'll
get the next meeting and so on,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.980
but it's very good to get back,
I think, into that rhythm.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.980
We received apologies for absence

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.620
for this meeting from Rakesh Kapoor.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.770
Can I just ask if anyone
has any interest to declare

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.570
in addition to those already declared

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.790
or relevant to any of the items

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.990
that we're about to
discuss in this meeting?

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.133
Thank you.

00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:39.150
And then can I ask for
the board's approval

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:43.230
to the minutes of the meeting
held on the 6th of October?

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.520
- Yeah.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.620
- Thank you very much.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.940
If I can just spend a very few minutes

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.663
just on chair's update.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.410
And Amanda I know will set out,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.990
probably in some high level detail,

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.990
I think, the autumn
statement and with Julian,

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.730
but actually I would just like to comment

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.250
on that discussion and agreement

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.790
with Treasury and Number
10 and the Department,

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.630
that the NHS budget clearly
will be 3.3 billion pounds

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.700
higher for the next two years

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.970
compared to the previous
spending review settlement.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.260
And it was also good, I think,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.930
that we saw the chancellor in government

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.580
essentially commit an investment

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.100
of a further 2.7 billion pounds

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.290
in social care over the next two years.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.050
We know from our boundary
issues where health,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.450
the boundary between health
and social care is drawn

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.470
and we particularly see that

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.620
in discharge issues in hospital,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.080
that that sort of extra
funding into social care

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.870
I think, is very sensible.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.470
Clearly the impact of inflation
and additional demand,

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.090
we're seeing increased demand

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.140
coming at our services
for many directions,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.300
means there's still a
significant amount of work

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.790
that we need to continue to do

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.480
to make sure that the right
resources get to the frontline

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.490
to meet those operational pressures

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.030
and to continue to reform
and transform for the future.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.623
But I think that that spending settlement,

00:02:11.623 --> 00:02:13.920
3.3 billion each year
for the next few years,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.010
is a positive result for
the National Health Service

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.500
and it certainly provides
good financial support

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.203
for those next two years.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.237
Within the autumn statement actually,

00:02:24.237 --> 00:02:26.943
and also we talked about
this at the Private Board,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.790
I was really pleased

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.300
to see the Patricia
Hewitt review announced.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.270
So Patricia, as we know, is the chair

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.433
of NHS Norfolk and Waveney
Integrated Care Board,

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.460
and the purpose behind it,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.250
to look at further, how to ensure

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.930
that we've got the
right levels of autonomy

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.090
for our integrated care systems.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.370
I think the direction is absolutely right.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.020
We welcome the review

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.113
and we will work closely with Patricia.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.340
And I think the first report
is due before Christmas,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.397
the interim report, and then a
final report early next year.

00:03:02.397 --> 00:03:04.770
And as I say, we'll work closely with that

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.293
and are wholly supportive of it.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.040
One other aspect, perhaps
not picked up as much,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.180
which I was really pleased to see

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.940
was the appointment actually of Tim Briggs

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.580
to join with Jim Mackey

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.820
essentially on the elective recovery

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.250
and bringing further
clinical input into the way

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.640
that we're looking at how
do we get after the problem

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.150
of the elective backlog,
the 7.1 million people

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.670
currently sort of on the waiting list.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.185
And Tim is chair of the Getting It Right

00:03:35.185 --> 00:03:38.880
First Time programme, a
role he will also continue,

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.290
and already works closely
with the elective backlog,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.880
the elective recovery programme.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:45.777
But I was really pleased to see that

00:03:45.777 --> 00:03:48.210
and I've had a number of meetings with Tim

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.310
over the past weeks and months actually.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.250
So I think that was a very good move.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:57.450
I would just like to call out
and welcome Sarah-Jane Marsh,

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.570
who's been appointed as
deputy chief operating officer

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.780
and the national director now
for urgent and emergency care,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.070
and she's currently the chief executive

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:09.270
of Birmingham Women's and
Children's NHS Foundation Trust

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.580
and she'll be leading on the
transformation with David

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.520
on our urgent and emergency care services

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.966
and she'll work alongside Pauline Philip

00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:20.673
during her transition and handover period.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.100
The merger, as I shorthand call it.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.230
So the board is very much aware

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:32.010
and very focused on
how we give good effect

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:37.010
to merging the entities at
the top of the structure,

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.140
So NHS England, Improvement,
Digital, X and HEE.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.853
And under Wol's chairmanship,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.968
that committee and the
work led by Mark Cubbon,

00:04:49.968 --> 00:04:52.053
I think, is proceeding well.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.697
So it's moving its speed,
but it's a complex execution.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.650
There are 22,600 roles effectively

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.223
and we're bringing that
together into one organisation.

00:05:04.223 --> 00:05:06.090
We look at how we reconvene

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.660
and reconfigure the programmes that we run

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.870
and clearly there's a
headcount consequence

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.400
of between 30 and 40%,

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.640
but that's not, as the board
knows, our main purpose.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.830
The main purpose is
about decentralisation,

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.860
debureaucratization, and
it goes back to the ICS,

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.000
is how do we make sure
that we can help the ICSs

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.000
become more autonomous as they
themselves grow in maturity.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.420
Now I raise it because actually

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.890
we have accelerated the
merger with NHS Digital

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.130
at the request of the Department
and the Secretary of State

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:45.240
and we're looking now to
effect that merger with Digital

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.540
by 31st of January, I think
is now the go-live date,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:51.660
but a very significant
additional pressure on the team,

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.020
on Wol and Mark and the
broader team looking at this

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.210
and all of the executives

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.900
and on the NHS Digital Board led by Laura.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.080
But it's something we're
going to achieve successfully

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.605
but it has put significant extra pressure

00:06:06.605 --> 00:06:09.540
upon the programme.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.000
I'd just like to comment
briefly if I might,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.360
on new non-executive directors.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:20.280
So we've been engaged for
several months now on a process

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.340
of appointing a number of
new non-executive directors

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.410
to the board, having undertaken

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:28.653
and advertised recruitment
process back in the summer.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:33.360
We are now, I hope, in
the final stages of that

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.610
and getting closer to appointment

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.280
and I certainly recently
received good reassurance

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.930
that we are in those final stages

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.580
so I think that would be helpful.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.000
And I'll just use that as a key

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.940
to thank colleagues on the
board because actually,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.830
there's a huge amount of
work being carried out,

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.990
not just in the board but
in all of the committees.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.010
And we would welcome actually

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.440
the appointment of new
non-executive directors

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.963
as soon as we can get that done.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.793
Just a couple of other points, if I might.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.460
I've had a number of
engagements with Chris Hopson

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.100
with communities of stakeholders.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:16.100
So we're communicating
much more frequently,

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.110
consulting with them, taking
their views, sharing our views

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.480
on a range of really
quite important topics.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.930
I've continued also to meet
with many of the Royal Colleges

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:28.170
Think Tanks, patient
representative groups,

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.960
either in formalised stakeholder sessions

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.670
where we bring the community together

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.460
or in individual bilateral meetings.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.230
I particularly valued on Monday actually

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.300
an opportunity to go to the
London Ambulance Service

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:40.133
and actually to meet

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:42.450
with the Black and minority ethnic staff

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:44.847
from a range of roles within
the London Ambulance Service.

00:07:44.847 --> 00:07:46.560
And I actually appreciated hugely

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.663
them taking their time out
of their very busy lives,

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.490
to tell me about the experiences

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:56.250
that they are currently seeing

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:00.480
and what they're experiencing
while working in the service.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.568
I recently visited Croydon
University Hospital,

00:08:03.568 --> 00:08:06.660
did a tour of the elective
centre, dedicated surgical wards,

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:11.070
the emergency department, and
the community rapid response.

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:14.400
And I was well hosted, a
delight actually to be met

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:15.900
and hosted by Mike Bell as the chair

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.390
and Matthew Kershaw as the CEO.

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:19.530
We had very good conversations

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.030
about how they've seen the performance

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:24.990
and how it's been improving in Croydon.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:27.840
A couple of times I've been
to the North East London

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.370
Health and Care Partnership actually,

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:34.370
where Marie Gabriel is
chair designated to the ICS.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:35.460
One thing to call out,

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.920
which actually struck me very powerfully,

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.750
I visited one of the
primary care practises,

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.270
which is the Woodgrange Medical Centre,

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.820
which has about 16,000 patients

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.310
and expected to go up
to about 20,000 patients

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.200
in the next couple of years.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.410
Now this is a community, the broader area,

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:57.300
Newham, Barking and Dagenham,
which has lots of deprivation,

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.370
lots of challenges to it.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.800
I think two out of nine of the population

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.590
has English as a first language.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.230
So it has lots and lots
of challenges to it.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.320
But I had a really powerful visit

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.590
and then did a sort of conversation

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.570
with the frontline staff,
about an hour and a half.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.100
And I then went back actually,

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.887
because the range of questions
that they wanted to ask

00:09:19.887 --> 00:09:22.020
and the range of views they
wanted to share with me,

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.360
and these were representatives
from nursing and midwifery

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:29.360
and occupational therapy, GPs, doctors,

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.410
so the full range, if you like, of skills

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.770
both within the hospitals and
within the primary community

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.360
made for me, and it was
quite selfish of me,

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.990
but it made for a very good
and interesting exchange.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.690
I was hugely impressed, as I always am,

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.670
with the commitment and enthusiasm

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:49.670
in spite of the pressures
of our frontline staff.

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:54.660
I was also up in Humber
and North Yorkshire ICS

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.280
where I met with Sue Symington, the chair,

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:57.420
and Stephen Eames, the CEO,

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.100
and met with the board actually,

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.280
and then visited the York
Hospital Emergency Department.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:03.810
I would say, and I
shared it with the board,

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.300
that all the pressures that we hear about

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.840
and we also see in other
visits, were stark actually,

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.260
and the team at York
were doing an amazing job

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.720
under huge pressure of demand.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:19.740
So it had been a very,
very difficult weekend

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.070
and Monday night, and I went
in on the Tuesday morning

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:24.120
and going to ED,

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.390
there were I think five
trolleys outside ED,

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:30.570
one of which was occupied
by a youngish man

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.360
and the other four were occupied
by very elderly patients.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:35.940
And in the ED itself,

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.770
I didn't count the number of cubicles,

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.340
but I guess around 15, one
of them had curtains drawn,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:42.720
so I don't know who was in there,

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:44.940
but all of the other 14

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:46.890
were occupied by very elderly patients,

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.980
essentially waiting to
go up into the hospital

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:51.630
into beds when they became available.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.210
So I saw very visibly the pressures

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:57.270
that we're under in our hospitals.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.120
But again, a compliment
to the people I met,

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.880
both in the ICS and also
in the hospital actually.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.610
It was a powerful visit.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:10.380
And I happened to visit their
new endoscopy department,

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.960
which was remarkable actually,

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.180
and has been a brilliant step forward

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.793
in their diagnostic capability.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.940
Recently, NHS Confederation,
they had the ICS network,

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.620
I was delighted to be invited

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.680
to go both to the dinner
and then to speak.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:29.700
It was a real opportunity

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:32.790
to meet a whole range
of different ICS leaders

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:33.750
and to share views.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:35.250
And that was particularly relevant

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:36.290
'cause it was after the announcement

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:38.973
of the Patricia Hewitt review.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:44.010
I recently went to the Oxford
Health Foundation Trust,

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.260
Warneford Mental Health campus,

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:47.760
David Walker, the trust chair,

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:50.880
and Nick Broughton, the
CEO, Professor John Geddes,

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.460
director of the Biomedical Research Centre

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:56.430
and I saw their vision
for their new campus

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:57.927
and the adolescent mental health unit

00:11:57.927 --> 00:11:59.760
and the clinical research facility.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.730
But I also saw actually, they work

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.940
and live in an extremely
old 19th century building.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:09.120
Remarkable, and yet in spite
of that, they provide, I think,

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:13.410
very good service to the
population and the people.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.320
And then we've also held a
primary care round table.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.020
We're doing these on a
relatively regular basis,

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:21.990
about every six weeks,
where I get the chance

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:26.160
to engage with frontline
primary care practitioners

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:29.672
and Amanda Doyle hosts those with me.

00:12:29.672 --> 00:12:30.752
And I think that's really interesting

00:12:30.752 --> 00:12:32.880
if you look at the
challenges in primary care,

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.490
particularly as we think
about the next year,

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:38.913
but also the 24/25 contract.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.150
Last thing just to mention, if I might,

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:46.440
is the long-term workforce plan,

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:49.500
I said in the past that
the bedevilling challenge

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.573
across many issues is workforce,

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:56.310
the number of vacancies or
the pressure on the workforce.

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:59.880
And so we have made very
significant progress

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.830
under Navina's leadership,

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:05.040
Navina Evans' leadership
with Amanda and Julian,

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:07.380
myself and Jeremy and
David Behan and others.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.480
And so thank you for all
your contribution to it,

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.560
and we're in, I think,
pretty good final ish stages

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:15.510
on a plan, I think, which
has real merit to it.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:17.010
We discussed it again
at the Private Board.

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:19.590
We're going to do a deep dive as a board

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:23.160
into the underlying factors
and how they play out.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:25.560
But for me it's a really,
really important piece of work.

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:27.600
And of course we saw in
the autumn statement,

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:30.150
unsurprisingly given the
chancellor is Jeremy Hunt

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:31.590
and he's always been very keen

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:33.690
to see us produce this piece of work

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:35.430
that actually also government commits

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:38.225
that we will do this
long-term workforce plan

00:13:38.225 --> 00:13:40.260
and it will be published
sometime next year,

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:42.360
I think sometime in spring of next year.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:43.680
But we're well advanced on it,

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:46.350
and I would compliment the team

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:47.547
who have been doing all the work

00:13:47.547 --> 00:13:49.020
and all of the modelling.

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:50.760
And just to share lastly on that,

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:53.460
and they've done it not just
in isolation themselves,

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:56.370
but they have engaged
with a very broad range

00:13:56.370 --> 00:14:01.260
of constituencies and think
tanks and colleges, ICS leaders.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:03.930
So actually it's gathered input

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:05.790
on the underlying assumptions,

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:09.210
but in the end it has ambition
to it, which I think we need

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:12.930
given the pressures we see
today in the workforce.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:16.320
So I will probably stop at that point.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:19.260
I haven't talked about industrial action,

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:24.260
but I know Amanda will
briefly talk to that I'm sure

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.450
in her chief executives report

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:29.370
and the preparations that have been made

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:32.733
to try and deal with the
industrial action as it occurs.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:37.410
So any reflections on anything I said

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:40.530
or should we go straight
to the chief execs report?

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:41.430
I can see a desire

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:42.690
to go straight to the chief execs report.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:43.590
Very good.

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:44.580
Amanda.
- Great.

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:46.650
Richard, thank you very much indeed.

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.830
So look, I will try and avoid
repeating some of the things

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:51.540
that Richard said, but just to allow me

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:56.540
to add my thanks to his to
Mark and Wol in particular,

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:00.810
but also to Tim and to
Navina and to Simon Bolton

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:01.920
for all the work that they've been doing

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:04.080
around the new NHS England

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.240
and in particular, supporting colleagues

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:08.250
through what has been a very challenging,

00:15:08.250 --> 00:15:10.710
difficult, rapid process.

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:12.720
But as Richard says,

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:14.850
a very clear set of ambitions there

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.290
for us to be, you know,

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.770
a better leaner organisation
at the end of this.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:23.940
Also like just to congratulate
not just Tim and Sarah-Jane,

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:26.460
as Richard said, but also Mark,

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:28.710
on being appointed as chief executive

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:33.450
of Manchester University Foundation Trust.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:35.190
We will be very sorry to lose you Mark,

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:36.510
but we're not losing you straight away

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:38.760
'cause you'll be here till
the end of the financial year.

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:42.000
But also really delighted
that Steve Russell has agreed

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.410
to then pick up the leadership
of the CDO directorate.

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:50.370
So thank you Steve and thank you Mark.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.800
So just moving on then,

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.260
Richard, you very helpfully
set out the headlines

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:56.910
from the autumn statement.

00:15:56.910 --> 00:16:00.300
I think perhaps just worth
again for the record,

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:01.470
just setting out,

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:04.710
I know Julian will talk about
the detail a little bit later,

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.830
but it does mean for
us that the NHS budget

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:11.850
will be 3.3 billion higher
for the next two years

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:14.280
compared with the previous SR settlement.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:17.370
And of course, again, as Richard noted,

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:19.590
hugely welcomed the announcement

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:21.630
about the additional 2.7 billion

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:25.440
being invested in social
care over the next two years.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.590
The impact of inflation
and additional demand

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:32.610
does mean that a significant
amount of work remains

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:35.610
to ensure the right
resources reach the frontline

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.290
to meet operational pressures

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:40.650
and to continue to transform
services for the future.

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:42.990
But I think we would say, you know,

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:46.860
that is a positive place
for the NHS to find itself

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:49.800
because it does give us some
really welcome certainty

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:51.930
about what the next two years brings

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:55.893
and it has allowed us to recommit
as a consequence to that,

00:16:55.893 --> 00:16:57.120
to a number of things.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:16:59.640
Again, with sort of permission, Board,

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.430
I will just set out again,
partly because I think

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.530
it is worth us just being really cognizant

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:08.070
of what we have said.

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:12.540
So what we have said is that
we will work together now

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.450
to develop a strategic recovery plan

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.160
for urgent emergency care services

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.110
to be published in the new year.

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:21.750
In that, we will be focusing

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:26.010
on improving ambulance response
times as a headline metric

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:30.360
and also year on year
improvements in A&amp;E waiting times.

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:32.400
That I think is something that again,

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.440
we know is very front
of mind for the public.

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:36.300
I'll talk a bit more
about what's happening

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:37.197
'cause there's a huge amount going on

00:17:37.197 --> 00:17:39.750
and David will cover that in some detail.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.850
But I think that gives us the opportunity

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:43.470
to think beyond the immediate,

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:46.590
to really start to set out
what we think that longer term

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.830
transformational recovery
journey will look like.

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:52.590
Secondly, we've committed
to do something similar

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:57.590
for primary care, again with
a particular focus on access

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:01.863
and that, as people will recall
from the plan for patients

00:18:01.863 --> 00:18:04.800
that the government
published earlier this year,

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:08.340
focused particularly on patients

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:11.370
who needed getting an
appointment on the same day

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:12.990
and those who needed
getting an appointment

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:14.310
within two weeks.

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.560
I think the thing that we
would also all recognise

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.990
is the importance of ease of access,

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:20.940
so ability to get through on the phone,

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.640
make appointments in a way
that's convenient for patients,

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:25.680
being an important
component of that as well.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.690
So we welcome the opportunity
to work on those things,

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:33.180
as I say, with the view
publication in the new year.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:34.440
We've also recommitted

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.470
on the back of the funding announcement

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:40.830
to the ambition set out in
the elective recovery plan,

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:45.720
in particular, around continuing
to focus on the long wait.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.570
So that's next big milestone for the NHS

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:52.050
is achieving 78 week maximum wait

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:52.980
with the exception of course

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.680
of patients for whom choice or complexity

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:59.790
would preclude that, by the
end of this financial year.

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:03.030
And also again, in the statement,

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:06.780
recommitting to the offer
of choice around that,

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:10.530
both for patients who are on the journey

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:12.810
and we know that has been something

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:14.340
that has been particularly valuable

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:15.390
for those parts of the country

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:17.310
that have been very capacity constrained,

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:19.680
the ability to offer
patients the opportunity

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:22.020
if they want it, to move between providers

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:23.910
and indeed even move out of region

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.220
but also to offer choice,
a point of referral,

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:29.067
which is something that we, I think again,

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:30.990
are very committed to doing.

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:33.180
So that's another component.

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.000
Richard's rightly just called
out two other important things

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.880
which is I think we
were delighted to hear,

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:42.660
again, the commitment to
publication of the workforce plan

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:45.930
and finally really
welcomed the announcement

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:48.600
of the work that Patricia
Hewitt is going to be doing,

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:50.670
which I think really
does build on the work

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:54.030
that Mark and others have led
on the NHS operating framework

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.730
to think about how now that we have ICBs

00:19:56.730 --> 00:20:00.330
as statutory entities, we
can really take the next step

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:03.540
in helping them to become
increasingly accountable

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:06.870
and also taking a greater
degree of local autonomy

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:09.150
to deliver for their population.

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:11.940
So really welcome both of those things.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:14.640
I should just say that whilst
not specifically mentioned

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.430
in the autumn statement,
we are also committed

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:21.240
to maintaining the mental
health service expansion

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:24.330
and funding growth as set
out in the long term plan

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:26.430
and the focus on early cancer diagnosis.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.350
So that's relevant for the conversation

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:30.150
we're going to be having
a little bit later.

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:35.150
So I think what just remains
for me to say about that

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:37.920
before Julian picks up on the detail later

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:42.570
is it really, I think in all of this,

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:43.920
I've talked about the
importance of making sure

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.480
the money gets to the frontline

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:49.980
and is there to enable people
to deliver and transform,

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:53.880
but it absolutely must
keep our focus on the need

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:57.420
to make sure that we are
evidencing value for money

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:00.960
because every pound we spend
is a pound of public money

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:02.370
that we have an absolute responsibility

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:04.470
to make sure we spend well.

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:09.000
So we have again, recommitted
to the efficiency savings.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:12.420
So that's 12 billion across
the three-year SL period

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:16.020
and indeed, potentially
going even further than that

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:18.150
in some areas should the
opportunity arise to.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:19.500
You might want to talk about that

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:21.750
but we certainly won't be at the moment

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.150
passing on any additional
efficiencies to the frontline.

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:25.500
As I say, we're committed to making sure

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:27.750
that the frontline gets
the funding it needs

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:31.800
to be able to have the
best possible opportunity

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:33.750
to deliver against that
set of very ambitious

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.423
but very important ambitions.

00:21:38.401 --> 00:21:39.720
So I think that that probably covers it

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.970
in terms of the autumn statement.

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:43.740
I think the other things
I wanted to talk about

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:47.790
was a little bit more on the here and now.

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:52.790
So we are, I suspect all of
us, are now very conscious

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:57.330
of the level of pressure
that we are seeing

00:21:57.330 --> 00:21:59.280
across the whole of the NHS.

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:01.140
We're seeing staff working as ever

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.360
unbelievably hard day in, day out

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:05.777
to do their absolute
best for their patients.

00:22:05.777 --> 00:22:09.330
But we are conscious that
we are likely to have,

00:22:09.330 --> 00:22:11.070
in fact, been formally announced

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:14.280
that we will be having
periods of industrial action

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.230
including this side of Christmas.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:19.467
So Navina, David, others, I know

00:22:19.467 --> 00:22:22.230
are going to be talking a bit
more about this in a moment,

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.750
but clearly whilst pay is a matter

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:27.000
for the trade unions and government,

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.490
part of our job is to make
sure we are supporting the NHS

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:31.620
to plan as effectively as possible

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:34.620
so that we are minimising
disruption to patients

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.630
over that period of time.

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:39.660
But also to make sure that we
are doing everything we can

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.760
to support our patients and our staff

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:42.593
through what is a, you know,

00:22:42.593 --> 00:22:44.913
going to be inevitably a difficult period.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:48.450
We have, at the same time

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:51.060
as thinking about some of those pressures,

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:52.710
I think the thing I just
wanted to talk about

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:55.557
was also some of the
progress that has been made

00:22:55.557 --> 00:22:57.120
and that we have seen.

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:00.780
particularly since publication
of the two winter letters,

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:03.570
one back in August and
then one more recently.

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:07.080
So it's been really good.

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.550
Richard just talked about
some of the visits he's done.

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:12.090
All my colleagues have had,
I suspect, similar stories

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:13.677
I know we have, about places we've been

00:23:13.677 --> 00:23:15.510
and some brilliant things that we've seen.

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.090
So at risk of all, it's always difficult

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:19.170
to call out any one place

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:20.190
because you're always conscious

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.020
of all the places you haven't called out,

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:25.260
but the things that we
highlighted in winter

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:27.540
that we were really encouraging

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.120
and supporting the NHS to deliver

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:31.800
because we could see
the evidence was there

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:33.570
that was going to make an impact,

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.520
the biggest impact for our patients,

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:37.140
I've just got a few I wanted to call out.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:40.440
So community-based fall service,

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:43.290
the brilliant work
that's happening in Hull,

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:44.580
the Hull first model,

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:48.120
bringing together ambulance,
acute, community, NHS teams

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:50.790
with fire services, councils, and others

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:53.340
to provide that rapid pickup service

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:55.860
for people who haven't suffered injury.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:59.310
Virtual wards like in Norfolk and Norwich

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:03.600
where their virtual model has
already saved 12,000 bed days

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:08.430
with a 98% satisfaction
rate from their patients.

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:11.400
Acute respiratory hubs like Sandwell,

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:13.410
where more than 8,000 families

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:16.350
have been given a same-day
alternative to A&amp;E

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:19.290
so effective that three
more are now being opened

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:20.880
in the Black country.

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:22.620
Support for care homes.

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:26.130
So like the One Weston care home hub

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:28.950
bringing together primary
community services,

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:31.680
substantially reducing
emergency admissions

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:35.220
and ambulance call outs, but
also making a real impact

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:36.960
on people's ability to
do things that matter,

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.020
like die in their place of choice

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:43.380
rather than in a acute hospital,

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:46.260
24/7 mental health capability

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:49.440
in ambulance operations
rooms and on the roads

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:52.200
like in Yorkshire and
Northwest Ambulance Services

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:54.780
where they've worked with
local mental health trusts,

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:56.820
but also partners like the police,

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:59.700
so that they can get the most
appropriate trained support

00:24:59.700 --> 00:25:02.880
quickly to people experiencing
mental health crisis.

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:05.100
And finally, last but not least,

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:09.390
24/7 system control centres
like the one in Kent &amp; Medway

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:12.240
where it's pulling
together real-time data,

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:15.960
not for the process of analysing it

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:18.840
or doing any sort of management
stuff in the background,

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:21.420
it's for real-time
clinical decision-making

00:25:21.420 --> 00:25:24.480
and we can already see the
difference that that is making.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:26.310
So just a few examples, I think,

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:28.950
of where we can see
this is not just words,

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:30.480
it is real action being taken

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:32.580
by our colleagues across the country.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:34.380
And I just want to say on behalf,

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:35.880
I think, of probably of all of us,

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:38.700
thank you for all the work
that colleagues are doing

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.310
to do the, as I say, absolute
best for their patients

00:25:41.310 --> 00:25:43.593
over what we recognise
it's a difficult time.

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:47.430
Couple of other things that
we've done since we last met

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:51.360
that I think is worth
noting in the sort of spirit

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:53.850
of getting a balance to
our discussions today

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:55.950
between some of the stuff that
we rightly need to focus on

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:57.960
where it's very challenging
in some of the areas

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.180
where actually really good
progress it's being made.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:04.260
We have launched the first
NHS genomics strategy

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:07.350
that included rolling out
whole genome sequencing

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:09.690
for seriously ill children and babies.

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:11.640
Because we are a single NHS,

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:13.020
we could make that announcement

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:15.780
and it could be done
for the whole of the NHS

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:18.900
for every patient who
needs it the same day.

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:21.360
And that for me was just
a wonderful example,

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:23.013
having met some of the babies and families

00:26:23.013 --> 00:26:26.160
that have benefited from
that of what we can do

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:27.720
because of the way that we are structured

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:28.890
and the way that we run.

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:31.440
And a big thank you to colleagues
in specialised services

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:34.020
who are such an important part of that.

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:36.270
Direct access to diagnostic testing.

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:38.430
Again, we've made that
announcement recently,

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:39.270
allowing people

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:41.340
just to go straight to
where they need a test,

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.410
particularly for those patients

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:48.120
who have a set of worrying symptoms

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:49.395
but don't neatly fit

00:26:49.395 --> 00:26:51.600
one of the two week wait cancer pathways

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.483
so that they're able to
get straight into testing,

00:26:55.369 --> 00:26:57.420
rather than having to
go through other routes.

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:59.010
So just some of the things that we've done

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:01.020
since we were last together.

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:03.990
I think then all I want
to say before I stop

00:27:03.990 --> 00:27:05.890
is I think we would recognise I think

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:08.460
as we get towards
Christmas that, you know,

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:12.750
this has been a, I think
Steve said it at an event

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:14.928
we were at the other day, you
said you always thought COVID

00:27:14.928 --> 00:27:18.709
would be a five-year, you
know, five year experience

00:27:18.709 --> 00:27:20.250
and we are halfway through.

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:22.350
So what we're in is we're in a year,

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:24.810
first year probably now, of recovery,

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:29.400
but it's going to be a journey

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:31.290
and it's not going to happen overnight.

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:35.910
So I think we always knew this
was going to be challenging

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:39.900
but we have done some things
that we should be proud of

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:42.750
and we should reflect back
on at the end of the year,

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:45.000
I think with real pride across the NHS.

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:48.990
We have completely recovered cancer checks

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:51.600
that we know didn't
happen over the pandemic

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:53.190
because people didn't come forward

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:55.380
in the main over that period.

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:58.410
We have also recovered
the number of people

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:01.500
who therefore are being diagnosed
at stage one and stage two

00:28:01.500 --> 00:28:03.180
and that matters so much.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:06.150
That's a real goal that
we're going for here.

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:09.570
General practice is
delivering record numbers

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:13.260
of appointments and we have
made really significant inroads

00:28:13.260 --> 00:28:17.340
on elective care, virtually
eliminating 104 week waits,

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:18.930
and now pulling out all the stops

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:21.240
to get to 78 weeks by the end of the year.

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:24.990
So next year is the
NHS's 75th anniversary.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:28.320
So that recovery will need
to continue into next year.

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:31.470
But we will also be focusing,
continue with my colleagues

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:34.470
to focus on those
transformative things that mean

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:36.630
that whilst we've been a
constantly evolving service

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:40.980
for the last 74 1/2 years,
we are setting ourselves up

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:45.570
to continue to be a
transformative reforming service

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:47.460
for the next, well who knows?

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:49.053
75 or more.

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:52.350
- [Richard] Probably beyond my time.

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:53.183
- Beyond mine too.

00:28:53.183 --> 00:28:54.016
- Yeah.

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:56.123
Thank you.

00:28:56.123 --> 00:28:59.340
Any comments or questions for Amanda

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:01.623
before we move to operational performance?

00:29:02.796 --> 00:29:05.100
- Can I just say how
important it is I think

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:07.770
to actually just draw attention

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:11.910
to all those examples of improved practise

00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:15.870
where response times are
improving, et cetera,

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:17.310
and sharing your examples

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:19.860
of where innovation is taking place.

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:21.450
I do believe we've got to acknowledge

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:24.120
the kind of reality of
the situation we're in,

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:26.070
but I also think it's a leadership task

00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:29.070
to hold out hope for the future.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:30.810
And I really encourage you

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:33.270
to carry on with the narrative, Amanda,

00:29:33.270 --> 00:29:35.797
of acknowledging reality but also saying,

00:29:35.797 --> 00:29:38.550
"Well, we're changing things
in quite a significant way."

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:39.510
I think it's terrific.

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:40.343
Thank you.

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:44.280
- I just endorse that David,

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:46.590
if I might and and just sort of well said,

00:29:46.590 --> 00:29:49.840
clearly we're under very,
very significant pressure

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:55.440
and demand is up, you know,
demand is going up and up and up

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:57.660
as the population ages,

00:29:57.660 --> 00:29:59.610
grows and ages,

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:01.770
and we get comorbidities
in the older population

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:03.060
and then we have obesity coming,

00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:05.790
we can see that with very high percentages

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:07.830
of the adult population are obese,

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:09.600
and actually we also see
that in children we see,

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:11.310
I think there was a
report that was put out,

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:13.500
was it on Monday or
Tuesday this week, I think,

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:17.130
that said that 18% of 16 to 24-year-olds

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:19.800
now are suffering mental health challenges

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:22.830
and that was an NHS established

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:24.360
piece of research and report.

00:30:24.360 --> 00:30:28.020
So we're seeing very
significant growth in the demand

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:33.020
and I think that some of those
examples that you shared,

00:30:33.240 --> 00:30:34.770
I think they are a really good example

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:37.740
of how we are absolutely
trying unstintingly

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:40.230
to meet that demand but
the demand is very real.

00:30:40.230 --> 00:30:41.280
- Yeah.
- And the growth

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:42.870
in that demand is very real.

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:45.330
But I would again echo your words

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:47.667
and thank, actually not
just the frontline staff

00:30:47.667 --> 00:30:49.230
but the frontline staff and all the people

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:52.710
who enable the frontline staff,
technologists, et cetera,

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:55.380
all who enable the frontline staff

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:58.440
to I think continue to
make unstinting efforts

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:00.903
to provide healthcare to the population.

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:04.080
So thank you for raising that.

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:07.440
Now I think we're going to
go to a performance update

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:09.660
and I think, David, are you
going to lead off on operations

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:11.880
and then Julian on
financials, is that right?

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:14.490
So won't page to in the report,

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:16.050
I'll assume everybody has read it,

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:18.120
but in a way this report I think

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:19.950
does reflect the
conversation we've just had,

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:21.990
which is a system which is under pressure,

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:23.340
of a system which is responding

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:24.840
and a system that is innovating

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.360
which is I think what we're
collectively aiming to do.

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:29.022
I'm going to touch briefly
on where we are with COVID

00:31:29.022 --> 00:31:31.260
and then draw attention to a few areas.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:32.430
I won't be touching on cancer

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:34.230
because we have our subject matter experts

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:35.880
in the room right here this afternoon,

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:38.250
nor maternity and neonatal services.

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:39.540
But since we last met

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:42.630
we have come out of the
fourth wave of COVID.

00:31:42.630 --> 00:31:46.110
Nonetheless we still do
have over 4,700 people

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:48.002
in our beds with COVID

00:31:48.002 --> 00:31:50.070
and I think that's quite a
sound free set of data there

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:54.690
that we've always had between
3,800 and 17,000 people

00:31:54.690 --> 00:31:56.940
in our beds with COVID at any one point

00:31:56.940 --> 00:31:58.490
during the course of this year.

00:31:59.538 --> 00:32:00.450
So that's where we're at at the moment.

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:02.100
But I'll hand over to Professor Powers

00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:03.360
to take a bit of a forward look

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:05.340
about where we think COVID
and respiratory disease

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:07.290
might be going over the
winter months ahead.

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:08.730
Steve.
- Yeah, thanks David.

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:11.340
Well, let me start with
flu rather than COVID

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:13.560
because last Friday, the
chief medical officer

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:15.480
and the chief pharmaceutical officer

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:18.960
issued the winter flu letter
which authorises primary care

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:22.200
to begin prescribing antivirals for flu.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:23.850
And in essence that recognises

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:26.400
that we are now in this
winter's flu season

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:27.870
and indeed, over the last week or two,

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:31.860
we've seen significant rises
in flu cases in the community

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:35.130
and that is translating
into significant numbers

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:36.600
or significant increases

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:39.060
in patients being admitted into hospitals.

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:40.740
And we're seeing particular
pressure at the moment

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:44.070
in our children's services,
in paediatric intensive care,

00:32:44.070 --> 00:32:45.750
paediatric general beds, that's flu

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:47.940
but it's also a range of
other respiratory illnesses

00:32:47.940 --> 00:32:49.380
that affect children as well.

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.000
And Ruth might want to say a little bit

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:54.120
about how we're responding
to that, in a minute.

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:56.670
Now flu has come in December this year.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:58.380
That is not totally unusual.

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:02.430
The last flu season we had in 2019/2020

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:06.507
was indeed a December flu
season, peaking around Christmas.

00:33:06.507 --> 00:33:09.240
And we expect it, because we
haven't seen significant flu

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:10.073
for the last two years,

00:33:10.073 --> 00:33:12.030
that we might get an earlier flu season

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:14.700
as indeed happened in
the southern hemisphere

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:17.880
in the summer and in
their winter obviously.

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:21.150
So my expectation is that
as we move towards Christmas

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:24.630
we will be seeing increasing
levels of flu in the community

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:28.290
and increasing cases of patients with flu,

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:29.400
other respiratory illnesses

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:32.070
that need to be admitted into hospital.

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:33.900
Now, on COVID, as you say David,

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:36.300
we have just come out of the fourth wave,

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:40.623
but in fact this week we
have started to see increases

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:42.870
in the number of new diagnoses

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:46.047
and admissions in hospitals week on week.

00:33:46.047 --> 00:33:47.910
And that is beginning to mean

00:33:47.910 --> 00:33:51.660
that occupancy of patients with COVID

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:54.510
is starting to go back on the rise again.

00:33:54.510 --> 00:33:57.750
There is a new variant circulating, BQ.1,

00:33:57.750 --> 00:34:00.660
which is becoming the
dominant variant in the UK,

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:04.157
another sub-lineage,
another subtype of Omicron

00:34:05.713 --> 00:34:06.990
and it seems likely

00:34:06.990 --> 00:34:08.850
that that is going to
drive further increases.

00:34:08.850 --> 00:34:10.590
In fact if you look into Europe

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:14.820
where some countries are a bit
ahead in the growth of BQ.1,

00:34:14.820 --> 00:34:18.420
you could already see increases
in hospital admissions.

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:21.300
I think it's more uncertain
the scale of increases

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:24.690
that we will see over the
next few months with COVID,

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:26.580
but I think it is absolutely clear

00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:30.570
that the next move is going to
be back up rather than down.

00:34:30.570 --> 00:34:32.430
And that does mean unfortunately

00:34:32.430 --> 00:34:34.530
that there is a realistic possibility

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:39.330
that we will see both COVID
and flu rising at the same time

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:41.880
as we move towards Christmas.

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:44.100
The system is under significant
pressure at the moment

00:34:44.100 --> 00:34:45.270
and that obviously means

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:47.970
that we are going to
see pressure increasing.

00:34:47.970 --> 00:34:50.400
Amanda has outlined all
the advanced planning

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:51.720
that we have done

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:55.170
but no doubt those
pressures will increase.

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:57.000
Of course there's still
things that the public can do

00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:59.070
in addition to the work we've been doing

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:01.500
and it's not too late
to get a flu booster,

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:03.690
the flu vaccine this winter
if you haven't had one

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:06.960
and it's not too late
to get the COVID booster

00:35:06.960 --> 00:35:08.490
if you haven't had it.

00:35:08.490 --> 00:35:11.100
Those are important
public health messages,

00:35:11.100 --> 00:35:13.320
important actions that public can take

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:14.400
to try and protect themselves

00:35:14.400 --> 00:35:16.530
and of course take the pressure off.

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:18.510
And then finally it's winter

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:20.970
and we see other respiratory illnesses,

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:25.669
RSV in children, for example,
bacterial pneumonias.

00:35:25.669 --> 00:35:26.940
And of course the other concern we've got

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:28.710
is that people won't turn their heating up

00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:30.300
quite to the extent that they usually do

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:32.250
because of the cost of living crisis.

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:35.880
And if people are colder
and we get cold snaps,

00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:38.940
then it's likely that we
will see increased admissions

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:40.620
because of that as well.

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:43.560
So I think, in summary,

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:45.360
we're heading into a difficult period.

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:46.710
A lot of work has gone on

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:49.710
because we expected there
will be a difficult period,

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.690
more the public can do
in terms of vaccination

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:53.250
but it's going to be challenging.

00:35:53.250 --> 00:35:54.240
- Okay, thank you, Steve.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:55.073
And Ruth, is there
anything you want to add?

00:35:55.073 --> 00:35:58.530
- Just a little bit on
children and young people.

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:01.590
As Steve said, we've got increasing RSV

00:36:01.590 --> 00:36:03.570
which is the children's respiratory virus

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:06.130
and of course we see that every year

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:09.630
except for the immediate
post-pandemic year.

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:11.460
But we are now at the position

00:36:11.460 --> 00:36:14.370
of the highest for the last five years

00:36:14.370 --> 00:36:18.960
with critical care capacity
being at 99% across England.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:20.610
Simon Kenny, our lead clinician,

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:23.220
and all of the children
and young people clinicians

00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:26.280
across England, and indeed across the UK

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:29.670
are working collaboratively,
they're working together

00:36:29.670 --> 00:36:33.390
to make sure that we are
able to transport patients

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:36.240
where we need them to get
the care that we need.

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:37.447
But it is really challenging

00:36:37.447 --> 00:36:40.020
and we're not yet at the position

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:44.910
of seeing the RSV numbers go down,

00:36:44.910 --> 00:36:46.920
but we're working hard on it.

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:47.753
- Okay, thank you.

00:36:47.753 --> 00:36:48.660
Thanks, Steve, thank you, Ruth.

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:49.770
And of course, as we've said,

00:36:49.770 --> 00:36:51.180
the most potent arrow in our quiver

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:53.640
is the vaccination programme
for both COVID and flu,

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:56.340
so that's covered in paras
81 to 84 in my report

00:36:56.340 --> 00:36:58.980
and Steve Russell will be happy
to put questions of detail

00:36:58.980 --> 00:37:00.750
when we get to the questions session,

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:02.370
if that would be helpful.

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:04.350
Three other areas I'd touch on.

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:09.350
Elective care is covered in
paras 14 to 19 in my report.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:10.923
You will note there, as Amanda has

00:37:10.923 --> 00:37:14.591
mentioned we are very
focused on the 78 week target

00:37:14.591 --> 00:37:19.560
and 244,000 patients with
pathways were completed

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:22.830
between August and September,
that's 244,000 people

00:37:22.830 --> 00:37:25.860
who would otherwise have
breached the 78 week standard

00:37:25.860 --> 00:37:28.200
and most importantly received their care.

00:37:28.200 --> 00:37:30.060
And you will see in para 17,

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:31.980
some of the innovation that's taking place

00:37:31.980 --> 00:37:33.840
within the elective care pathway

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:35.730
to ensure that we're treating people

00:37:35.730 --> 00:37:37.143
as quickly as we are able.

00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:40.905
The sanction on urgent
care begins in para 20

00:37:40.905 --> 00:37:43.650
and it is of note that
in October this year,

00:37:43.650 --> 00:37:47.940
2,172,615 patients were seen

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:50.790
across accident emergency
departments in England

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:53.730
which is the highest volume
on record for any October.

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:56.940
So it is a system which
is seeing material demand

00:37:56.940 --> 00:37:58.680
and you see that category one incidents

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:03.630
were 2.3% higher this
October than last October.

00:38:03.630 --> 00:38:05.460
Now clearly our absolute focus

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:07.650
is to implement all
elements of the winter plan

00:38:07.650 --> 00:38:09.930
that has been discussed at
previous board meetings.

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:12.270
We are on track, we are ahead of plan

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:13.980
in terms of opening additional capacity

00:38:13.980 --> 00:38:15.750
including the virtual world capacity

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:17.190
that we discussed earlier.

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:19.440
And you will note that we
launched our winter collaborative

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:22.710
on the 1st of November
with a very specific focus

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:25.740
on ambulance handovers because
that's the absolute focus

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:27.690
of effort and energy out there.

00:38:27.690 --> 00:38:29.610
And finally on the urgent care pathway,

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:32.010
you'll note in para 30
the progress we're making

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:34.860
about recruiting more 999 and 111

00:38:34.860 --> 00:38:36.663
call handlers into the pathway.

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:40.770
Two other areas, just diagnostics again.

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:44.550
In September we did almost 2 million

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:46.920
of the key 15 key diagnostic tests,

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:49.800
more than we've ever done in a September.

00:38:49.800 --> 00:38:53.100
We remain very, very focused

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:57.750
on achieving the 95% target by March 2025.

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:00.630
And in support of that,
good progress is being made

00:39:00.630 --> 00:39:03.060
with the opening of our
community diagnostic centres.

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:06.090
We're actually overall,
we're ahead of plan activity

00:39:06.090 --> 00:39:07.350
although there are one or two areas

00:39:07.350 --> 00:39:10.470
which is one or two specific
sites which are slightly behind

00:39:10.470 --> 00:39:13.440
which we have recovery plans in place.

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:16.140
And finally I would note on
the mental health section,

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:19.950
para 51 to 57, pressure
on mental health services

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:22.050
remain also high to record demands.

00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:22.883
So this demand

00:39:22.883 --> 00:39:25.470
is not just in the physical
healthcare pathway,

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:27.870
it is also in the mental health pathway.

00:39:27.870 --> 00:39:29.970
But as you'll note in para 53,

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:32.880
again real progress is made
in primary care networks

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:35.220
having reported a mental
health practitioner in place,

00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:39.087
that's up to from 38% to 50% of PCNs

00:39:39.087 --> 00:39:41.490
and we continue to meet
the early intervention

00:39:41.490 --> 00:39:43.830
in psychosis two week standard

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:48.240
and in fact, are performing
better than the expected plan.

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:51.720
So as I've said, I think a
system definitely under pressure,

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:54.210
under challenge, a system
that is responding,

00:39:54.210 --> 00:39:56.040
and a system that is innovating.

00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:57.780
Quite happy to take questions

00:39:57.780 --> 00:39:59.980
on anything that I've
covered in the report.

00:40:02.555 --> 00:40:03.578
- Any questions?

00:40:03.578 --> 00:40:05.977
So Munir first, then Andrew.

00:40:05.977 --> 00:40:06.975
Munir?

00:40:06.975 --> 00:40:07.890
- Thanks.

00:40:07.890 --> 00:40:12.383
Just to highlight that despite
the new COVID variant, BQ.1,

00:40:13.725 --> 00:40:15.930
it is important to note, this
current evidence suggests

00:40:15.930 --> 00:40:18.900
that it is still the vaccines will protect

00:40:18.900 --> 00:40:21.000
against severe disease with this variant

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.730
hence the importance of
continuing vaccination.

00:40:23.730 --> 00:40:25.470
- Good point, thank you.

00:40:25.470 --> 00:40:26.430
Andrew?

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:28.800
- David, can I just
raise a couple of points?

00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:32.160
The first one is to
congratulate you and the team.

00:40:32.160 --> 00:40:34.020
I think this year is, you know,

00:40:34.020 --> 00:40:36.540
the year that you've started
to prepare for winter

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:38.400
way back in the spring

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:40.110
and there's a whole series of measures

00:40:40.110 --> 00:40:41.910
that are taking place.

00:40:41.910 --> 00:40:45.040
So I think we're in a better
state than we've ever been

00:40:46.325 --> 00:40:47.610
to cope with the winter.

00:40:47.610 --> 00:40:51.660
One bit that's missing for me
is around delayed discharges

00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:55.620
and the availability of the 500 million.

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:57.360
Can you just comment on that

00:40:57.360 --> 00:41:00.510
in terms of what the potential
impact is going to be

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:02.040
in terms of numbers of people

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:04.080
you envisage being kind of released?

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:06.510
'Cause we need to reestablish the flow

00:41:06.510 --> 00:41:08.580
in lots of our organisations

00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:10.550
and this money will be, you know,

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:12.330
we will have a bigger step next year

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:14.850
but 500 million is quite
a significant investment,

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:16.830
recognising that we've got four months

00:41:16.830 --> 00:41:18.330
of the financial year left.

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:19.410
- Yes, thank you, Andrew.

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:20.850
And actually the discharge element

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:23.550
is covered in paras 46 all in my report,

00:41:23.550 --> 00:41:25.560
but two things to mention actually.

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:28.080
Firstly, as you're aware,
we have been very focused

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:30.420
on the 100-day sprint we put in place

00:41:30.420 --> 00:41:31.950
around discharges from hospital,

00:41:31.950 --> 00:41:33.480
which were actually within the control

00:41:33.480 --> 00:41:34.950
of the hospital itself.

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:36.810
That work is continuing and actually,

00:41:36.810 --> 00:41:39.120
as a percentage of total
delayed discharges,

00:41:39.120 --> 00:41:41.430
that number is going
down, continuing to drop

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:43.170
as a proportion of the whole.

00:41:43.170 --> 00:41:44.790
As you rightly say, I'm delighted

00:41:44.790 --> 00:41:46.650
that we have had an announcement

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:49.140
that 500 million pounds
will now be released

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:50.083
to put towards adult social care

00:41:50.083 --> 00:41:53.070
as part of the discharge fund,

00:41:53.070 --> 00:41:55.620
but the systems have now been informed

00:41:55.620 --> 00:41:58.230
and they are putting their
own local plans in place

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:01.050
to determine how that
fund will be best deployed

00:42:01.050 --> 00:42:03.450
to help discharge people from hospital

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:06.150
who do not meet the
rights to reside criteria

00:42:06.150 --> 00:42:07.245
within those hospitals.

00:42:07.245 --> 00:42:10.380
I can't say specifically
here today numbers

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:11.430
because those are the plans

00:42:11.430 --> 00:42:13.200
that are being built up from the bottom

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:15.330
by each individual ICS.

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:18.780
But it is absolutely a
fundamental important lever

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:20.700
that we need to pull to maximum effect

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:23.160
if we are to get the flow
where we need it to be

00:42:23.160 --> 00:42:25.080
to get us through the rest of the winter.

00:42:25.080 --> 00:42:27.062
- Thank you.
- I know Wol has a question

00:42:27.062 --> 00:42:28.650
or comment, and David,
I think you do as well.

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:29.940
So Wol?

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:31.350
- Andrew has of course
started my question.

00:42:31.350 --> 00:42:32.183
Thank you.

00:42:33.315 --> 00:42:36.690
But it's fine, I've got another one.

00:42:36.690 --> 00:42:39.500
I'm not sure this is for you
David, perhaps with Steve.

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:40.890
So I mean vaccination you mentioned

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:44.230
is one of the key arrows in our quiver

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:47.772
and, you know, in years
gone by, as a board

00:42:47.772 --> 00:42:49.680
we used to sort of proudly stamp ourselves

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:52.830
having had our, you know, flu vaccinations

00:42:52.830 --> 00:42:53.663
and what have you, you know,

00:42:53.663 --> 00:42:56.193
achieving 100% compliance
across the board.

00:42:57.750 --> 00:43:00.150
One of the things that
could derail your plans

00:43:00.150 --> 00:43:01.863
is the lack of availability with staff.

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:03.930
Is there more we can be doing?

00:43:03.930 --> 00:43:04.763
Where are we?

00:43:04.763 --> 00:43:07.620
What are we doing to make
sure the staff understand

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:10.530
that this is a key element
of delivering for the service

00:43:10.530 --> 00:43:13.240
which is under pressure, as
we discussed across the board.

00:43:13.240 --> 00:43:14.993
- Okay, I think I will hand that to Steve.

00:43:18.270 --> 00:43:20.243
- Thanks for stealing
his question as well.

00:43:21.570 --> 00:43:22.403
So you're right, Wol,

00:43:22.403 --> 00:43:25.170
the NHS has got a strong
history of vaccination

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:30.003
off 100% percent level
to healthcare workers.

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:35.040
We've never achieved
100% uptake in the past

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:37.020
but flu and COVID vaccination this year

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:38.880
continue to be high up the radar

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:41.340
of all of the providers in England.

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:44.040
Everyone has made very meaningful offers

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:47.580
available to their staff through
clinics that they can book,

00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:51.690
roving teams where people
go into departments

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:53.910
to make it as easy as possible

00:43:53.910 --> 00:43:57.810
and trusts where uptake
has been a bit lower,

00:43:57.810 --> 00:44:00.570
have been thinking even more
innovatively than in the past.

00:44:00.570 --> 00:44:03.458
So London Ambulance Service have got 20,

00:44:03.458 --> 00:44:08.458
I think 27 kind of popups
across hospitals in London

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:13.170
so that as ambulance
crews are doing their work

00:44:13.170 --> 00:44:15.030
and taking people to hospital,

00:44:15.030 --> 00:44:17.190
if they have time and want
to take the opportunity,

00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:18.660
they're able to get a vaccination

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:21.450
opportunistically at that point.

00:44:21.450 --> 00:44:23.490
So I think it's really clear

00:44:23.490 --> 00:44:26.670
that organisations are
taking it very seriously

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:27.780
and thinking carefully

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:30.090
about how to make it as
convenient as possible.

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:32.550
And of course people can self declare

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:33.990
on the national booking system

00:44:33.990 --> 00:44:37.380
and go to any one of the
3,000 sites in England

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:38.853
for a vaccination as well.

00:44:39.810 --> 00:44:43.696
It is fair to say the rate of the uptake

00:44:43.696 --> 00:44:45.060
of vaccination in healthcare workers

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:48.360
is slightly lower than last year for flu

00:44:48.360 --> 00:44:50.973
and it's quite a lot lower for COVID.

00:44:52.170 --> 00:44:54.270
The board will be aware that
the environment this year

00:44:54.270 --> 00:44:56.040
is different, particularly for COVID.

00:44:56.040 --> 00:45:00.570
So last year, vaccination
was due to become mandatory.

00:45:00.570 --> 00:45:05.070
That obviously didn't
subsequently take place

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:06.150
but that did have an effect

00:45:06.150 --> 00:45:09.507
of driving up COVID vaccination rates.

00:45:09.507 --> 00:45:11.070
And the final thing perhaps just to say

00:45:11.070 --> 00:45:14.310
on what we do to encourage
vaccination for COVID

00:45:14.310 --> 00:45:16.500
in the here and now alongside flu

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:21.500
is JCVI did say that in order
to protect the NHS the most,

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:24.360
people should consider,
healthcare workers should consider

00:45:24.360 --> 00:45:26.680
getting vaccinated closer to December

00:45:27.570 --> 00:45:29.490
in comparison to the
rest of the population

00:45:29.490 --> 00:45:31.380
where the message was get vaccinated

00:45:31.380 --> 00:45:33.300
as soon as you are invited.

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:36.930
And so we are having a
renewed push through December

00:45:36.930 --> 00:45:38.100
on both flu and COVID

00:45:38.100 --> 00:45:40.486
to try and get rates as high as possible.

00:45:40.486 --> 00:45:41.470
- Thank you.
- Very good.

00:45:41.470 --> 00:45:45.150
- Actually, I'd just like
to also to sort of express

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:48.450
that my own experience
getting my own COVID booster

00:45:48.450 --> 00:45:49.713
and then my flu jab.

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:54.578
I'd just also like to thank
all the volunteers actually,

00:45:54.578 --> 00:45:56.970
you know, 3,000 centres who actually

00:45:56.970 --> 00:45:59.250
also made sort of
industrial strength actually

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:01.860
in terms of the offer is really good.

00:46:01.860 --> 00:46:03.270
David, you have questions?

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:06.690
- So it's part question, part
comment, Richard, I think.

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:09.240
I'm struck by aligning Steve's report

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:12.877
which says life expectancy
is decreased in 2020

00:46:14.295 --> 00:46:15.510
and I just want to link that

00:46:15.510 --> 00:46:18.120
to, I think on your comments, David,

00:46:18.120 --> 00:46:23.010
on cancer, on mental health,
on primary care access,

00:46:23.010 --> 00:46:24.870
all talked about an increased number

00:46:24.870 --> 00:46:29.157
of people presenting who required services

00:46:29.157 --> 00:46:33.607
and I just wonder how far
that increase in demand

00:46:33.607 --> 00:46:35.010
is significantly contributing

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:37.230
to the pressure that is around?

00:46:37.230 --> 00:46:39.990
And it's something that I'm not sure

00:46:39.990 --> 00:46:42.330
I see enough in the media

00:46:42.330 --> 00:46:44.220
of an explanation of what's going on

00:46:44.220 --> 00:46:46.890
is just the sheer increase
in the number of people

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:49.470
walking through the
door requesting services

00:46:49.470 --> 00:46:51.990
to which the services are responding.

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:54.030
So as I say, it's as much a statement

00:46:54.030 --> 00:46:57.600
as it is a question, Richard,

00:46:57.600 --> 00:46:59.910
but it really leapt out at me

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:02.820
reading these papers in
advance of the meeting

00:47:02.820 --> 00:47:04.230
just how many paragraphs

00:47:04.230 --> 00:47:08.100
talked about more people
presenting who needed services.

00:47:08.100 --> 00:47:09.210
They weren't being turned away,

00:47:09.210 --> 00:47:10.980
they were actually receiving services.

00:47:10.980 --> 00:47:15.090
So I think it's when we're
appreciating the efforts

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:16.050
that people are going under

00:47:16.050 --> 00:47:18.030
and acknowledging the pressure
that people are under,

00:47:18.030 --> 00:47:19.800
I think it is also worth pointing out

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:22.230
there are more people
presenting and in part

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:25.230
we're an ageing society and
life expectancy's decreasing.

00:47:25.230 --> 00:47:28.470
So I think they're presenting
with complex problems as well,

00:47:28.470 --> 00:47:29.670
not just simple problems.

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:31.743
And that I think also needs pulling out.

00:47:33.060 --> 00:47:35.190
- I think that's, I mean it's a comment,

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:36.600
I think it's a comment
more than a question,

00:47:36.600 --> 00:47:37.860
but it's a well placed comment.

00:47:37.860 --> 00:47:38.947
Actually, I agree with this.

00:47:38.947 --> 00:47:41.880
I had the same impression
reading the papers, actually.

00:47:41.880 --> 00:47:43.140
It comes through starkly

00:47:43.140 --> 00:47:45.660
just how that we're seeing
such a growth in demand

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:49.470
almost in all 360 degrees of
the circle of demand actually.

00:47:49.470 --> 00:47:51.630
So thank you for that.

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:53.190
I'm going to bring Julian in.

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:55.710
Financial performance update, Julian?

00:47:55.710 --> 00:47:57.810
- So first of all,

00:47:57.810 --> 00:48:00.240
I mean, largely the impact
to autumn statement,

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:03.213
I think, Richard, you and
Amanda have mostly covered.

00:48:04.200 --> 00:48:06.990
I mean, we are genuinely grateful

00:48:06.990 --> 00:48:10.500
for the support and recognition
of the pressure on the NHS

00:48:10.500 --> 00:48:12.243
as a result of higher inflation.

00:48:13.230 --> 00:48:15.420
At the public board, last time round,

00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:17.480
I think I estimated
the potential pressures

00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:20.430
at sort of 6 to 7 billion pounds.

00:48:20.430 --> 00:48:21.900
But what has changed since then

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:23.790
and why has Amanda been able to say

00:48:23.790 --> 00:48:26.100
that she thinks that the extra funding

00:48:26.100 --> 00:48:29.340
should mean we can focus
on our core priorities

00:48:29.340 --> 00:48:32.820
is that the inflation forecast
in the autumn statement

00:48:32.820 --> 00:48:35.220
was clearly much lower
than the number we used

00:48:35.220 --> 00:48:38.020
from the Bank of England's
quarterly report at the time.

00:48:40.320 --> 00:48:42.000
That's the forecast in
the autumn statement.

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:43.920
I mean it was closer to the GDP deflator,

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:48.660
was just over 3% versus a
Bank of England forecast

00:48:48.660 --> 00:48:51.390
at the time for CPI of around nine

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:55.810
and we have been funded in the previous SR

00:48:56.790 --> 00:49:00.300
for a pay settlement of 2%.

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:05.040
Now we are able, in line
with government requirements,

00:49:05.040 --> 00:49:08.350
to kind of hold a billion
pounds general contingency

00:49:09.450 --> 00:49:12.170
and we will just need to see
what the government policy is

00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:15.057
or what the pay review
bodies recommendations are

00:49:15.057 --> 00:49:17.670
and the government's response
to those recommendations

00:49:17.670 --> 00:49:18.873
in due course.

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:24.600
So that is why we think the 3.3 is fine

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:27.030
for us to say it should enable us

00:49:27.030 --> 00:49:29.490
to focus on our core priorities.

00:49:29.490 --> 00:49:32.010
I would just say just
before the autumn statement,

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:33.600
some excellent research was published

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:34.960
by the Health Foundation

00:49:35.910 --> 00:49:39.780
which basically the latest OECD data,

00:49:39.780 --> 00:49:43.920
which compares spending per
head of population for the EU14.

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:45.930
There's been a lot of coverage

00:49:45.930 --> 00:49:48.330
about spending as a shared GDP.

00:49:48.330 --> 00:49:49.530
What you've really got to look at is, yes,

00:49:49.530 --> 00:49:52.380
but what is the spend
per head of population?

00:49:52.380 --> 00:49:56.730
It flagged that over
the previous 10 years,

00:49:56.730 --> 00:49:58.410
spend per head of population in the UK

00:49:58.410 --> 00:50:02.250
is about 3,000 pounds per head
lower than the average EU14,

00:50:02.250 --> 00:50:03.540
even at the peak of COVID,

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:05.940
when clearly we had a lot
of extra cost going in.

00:50:05.940 --> 00:50:09.240
I think it's about 2,000
pounds per head lower.

00:50:09.240 --> 00:50:11.400
So I think it's important to realise

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:14.070
what is the sort of actual levels,

00:50:14.070 --> 00:50:16.860
sort of comparable levels of
investment that are going in.

00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:19.800
And capital investment historically
has been about 50% lower

00:50:19.800 --> 00:50:22.830
than the historical EU average,

00:50:22.830 --> 00:50:27.240
but we recognise our job
with the good settlement

00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:29.520
that we have got to do the best we can

00:50:29.520 --> 00:50:31.980
for patients and taxpayers.

00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:33.060
To get the best value,

00:50:33.060 --> 00:50:36.270
we've committed to work
off the sort of excess cost

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:39.870
we have seen through
COVID over three years,

00:50:39.870 --> 00:50:42.090
despite the fact that we
are basically planning now

00:50:42.090 --> 00:50:43.650
that COVID needs to be endemic,

00:50:43.650 --> 00:50:45.540
it has a structural impact

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:49.020
on sort of operational
activities within NHS,

00:50:49.020 --> 00:50:51.570
has a structural impact on our cost base.

00:50:51.570 --> 00:50:53.073
So that just puts the
requirement for us to work out,

00:50:53.073 --> 00:50:58.073
you know, how we can deal
with that, adapt our services

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:03.390
and get back to sort
of really good levels,

00:51:03.390 --> 00:51:05.590
very high levels of
operational performance.

00:51:07.020 --> 00:51:09.753
With that said, just
turning to this year then,

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:15.150
clearly as we have discussed before,

00:51:15.150 --> 00:51:18.267
the kind of operating
conditions of the NHS

00:51:18.267 --> 00:51:20.700
are very different to those
we asked people to plan on

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:23.193
and indeed the government
asked us to plan on,

00:51:24.750 --> 00:51:27.060
in fact, this time last year.

00:51:27.060 --> 00:51:31.350
As we've discussed, COVID levels
actually have not gone down

00:51:31.350 --> 00:51:33.300
to those we saw in summer '21,

00:51:33.300 --> 00:51:36.330
they have been materially
higher, indeed higher this year

00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:38.070
than pretty much in any point

00:51:38.070 --> 00:51:40.470
over the last two years actually.

00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:44.730
And inflation and pay have
clearly put huge pressure

00:51:44.730 --> 00:51:46.140
and the impact of all of that

00:51:46.140 --> 00:51:48.480
is our sickness absence is higher,

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:51.480
our temporary agency bill is higher.

00:51:51.480 --> 00:51:54.900
We are dealing with the
impact on, for example,

00:51:54.900 --> 00:51:56.730
continuing healthcare services

00:51:56.730 --> 00:51:59.790
of the fair cost of funding formula

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:02.490
that has been applied to social care,

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:05.133
so I mean, significant cost pressures.

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:09.600
Systems are working incredibly hard

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:12.603
to apply proper financial control.

00:52:13.590 --> 00:52:18.120
We are currently off the
back of sort of month six

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:20.460
and month seven outturn and forecast,

00:52:20.460 --> 00:52:22.140
working through with systems

00:52:22.140 --> 00:52:25.050
where they are going to
outturn at the end of the year.

00:52:25.050 --> 00:52:28.140
I am confident

00:52:28.140 --> 00:52:32.100
that we will live within the
mandate we have been given.

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:35.250
So the NHS will live within its resources

00:52:35.250 --> 00:52:37.800
whilst dealing with all of those pressures

00:52:37.800 --> 00:52:40.890
and seeking to deal or kind of deliver

00:52:40.890 --> 00:52:42.060
the performance outcomes

00:52:42.060 --> 00:52:44.343
which David and Amanda have described.

00:52:45.270 --> 00:52:49.083
We are definitely seeing systems
under financial pressure,

00:52:50.430 --> 00:52:51.660
but we are working with them

00:52:51.660 --> 00:52:53.970
to see, look, how close to
break-even can they get.

00:52:53.970 --> 00:52:56.790
I suspect we will find
some that don't get there,

00:52:56.790 --> 00:52:59.910
but again, we are working
with systems very closely

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:02.700
and working out what
flexibilities can be given

00:53:02.700 --> 00:53:05.040
to give them the best
chance of doing that.

00:53:05.040 --> 00:53:09.060
But, we will live within
the total NHS budget,

00:53:09.060 --> 00:53:11.760
I'm confident of that despite the risks

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:13.310
and issues we are dealing with.

00:53:14.310 --> 00:53:17.250
Worth saying just on capital, similarly,

00:53:17.250 --> 00:53:20.310
we are very much on the
same sort of trend of spend

00:53:20.310 --> 00:53:22.860
that we saw last year.

00:53:22.860 --> 00:53:27.090
So whilst at the end of month six

00:53:27.090 --> 00:53:29.670
we'd only spent about 1.8 billion pounds

00:53:29.670 --> 00:53:32.490
of our total budget, that's about 30%,

00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:34.890
that's a very similar pattern to last year

00:53:34.890 --> 00:53:37.350
where you tend to see a
sort of hockey stick effect

00:53:37.350 --> 00:53:41.883
as capital investments are
progressed through the year,

00:53:42.930 --> 00:53:44.043
equipment bought.

00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:48.600
So I think we're going
to come in somewhere

00:53:48.600 --> 00:53:51.780
probably around 180
million pounds under budget

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:55.110
and we've asked with the
department for flexibility

00:53:55.110 --> 00:53:57.180
to carry over that spend into next year

00:53:57.180 --> 00:54:00.600
so we can see through the
investment in diagnostic centres,

00:54:00.600 --> 00:54:03.663
surgical hubs and some of
the key things we need to do.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:06.481
- Very good, thank you.

00:54:06.481 --> 00:54:09.780
Laura, I think you have a, yes, Laura?

00:54:09.780 --> 00:54:12.150
- It was really linking
partly this year's performance

00:54:12.150 --> 00:54:14.400
and then something David referenced,

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:16.260
but I don't think we've
sort of picked up on,

00:54:16.260 --> 00:54:21.260
which was as we face into the
prospect of industrial action,

00:54:22.230 --> 00:54:26.040
how that might play out and
what planning we've got in place

00:54:26.040 --> 00:54:28.770
to help ensure that
we're protecting services

00:54:28.770 --> 00:54:30.963
as much as possible for our patients.

00:54:33.150 --> 00:54:35.037
- Okay, I'll touch on that, is the SRO

00:54:35.037 --> 00:54:38.580
for the industrial actions
that we have around.

00:54:38.580 --> 00:54:39.413
What we have done,

00:54:39.413 --> 00:54:41.420
we have issued guidance to all trust

00:54:41.420 --> 00:54:44.380
in all organisations
we have across the NHS

00:54:45.300 --> 00:54:48.210
where industrial action
is planned to take place

00:54:48.210 --> 00:54:49.530
to ensure that they work locally

00:54:49.530 --> 00:54:51.150
with their local trade union colleagues

00:54:51.150 --> 00:54:54.720
to ensure the absolute
primary focus is on safety

00:54:54.720 --> 00:54:56.550
to ensure all patients are kept safe.

00:54:56.550 --> 00:54:58.350
There will be some service disruption,

00:54:58.350 --> 00:54:59.880
but I think that will be very localised

00:54:59.880 --> 00:55:01.740
and it's difficult to
articulate at this level

00:55:01.740 --> 00:55:03.180
what that will look like.

00:55:03.180 --> 00:55:04.500
But we have worked really hard

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:06.300
to ensure guidance is out there

00:55:06.300 --> 00:55:08.640
that people are clear
about our expectations

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:09.930
about those local discussions

00:55:09.930 --> 00:55:11.880
and the local negotiations that take place

00:55:11.880 --> 00:55:15.060
to ensure, as I say,
patients are kept safe

00:55:15.060 --> 00:55:17.490
and as many services as we possibly can

00:55:17.490 --> 00:55:19.140
are kept on the road as well.

00:55:20.026 --> 00:55:21.522
- Navina, do you want to...

00:55:21.522 --> 00:55:23.013
- If I may, just to add,

00:55:23.013 --> 00:55:27.150
that we are, I'll bring
Ruth in as well, if I may,

00:55:27.150 --> 00:55:30.420
we are planning at every
level of the organisation

00:55:30.420 --> 00:55:34.020
around different scenarios
and possibilities.

00:55:34.020 --> 00:55:36.480
Our services at the frontline

00:55:36.480 --> 00:55:38.910
absolutely engaged with planning.

00:55:38.910 --> 00:55:40.170
Even the organisations

00:55:40.170 --> 00:55:44.100
where they hadn't reached
the threshold for action

00:55:44.100 --> 00:55:45.570
are involved in the planning.

00:55:45.570 --> 00:55:49.830
So we are very confident about
the degree of the planning.

00:55:49.830 --> 00:55:52.350
Also, together with the department

00:55:52.350 --> 00:55:55.770
and NHS employers as well,
so covered at all levels.

00:55:55.770 --> 00:55:57.810
And then in terms of what services

00:55:57.810 --> 00:55:59.910
we want to try and protect,

00:55:59.910 --> 00:56:03.450
Ruth has been very engaged
in those discussions

00:56:03.450 --> 00:56:04.980
and might want to say a little bit more.

00:56:04.980 --> 00:56:07.590
And those discussions also
happen at a local level

00:56:07.590 --> 00:56:10.053
as well as a national
level and are connected.

00:56:11.070 --> 00:56:13.800
- Just very briefly,
you know, that any nurse

00:56:13.800 --> 00:56:17.520
and midwife colleague thinking
about industrial action,

00:56:17.520 --> 00:56:19.353
it's a very difficult discussion,

00:56:19.353 --> 00:56:21.990
it's very difficult thought process

00:56:21.990 --> 00:56:23.400
for them to go through.

00:56:23.400 --> 00:56:27.690
But our job is to support
those nurses and midwives,

00:56:27.690 --> 00:56:30.873
whatever their decision has
been and will be on the day.

00:56:31.860 --> 00:56:33.390
We are working very closely.

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:36.690
We're working very closely with
the Royal College of Nursing

00:56:36.690 --> 00:56:39.690
at a national level and
indeed at a regional,

00:56:39.690 --> 00:56:43.080
chief nurses are with
the regional directors.

00:56:43.080 --> 00:56:44.880
And indeed we are working very closely

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:48.390
with all College of Nursing
across all four nations

00:56:48.390 --> 00:56:49.620
with the four CNOs.

00:56:49.620 --> 00:56:53.820
But there will be disruption
but we will work very closely

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:55.380
on a minute by minute basis

00:56:55.380 --> 00:56:59.730
to put patient safety
at the centre of that.

00:56:59.730 --> 00:57:02.910
- May I just add that we
also now know the outcome

00:57:02.910 --> 00:57:07.590
for UNISON and GMB, so we're
also working with them as well.

00:57:07.590 --> 00:57:08.850
- Thank you.

00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:10.860
- I think, Steve's first.
- Steve, do you want to go?

00:57:10.860 --> 00:57:12.870
- Well, yes, and I just
wanted to draw together

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:15.660
some of the various points
that people have made

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:17.280
because I think it's worth emphasising

00:57:17.280 --> 00:57:19.980
if we sort of take a
sort of higher level view

00:57:19.980 --> 00:57:22.134
of the pressures we're under.

00:57:22.134 --> 00:57:25.140
So the demographics, the
growth in the over 70s

00:57:25.140 --> 00:57:27.270
is absolutely something
that has been occurring

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:30.390
over the last decade and
will continue to occur

00:57:30.390 --> 00:57:32.490
over the next decade, and of course

00:57:32.490 --> 00:57:35.310
although we have been
making a lot of progress

00:57:35.310 --> 00:57:36.630
in managing disease,

00:57:36.630 --> 00:57:39.540
that is still a population
that has comorbidities,

00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:41.790
multiple diseases occurring together.

00:57:41.790 --> 00:57:43.740
That underlying growth has been occurring

00:57:43.740 --> 00:57:45.300
in the background continuing,

00:57:45.300 --> 00:57:49.620
and of course the COVID pressures
David mentioned, you know,

00:57:49.620 --> 00:57:52.860
we rarely been below four
and a half thousand patients

00:57:52.860 --> 00:57:55.800
with COVID in hospitals this year.

00:57:55.800 --> 00:57:58.800
And even though it is less severe

00:57:58.800 --> 00:58:00.900
and many of those are having incidentally

00:58:00.900 --> 00:58:02.940
rather than as the primary diagnosis,

00:58:02.940 --> 00:58:05.760
it is still having an operational impact.

00:58:05.760 --> 00:58:08.220
And our analysis suggests

00:58:08.220 --> 00:58:10.860
that it is having an impact
on increased length of stay.

00:58:10.860 --> 00:58:13.830
And so we have that
long-term demographic growth

00:58:13.830 --> 00:58:16.980
with the pressures likely
to last a number of years,

00:58:16.980 --> 00:58:20.400
as Amanda says, because we are
seeing these continued waves

00:58:20.400 --> 00:58:24.870
as COVID becomes endemic,
leading to pressures,

00:58:24.870 --> 00:58:27.330
particularly on the urgent
emergency care system

00:58:27.330 --> 00:58:29.130
and that is going to continue.

00:58:29.130 --> 00:58:31.710
So having a recovery plan
and a long-term strategy,

00:58:31.710 --> 00:58:34.680
is how we manage that, we
have a prevention paper later

00:58:34.680 --> 00:58:37.470
because some of this is
mitigatable and preventable,

00:58:37.470 --> 00:58:39.540
I think is going to be really important.

00:58:39.540 --> 00:58:40.680
- Thank you, I think I...

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:41.550
Wol, have you got a-

00:58:41.550 --> 00:58:44.247
- Yeah, just to take
you back to your paper

00:58:44.247 --> 00:58:47.280
and just ask you whether
you want to comment

00:58:47.280 --> 00:58:50.177
on capital at all?

00:58:50.177 --> 00:58:54.000
'Cause it was not covered in
the autumn statement really

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:57.453
and you did mention it
as to where we are now.

00:58:58.592 --> 00:59:01.830
- So the department has
been given a capital budget

00:59:01.830 --> 00:59:04.233
that runs through to the end of 24/25.

00:59:05.310 --> 00:59:09.150
There's been no indication that
that is going to be changed.

00:59:09.150 --> 00:59:11.400
I say no indication, I mean I'm not privy

00:59:11.400 --> 00:59:15.420
to have all Treasury
thinking at this point,

00:59:15.420 --> 00:59:17.920
but there's no indication
in the autumn statement.

00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:20.430
For us, the focus right now

00:59:20.430 --> 00:59:23.610
is I set three-year system allocations,

00:59:23.610 --> 00:59:27.960
that we hold to those
three-year system allocations.

00:59:27.960 --> 00:59:30.430
We have some money still to allocate out

00:59:31.519 --> 00:59:34.203
which we tied to actually
financial performance,

00:59:35.340 --> 00:59:37.140
so the delivery of a break-even plan,

00:59:37.140 --> 00:59:38.700
and we will continue to see that through.

00:59:38.700 --> 00:59:41.370
It's why actually, just
people need to keep working

00:59:41.370 --> 00:59:43.220
towards delivering a break-even plan.

00:59:44.520 --> 00:59:46.290
We need to see through the investment

00:59:46.290 --> 00:59:50.130
in that we have identified
in technology, diagnostics,

00:59:50.130 --> 00:59:52.650
some of the surgical capacity.

00:59:52.650 --> 00:59:56.370
The key next decision for us
is the government's decision

00:59:56.370 --> 00:59:59.430
on the approach to the
new hospital programme,

00:59:59.430 --> 01:00:02.070
which we really need to sort
of finalise programme, scope

01:00:02.070 --> 01:00:06.333
and budget and sort of wait
to see what happens there.

01:00:07.230 --> 01:00:11.133
So at the moment I'd say on
capital, steady as she goes,

01:00:12.900 --> 01:00:15.300
you know, we no doubt
we'll want to make the case

01:00:15.300 --> 01:00:18.180
for ongoing capital
investment as we look ahead,

01:00:18.180 --> 01:00:21.610
particularly if we are
going to just drive out

01:00:22.980 --> 01:00:27.030
kind of productivity,
change in the service model

01:00:27.030 --> 01:00:28.860
that we all know we need for the future

01:00:28.860 --> 01:00:32.520
if we are going to make
the NHS sustainable

01:00:32.520 --> 01:00:34.020
for taxpayers and patients.

01:00:34.020 --> 01:00:35.760
And I think that is, you know,

01:00:35.760 --> 01:00:39.243
more probably a post-24/25 question.

01:00:41.340 --> 01:00:42.480
So let's see.

01:00:42.480 --> 01:00:45.510
- So given time, I'm going to move us on

01:00:45.510 --> 01:00:48.270
but I also do know that your team, Julian,

01:00:48.270 --> 01:00:49.380
is working really hard

01:00:49.380 --> 01:00:52.290
to sort of get to grips with
the new hospital programme

01:00:52.290 --> 01:00:54.720
and the budgets and how it plays out.

01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:56.550
We are given, what, responsibility for it

01:00:56.550 --> 01:00:58.620
about two, three months ago I think?

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:02.250
And I know your team is
working hugely hard on that.

01:01:02.250 --> 01:01:03.480
And then just also, just to reflect,

01:01:03.480 --> 01:01:06.930
I mean that Health Foundation
report you alluded to earlier,

01:01:06.930 --> 01:01:08.130
I think I first saw it on,

01:01:08.130 --> 01:01:09.900
I think it was the front
page of the "Financial Times"

01:01:09.900 --> 01:01:12.330
and it was quite an interesting capture

01:01:12.330 --> 01:01:13.590
by the "Financial Times,"

01:01:13.590 --> 01:01:15.960
but then actually I then read the report

01:01:15.960 --> 01:01:17.370
and it is interesting really

01:01:17.370 --> 01:01:19.680
in terms of how to think
about the level of investment

01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:22.899
over recent history, last 10 years or so,

01:01:22.899 --> 01:01:24.870
and how it truly compares
across, in this case,

01:01:24.870 --> 01:01:27.780
the 14 EU countries,
the main 14 countries.

01:01:27.780 --> 01:01:28.613
- I will just say one thing,

01:01:28.613 --> 01:01:29.446
the department's actually
responsible still

01:01:29.446 --> 01:01:30.900
for the new hospital programme.

01:01:30.900 --> 01:01:33.270
We have people supporting it

01:01:33.270 --> 01:01:35.580
and Morag, who's leading
it is employed by us

01:01:35.580 --> 01:01:38.940
but at this point that is a-

01:01:38.940 --> 01:01:41.410
- I sit corrected.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:01:41.410 --> 01:01:43.170
- Okay, thank you.

01:01:43.170 --> 01:01:44.610
Let's go on the next item.

01:01:44.610 --> 01:01:48.480
So Ruth, I think, it's
maternity and neonatal services,

01:01:48.480 --> 01:01:50.100
an update for the board?

01:01:50.100 --> 01:01:51.507
- Thank you very much, Chair.

01:01:51.507 --> 01:01:55.020
And I guess I wanted to bring
to the board's attention

01:01:55.020 --> 01:01:57.690
again, but in public
this is our first time

01:01:57.690 --> 01:01:58.860
that we've had the opportunity

01:01:58.860 --> 01:02:03.577
to talk about the Bill Kirkup
reporting to East Kent,

01:02:03.577 --> 01:02:04.740
"Reading the Signals,"

01:02:04.740 --> 01:02:08.010
and we'll come back to
the title of this report

01:02:08.010 --> 01:02:12.113
as one of the main recommendations

01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:16.110
that Bill has talked about.

01:02:16.110 --> 01:02:19.843
So it's a report that was
published on the 19th of October,

01:02:19.843 --> 01:02:23.340
just a few weeks later
than we had anticipated

01:02:23.340 --> 01:02:27.180
because of the late Her
Majesty's period of mourning.

01:02:27.180 --> 01:02:32.180
It's distressing reading,
when you review this report,

01:02:32.520 --> 01:02:35.220
into East Kent hospitals.

01:02:35.220 --> 01:02:40.220
These families have suffered
unimaginable loss and harm

01:02:44.909 --> 01:02:47.913
during their experiences at East Kent.

01:02:49.170 --> 01:02:53.610
They bravely shared their stories

01:02:53.610 --> 01:02:57.330
and have given us the
opportunity, I guess,

01:02:57.330 --> 01:02:58.650
and the challenge,

01:02:58.650 --> 01:03:01.540
to really think about
how we truly hear them

01:03:02.490 --> 01:03:06.903
and how we act on what
this report tells us.

01:03:08.190 --> 01:03:10.530
- I know, it's very emotional.

01:03:10.530 --> 01:03:12.000
I hear that in your voice

01:03:12.000 --> 01:03:14.940
and it is a very distressing report.

01:03:14.940 --> 01:03:16.740
And let me just help you for a moment

01:03:16.740 --> 01:03:19.200
because you've been joined,
I think, by three colleagues,

01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:20.033
which is really good.

01:03:20.033 --> 01:03:23.768
So by Jackie Dunkley-Bent
and Donald Peebles,

01:03:23.768 --> 01:03:25.590
and Acosia Nyanin,

01:03:25.590 --> 01:03:27.060
and welcome.

01:03:27.060 --> 01:03:29.730
And Ruth, I don't know how
you want to deploy your team

01:03:29.730 --> 01:03:30.720
in this conversation.
- Yeah, that's fine.

01:03:30.720 --> 01:03:31.980
That's fantastic.

01:03:31.980 --> 01:03:34.410
So there are a number of areas for action.

01:03:34.410 --> 01:03:36.810
He's got four key area of actions

01:03:36.810 --> 01:03:38.400
monitoring safety performance

01:03:38.400 --> 01:03:40.320
and finding the signals amongst noise.

01:03:40.320 --> 01:03:43.620
And last week Bill Kirkup

01:03:43.620 --> 01:03:45.720
met with Aiden and Amanda and myself,

01:03:45.720 --> 01:03:47.730
who went through these actions

01:03:47.730 --> 01:03:50.190
and particularly concentrated

01:03:50.190 --> 01:03:54.423
on how we take the
information that's there,

01:03:55.260 --> 01:03:58.350
how we look at that in a more real time,

01:03:58.350 --> 01:03:59.490
look at the trends

01:03:59.490 --> 01:04:04.320
and not just the CQC
ratings of an organisation,

01:04:04.320 --> 01:04:09.000
but how we look at things
over a period of time

01:04:09.000 --> 01:04:11.520
and that was really
important powerful stuff.

01:04:11.520 --> 01:04:14.490
The second one was about
standards of clinical behaviour.

01:04:14.490 --> 01:04:16.290
The technical care is not enough

01:04:16.290 --> 01:04:19.230
and it was about how
those women and families

01:04:19.230 --> 01:04:23.943
were treated by nurses,
midwives, and obstetricians,

01:04:25.230 --> 01:04:29.160
and how we want to make sure
they're much more professional.

01:04:29.160 --> 01:04:31.470
The third action was
about floor team working

01:04:31.470 --> 01:04:34.620
and pulling the different
directions, having one goal.

01:04:34.620 --> 01:04:37.338
And the fourth thing was
about organisational behaviour

01:04:37.338 --> 01:04:39.600
and about how organisations

01:04:39.600 --> 01:04:44.600
need to think about reputation management

01:04:44.910 --> 01:04:46.830
and think about that should
be more about the honesty

01:04:46.830 --> 01:04:51.830
and openness that they provide.

01:04:52.770 --> 01:04:55.470
So we've done a lot over
this last several years,

01:04:55.470 --> 01:04:59.100
particularly when we've had
Donna Ockenden recommendations.

01:04:59.100 --> 01:05:01.830
We've invested each year as an NHS England

01:05:01.830 --> 01:05:05.460
and I know each NHS
organisation themselves

01:05:05.460 --> 01:05:08.910
has continued to invest
in maternity services.

01:05:08.910 --> 01:05:12.000
We've seen big increases, and
you'll see it in the paper,

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:14.370
of the establishments for midwives,

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:19.370
obstetricians and other
clinical colleagues.

01:05:19.560 --> 01:05:21.750
But actually, you'll also see in the paper

01:05:21.750 --> 01:05:26.750
how we, you know, there is
need for further investment

01:05:27.900 --> 01:05:30.570
in the clinical workforce

01:05:30.570 --> 01:05:33.480
and that's going to be really important.

01:05:33.480 --> 01:05:38.480
We agreed with Bill and
we've agreed as a team

01:05:38.580 --> 01:05:40.680
that one of the things we do now

01:05:40.680 --> 01:05:45.000
is to bring together one maternity plan

01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:48.570
because at the moment organisations
have got lots of reports

01:05:48.570 --> 01:05:50.010
and lots of recommendations

01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:52.710
and we want to make this
much easier for them.

01:05:52.710 --> 01:05:54.120
How do we bring it together?

01:05:54.120 --> 01:05:55.950
So we've covered the moment

01:05:55.950 --> 01:05:57.640
and we are engaging with
colleagues at the moment

01:05:57.640 --> 01:06:01.897
about how we have four
big chunks to this work

01:06:01.897 --> 01:06:04.680
which are set out in this paper,

01:06:04.680 --> 01:06:08.730
listening to women, workforce,
culture and leadership,

01:06:08.730 --> 01:06:11.370
and standards and infrastructure.

01:06:11.370 --> 01:06:16.370
We will need to make sure that
we bring that into a plan.

01:06:17.010 --> 01:06:22.010
We'll bring this back through
to this board in next quarter.

01:06:22.860 --> 01:06:24.840
We're engaging at the moment,

01:06:24.840 --> 01:06:27.600
and of course what East Country Board does

01:06:27.600 --> 01:06:31.860
is put this not just within
the maternity services,

01:06:31.860 --> 01:06:35.220
this is much wider, you know,
talking about the training

01:06:35.220 --> 01:06:37.620
and education of our future workforce,

01:06:37.620 --> 01:06:39.900
working with health education colleagues,

01:06:39.900 --> 01:06:42.000
working across with strategy colleagues,

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:44.070
and of course working with Julian

01:06:44.070 --> 01:06:47.400
about the long-term
financial sustainability

01:06:47.400 --> 01:06:49.740
in maternity services.

01:06:49.740 --> 01:06:50.940
I know I've got colleagues here,

01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:52.980
I've got Acosia from the
regional chief nurse,

01:06:52.980 --> 01:06:56.730
which has been leading the
work directly with East Kent,

01:06:56.730 --> 01:07:00.360
able to say a few words if
you want in certain questions.

01:07:00.360 --> 01:07:03.720
And Donald and Jackie are resident experts

01:07:03.720 --> 01:07:06.600
here to answer some of
the questions with me too.

01:07:06.600 --> 01:07:11.600
But ask the board to note
the progress we've made

01:07:12.300 --> 01:07:14.760
but note the challenge
we've still got too,

01:07:14.760 --> 01:07:16.020
particularly around workforce

01:07:16.020 --> 01:07:19.140
and how we convert those established posts

01:07:19.140 --> 01:07:21.690
to actual being filled.

01:07:21.690 --> 01:07:25.500
How we take this then into one plan

01:07:25.500 --> 01:07:27.723
including the financial plan for it.

01:07:29.520 --> 01:07:32.430
And Andrew may want to say something

01:07:32.430 --> 01:07:35.400
as you're our maternity head.

01:07:35.400 --> 01:07:36.510
- Acosia do you want to say,

01:07:36.510 --> 01:07:37.770
you were given sort of billing there?

01:07:37.770 --> 01:07:40.440
Would you want to say
anything before we go to Q&amp;A?

01:07:40.440 --> 01:07:44.280
- If I could please share
just a couple of things.

01:07:44.280 --> 01:07:45.570
- [Richard] You may need
to speak up slightly.

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:46.650
- Can you hear me?

01:07:46.650 --> 01:07:48.030
- Yeah.
- That's good.

01:07:48.030 --> 01:07:51.310
So yeah, if I could, so I think firstly

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:56.070
to absolutely endorse what
Ruth was setting out there

01:07:56.070 --> 01:07:59.160
in terms of the difficulty
around reading the report

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:00.480
and absolutely acknowledging

01:08:00.480 --> 01:08:04.083
the unacceptable and poor
care and missed opportunities.

01:08:05.820 --> 01:08:09.510
I think that the report
reconfirms a requirement

01:08:09.510 --> 01:08:11.970
for East Kent and the leadership team

01:08:11.970 --> 01:08:14.100
and every board to remain focused

01:08:14.100 --> 01:08:18.003
on personalised safe
maternity and neonatal care.

01:08:19.080 --> 01:08:21.780
I also absolutely concur with Ruth

01:08:21.780 --> 01:08:25.230
that actually this goes beyond
maternity and neonatal care.

01:08:25.230 --> 01:08:27.840
The issues around leadership and culture,

01:08:27.840 --> 01:08:29.760
around quality surveillance

01:08:29.760 --> 01:08:31.860
and around looking at things in the round

01:08:31.860 --> 01:08:36.750
apply to every aspect of
NHS care and delivery.

01:08:36.750 --> 01:08:39.390
But specifically for East Kent,

01:08:39.390 --> 01:08:43.650
before handing over to Jackie and Donald,

01:08:43.650 --> 01:08:46.980
I do want to say that our area of focus

01:08:46.980 --> 01:08:48.870
for the organisation at the moment

01:08:48.870 --> 01:08:52.170
is absolutely around that safety culture

01:08:52.170 --> 01:08:55.440
and working with them on
the areas of the report

01:08:55.440 --> 01:08:57.720
that really set out
failings in team working

01:08:57.720 --> 01:09:01.900
and common purpose and
responding to challenge

01:09:03.420 --> 01:09:04.980
with an open mind and honesty.

01:09:04.980 --> 01:09:07.770
The leadership team within East Kent

01:09:07.770 --> 01:09:11.190
are absolutely behind all of that

01:09:11.190 --> 01:09:13.950
because it's important to the women,

01:09:13.950 --> 01:09:16.380
babies and their families.

01:09:16.380 --> 01:09:17.353
- Thank you, Acosia, for that.

01:09:17.353 --> 01:09:20.550
Before I get Jackie and Donald's comment,

01:09:20.550 --> 01:09:22.167
can I just open up for
comments or questions?

01:09:22.167 --> 01:09:24.921
And actually you might well
want to respond to those I think

01:09:24.921 --> 01:09:25.754
as we do this.

01:09:25.754 --> 01:09:27.990
Andrew, I saw your hand
go up and Steve as well.

01:09:27.990 --> 01:09:30.283
So Andrew?
- Okay.

01:09:30.283 --> 01:09:33.090
So Ruth, you've put a
hell of a lot of work in

01:09:33.090 --> 01:09:35.640
with Jackie, Donald

01:09:35.640 --> 01:09:38.670
to try and address the
various recommendations

01:09:38.670 --> 01:09:41.160
that have come from recent reviews.

01:09:41.160 --> 01:09:44.370
I think it's really
important that we apply them

01:09:44.370 --> 01:09:47.190
to the whole of the NHS

01:09:47.190 --> 01:09:51.510
and one of the challenges
we have is, you know,

01:09:51.510 --> 01:09:56.370
we have a number of
organisations who run hospitals

01:09:56.370 --> 01:10:00.210
and we just, we are relying
upon them to change the culture

01:10:00.210 --> 01:10:02.730
or ensure that the culture is appropriate

01:10:02.730 --> 01:10:04.260
in each organisation.

01:10:04.260 --> 01:10:06.540
So I'd be interested to understand better

01:10:06.540 --> 01:10:08.250
the metrics we are using

01:10:08.250 --> 01:10:11.940
to demonstrate some
improvement around culture.

01:10:11.940 --> 01:10:14.310
The second set of issues
that I want to raise

01:10:14.310 --> 01:10:17.742
is around staffing, 'cause if
you look at paragraph five.

01:10:17.742 --> 01:10:19.260
- Yes, the end of five,
- Gosh, these numbers

01:10:19.260 --> 01:10:21.630
are quite frightening if I'm honest,

01:10:21.630 --> 01:10:23.520
in terms of gaps.

01:10:23.520 --> 01:10:25.620
And if you look at midwives, Jackie,

01:10:25.620 --> 01:10:28.200
I mean we're saying we've
currently got a shortage

01:10:28.200 --> 01:10:30.840
of around 1,100.

01:10:30.840 --> 01:10:34.230
We've also got a bit of a kind
of demographic issue looming

01:10:34.230 --> 01:10:36.090
with a number of people, you know,

01:10:36.090 --> 01:10:37.860
potentially exiting the service

01:10:37.860 --> 01:10:40.890
through retirement going forward.

01:10:40.890 --> 01:10:43.800
And I'm not sure whether we've got enough,

01:10:43.800 --> 01:10:46.440
I know we've put extra
trainees in the pipeline,

01:10:46.440 --> 01:10:48.900
but I'm not sure whether
the two balance off.

01:10:48.900 --> 01:10:52.410
The second is around the docs,

01:10:52.410 --> 01:10:55.470
both the neonatal docs
and the obstetricians.

01:10:55.470 --> 01:11:00.470
And I'd like us to be as
demanding of organisations

01:11:00.570 --> 01:11:03.210
as we are say, for the
emergency department.

01:11:03.210 --> 01:11:06.060
We would expect a consultant to be on duty

01:11:06.060 --> 01:11:09.090
in an emergency department,
8 till 10 o'clock,

01:11:09.090 --> 01:11:11.250
midnight, seven days a week.

01:11:11.250 --> 01:11:14.280
We haven't set that
similar expectation out

01:11:14.280 --> 01:11:15.827
for this particular service.

01:11:15.827 --> 01:11:18.210
I know we've said we're
were expecting people

01:11:18.210 --> 01:11:21.090
to ensure that at least two ward rounds

01:11:21.090 --> 01:11:24.630
are undertaken each
day, seven days a week.

01:11:24.630 --> 01:11:28.260
But that is very different
to the type of service

01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:31.200
that I think is, you know, appropriate

01:11:31.200 --> 01:11:35.010
for this type of semi-emergency operation

01:11:35.010 --> 01:11:37.530
that most trusts, you know, run.

01:11:37.530 --> 01:11:40.200
So I'd just welcome to
comment on those three points.

01:11:40.200 --> 01:11:42.603
- Before, you probably
need to either note them,

01:11:42.603 --> 01:11:43.778
memorise them,

01:11:43.778 --> 01:11:45.919
because I'm going to
gather other comments first

01:11:45.919 --> 01:11:47.940
and have them sort of come at
you in a batch if that's okay.

01:11:47.940 --> 01:11:48.870
But Steve do you want to-

01:11:48.870 --> 01:11:49.950
- Yes, that and I'm sure Donald

01:11:49.950 --> 01:11:53.550
will want to pick up the
point on obstetric covers

01:11:53.550 --> 01:11:56.370
is really important,
but equally challenging.

01:11:56.370 --> 01:11:58.530
And we've talked about
workforce earlier on

01:11:58.530 --> 01:12:00.330
in terms of the workforce plan,

01:12:00.330 --> 01:12:03.840
but I think like Ruth and
like myself, you know,

01:12:03.840 --> 01:12:05.780
this is a truly shocking
report and when you read it,

01:12:05.780 --> 01:12:07.167
it truly shakes you.

01:12:07.167 --> 01:12:10.440
And I know many colleagues in obstetrics

01:12:10.440 --> 01:12:11.940
and I'm sure Donald will highlight this

01:12:11.940 --> 01:12:15.720
of being shaken by what they have read.

01:12:15.720 --> 01:12:20.720
But I too want to echo that
this is not just about maternity

01:12:21.090 --> 01:12:23.840
and I think there are many,
many lessons in that report

01:12:25.719 --> 01:12:26.552
that are generalizable

01:12:26.552 --> 01:12:27.927
and we need to think about.
- Cultural.

01:12:27.927 --> 01:12:29.883
- And one I would mention is teams.

01:12:31.080 --> 01:12:34.620
You know, medicine is an
intensely personal experience

01:12:34.620 --> 01:12:35.730
in a sense the relationship

01:12:35.730 --> 01:12:37.800
between the individual doctor and patient,

01:12:37.800 --> 01:12:39.600
the individual nurse and the patient,

01:12:39.600 --> 01:12:42.120
but increasingly it's team based

01:12:42.120 --> 01:12:45.100
and it's the delivery and
the effectiveness of the team

01:12:47.758 --> 01:12:52.290
that delivers the best care
and is what drives best care.

01:12:52.290 --> 01:12:55.080
And there a huge number
of wonderfully functioning

01:12:55.080 --> 01:12:57.330
high performing teams in the NHS,

01:12:57.330 --> 01:13:00.270
but unfortunately we do
see dysfunctional teams

01:13:00.270 --> 01:13:01.890
and I think we need to do a lot more

01:13:01.890 --> 01:13:04.440
in our training and our supporter staff

01:13:04.440 --> 01:13:05.523
to minimise the risks

01:13:05.523 --> 01:13:07.950
that that dysfunctionality
within teams occurs.

01:13:07.950 --> 01:13:10.170
- So I've got two other questioners

01:13:10.170 --> 01:13:12.622
or comments coming your way

01:13:12.622 --> 01:13:14.100
and then I'll give you a
chance to respond to that.

01:13:14.100 --> 01:13:15.690
David, I know you want to comment

01:13:15.690 --> 01:13:17.940
and Navina you wanted to
comment or ask a question.

01:13:17.940 --> 01:13:19.230
David?
- Oh, thanks, Richard.

01:13:19.230 --> 01:13:22.200
Ruth and colleagues, thanks for this.

01:13:22.200 --> 01:13:23.460
It's good to see, you know,

01:13:23.460 --> 01:13:25.230
how you've begun to shape your thinking

01:13:25.230 --> 01:13:30.230
about moving forward
and those four headings

01:13:31.140 --> 01:13:32.970
make some sense to me.

01:13:32.970 --> 01:13:34.350
But when you introduce, Ruth,

01:13:34.350 --> 01:13:36.490
you talked about the title of the report

01:13:37.484 --> 01:13:42.000
and, you know, quite significantly
it's reading the signals

01:13:42.000 --> 01:13:46.379
and the first chapter begins
on missed opportunities

01:13:46.379 --> 01:13:48.750
and I think Kirkup was making a point

01:13:48.750 --> 01:13:51.450
about where the hard, the quantity data

01:13:51.450 --> 01:13:54.420
and the softer intelligence
gets put together

01:13:54.420 --> 01:13:56.820
and how that might flag
for the early warnings.

01:13:56.820 --> 01:13:59.100
And I couldn't quite fit that

01:13:59.100 --> 01:14:01.770
and if I'm right in
reading it in that way,

01:14:01.770 --> 01:14:04.710
I couldn't quite fit that
into those four headings.

01:14:04.710 --> 01:14:07.260
And I just wondered as you
begin to mature the action plan

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:10.020
and bring it back, whether
we could just think

01:14:10.020 --> 01:14:12.600
about how what I thought

01:14:12.600 --> 01:14:15.000
were some quite powerful
messages in his report,

01:14:15.000 --> 01:14:16.920
about reading the signals and the signs,

01:14:16.920 --> 01:14:17.790
is actually captured

01:14:17.790 --> 01:14:20.520
and built into what it is
we're going to bring forward.

01:14:20.520 --> 01:14:21.480
Because that's where I think

01:14:21.480 --> 01:14:24.210
some of the preventative
interventions take place.

01:14:24.210 --> 01:14:27.270
Otherwise a lot of this is
post-hoc, it's after the event.

01:14:27.270 --> 01:14:30.690
And actually, you know,
I would hope the focus

01:14:30.690 --> 01:14:32.430
is how can we stop these things happening,

01:14:32.430 --> 01:14:35.881
not how can we get better at
managing them when they do.

01:14:35.881 --> 01:14:37.710
We need to do that as well, sadly.

01:14:37.710 --> 01:14:38.543
- Thank you.

01:14:38.543 --> 01:14:41.485
Navina, and then I'm going
to give Jackie and Donald

01:14:41.485 --> 01:14:43.590
and Acosia a chance to answer.

01:14:43.590 --> 01:14:46.080
- So three very quick points to make.

01:14:46.080 --> 01:14:49.800
First of all, there's one thing
about education and training

01:14:49.800 --> 01:14:53.400
in terms of supply and we
are having a discussion

01:14:53.400 --> 01:14:54.930
at the Health Education England Board

01:14:54.930 --> 01:14:58.770
about what we can do differently

01:14:58.770 --> 01:15:01.530
in that space for education and training,

01:15:01.530 --> 01:15:03.960
but also how we listen to our students',

01:15:03.960 --> 01:15:05.763
learners and trainees early on,

01:15:07.080 --> 01:15:10.464
not just in maternity but
also across the board.

01:15:10.464 --> 01:15:12.483
The second thing I'd like
to say is that I think

01:15:12.483 --> 01:15:14.550
that now that we are coming together

01:15:14.550 --> 01:15:16.320
as a single organisation,

01:15:16.320 --> 01:15:19.260
it'll give us much greater opportunities

01:15:19.260 --> 01:15:23.550
to build on the work that
we can do to improve.

01:15:23.550 --> 01:15:24.383
The third thing to say

01:15:24.383 --> 01:15:27.120
is that around the whole
issue of leadership,

01:15:27.120 --> 01:15:31.200
culture and behaviour in this area

01:15:31.200 --> 01:15:32.640
but also in many other areas,

01:15:32.640 --> 01:15:35.160
we've seen this as a kind of theme

01:15:35.160 --> 01:15:37.400
where we have difficulties
and Steve has talked about it,

01:15:37.400 --> 01:15:39.480
it is going to be a focus of work

01:15:39.480 --> 01:15:40.770
and I'm really pleased to see

01:15:40.770 --> 01:15:44.310
that it is one of the main areas

01:15:44.310 --> 01:15:48.240
that we will, in the workforce
training education function,

01:15:48.240 --> 01:15:52.590
work very closely across
the board to address it.

01:15:52.590 --> 01:15:55.620
I think the point made about metrics

01:15:55.620 --> 01:15:57.297
is a really important one

01:15:57.297 --> 01:16:00.657
and it comes back to
the improvement regime

01:16:00.657 --> 01:16:02.580
and the framework that we will use

01:16:02.580 --> 01:16:04.980
and we can tie all of that together.

01:16:04.980 --> 01:16:08.160
- Thank you, so now apologies

01:16:08.160 --> 01:16:11.220
for having batched up various
comments and questions

01:16:11.220 --> 01:16:14.340
and I don't know how you
want to divide the ability

01:16:14.340 --> 01:16:16.080
to comment, and I'm not looking at Ruth,

01:16:16.080 --> 01:16:19.140
I'm coming at Jackie and Donald and Acosia

01:16:19.140 --> 01:16:22.168
for response to those comments
or questions at this point.

01:16:22.168 --> 01:16:24.060
- Would you like me to start
with culture and leadership

01:16:24.060 --> 01:16:26.520
and then talk to numbers and then?

01:16:26.520 --> 01:16:27.920
- And then to meet the rest.

01:16:29.640 --> 01:16:32.510
- That's only 'cause
I can't remember that.

01:16:34.950 --> 01:16:36.300
I'm an obstetrician

01:16:36.300 --> 01:16:39.330
and I think I speak for maternity services

01:16:39.330 --> 01:16:43.080
when I say just what a profound impact

01:16:43.080 --> 01:16:44.760
these reports have had.

01:16:44.760 --> 01:16:48.810
I was at a conference
recently hearing the parents

01:16:48.810 --> 01:16:53.810
describing their interactions
with various trusts

01:16:54.030 --> 01:16:59.030
and one felt sort of close to tears,

01:17:00.090 --> 01:17:03.030
that the impact that their care

01:17:03.030 --> 01:17:05.580
and the failure to communicate
properly with them afterwards

01:17:05.580 --> 01:17:08.883
had had on them and the
rest of their lives.

01:17:10.200 --> 01:17:15.200
So I think we feel a real
urgency, but a realistic urgency,

01:17:16.830 --> 01:17:20.160
around responding to
these recommendations,

01:17:20.160 --> 01:17:22.473
incredibly helpful recommendations.

01:17:24.750 --> 01:17:28.710
Culture and leadership have
been raised a number of times

01:17:28.710 --> 01:17:31.770
and they really, really ring out

01:17:31.770 --> 01:17:34.233
in Bill Kirkup's report from East Kent.

01:17:36.690 --> 01:17:41.690
We've been working and
co-producing across the NHS

01:17:44.220 --> 01:17:49.220
a programme which is just
unrolling for maternity services

01:17:49.620 --> 01:17:52.620
where we're focusing on the team.

01:17:52.620 --> 01:17:54.180
And this is, I agree with Steve,

01:17:54.180 --> 01:17:59.100
it's all around teams and
it's the team of midwife,

01:17:59.100 --> 01:18:01.380
obstetrician, porter,

01:18:01.380 --> 01:18:04.053
everyone who's involved
in delivering that care.

01:18:04.980 --> 01:18:09.980
So where we're focusing on
a quadrant of neonatologist,

01:18:10.110 --> 01:18:13.713
obstetrician, midwife and operations lead,

01:18:15.360 --> 01:18:17.880
training them together about leadership

01:18:17.880 --> 01:18:22.880
so that they recognise the
importance of each other's roles,

01:18:23.670 --> 01:18:27.180
which we think is really key
and will promote communication.

01:18:27.180 --> 01:18:30.870
And if you start at the top,
we think it'll spread down

01:18:30.870 --> 01:18:33.960
throughout the maternity service.

01:18:33.960 --> 01:18:37.710
Linked to that programme is a piece

01:18:37.710 --> 01:18:40.890
where we're going to look at the culture

01:18:40.890 --> 01:18:43.350
of every single maternity service.

01:18:43.350 --> 01:18:47.520
I don't have an answer for
you, Andrew, around the metric

01:18:47.520 --> 01:18:50.580
that accurately sums up
the culture of a service,

01:18:50.580 --> 01:18:52.410
but it's for sure something

01:18:52.410 --> 01:18:54.690
that we would like to distil out

01:18:54.690 --> 01:18:57.060
and the work that we're going to be doing

01:18:57.060 --> 01:18:59.403
will attempt to do that.

01:19:01.230 --> 01:19:03.450
Jackie and I have had
a series of road shows

01:19:03.450 --> 01:19:06.690
with senior executives and in
all trusts across the country

01:19:06.690 --> 01:19:09.420
during the last year we've
spoken to most of them

01:19:09.420 --> 01:19:11.700
and a central element of that discussion

01:19:11.700 --> 01:19:15.090
has been around culture and leadership.

01:19:15.090 --> 01:19:18.150
What do you know about the
culture of your maternity service

01:19:18.150 --> 01:19:20.313
and what are you doing to improve it?

01:19:22.560 --> 01:19:24.660
I think Kirkup made it clear

01:19:24.660 --> 01:19:29.190
that that was possibly more
important an issue at East Kent

01:19:29.190 --> 01:19:33.240
than the actual numbers
of midwives, doctors.

01:19:33.240 --> 01:19:36.360
That's not to say that it's unimportant.

01:19:36.360 --> 01:19:37.530
And I think that overall,

01:19:37.530 --> 01:19:40.233
in response to your question
about doctor numbers,

01:19:42.330 --> 01:19:44.490
we all share an ambition

01:19:44.490 --> 01:19:47.280
to increase the number of obstetricians

01:19:47.280 --> 01:19:49.290
and part of that increased time

01:19:49.290 --> 01:19:51.270
will be spent on labour wards.

01:19:51.270 --> 01:19:52.826
And indeed that's one
of the recommendations

01:19:52.826 --> 01:19:55.380
from Donna Ockenden's review

01:19:55.380 --> 01:19:57.540
that actually we should do that.

01:19:57.540 --> 01:20:00.570
The numbers described in
the paper that you have

01:20:00.570 --> 01:20:02.490
are a step in the right direction

01:20:02.490 --> 01:20:05.880
and that partly is driven
by the ward rounds,

01:20:05.880 --> 01:20:07.680
the two ward rounds that you described,

01:20:07.680 --> 01:20:09.230
in the morning and the evening.

01:20:10.260 --> 01:20:14.790
There is not a workforce standard
around this at the moment.

01:20:14.790 --> 01:20:19.480
The RCOG have changed their
stance over the last 10 years

01:20:20.640 --> 01:20:24.240
from one where I think
they were quite directive

01:20:24.240 --> 01:20:26.580
about if you had so many births in a unit,

01:20:26.580 --> 01:20:29.100
you should have so many obstetricians.

01:20:29.100 --> 01:20:31.050
And I think one of the
reasons for backing off

01:20:31.050 --> 01:20:33.750
is that they've realised
there are so many variables

01:20:33.750 --> 01:20:35.940
that coming up with a one-size-fits-all

01:20:35.940 --> 01:20:38.130
is incredibly challenging

01:20:38.130 --> 01:20:42.000
and the variables are size
of unit, acuity of unit.

01:20:42.000 --> 01:20:45.060
The fact that obstetricians
are often in this country

01:20:45.060 --> 01:20:47.790
also gynaecologists with that workload

01:20:47.790 --> 01:20:52.790
and increasingly gaps
in middle grade rotas

01:20:53.130 --> 01:20:55.140
and the need for consultants to step up

01:20:55.140 --> 01:20:56.740
and cover those gaps as well as.

01:20:57.870 --> 01:21:00.720
So all of those things put together

01:21:00.720 --> 01:21:03.450
mean we don't have a workforce standard.

01:21:03.450 --> 01:21:06.240
What we do have and pointing to the future

01:21:06.240 --> 01:21:09.750
is a fantastic document
that the college produced

01:21:09.750 --> 01:21:14.750
earlier this year about
the expected behaviours

01:21:15.060 --> 01:21:18.510
and roles of obstetric
consultants on labour wards,

01:21:18.510 --> 01:21:22.110
which makes it really clear
what the expectation is.

01:21:22.110 --> 01:21:23.610
And secondly, a piece of work

01:21:23.610 --> 01:21:27.210
that's currently going on
commissioned from the RCOG

01:21:27.210 --> 01:21:31.480
about an obstetric safer staffing tool

01:21:32.340 --> 01:21:37.320
akin to birth rate plus,

01:21:37.320 --> 01:21:39.030
which they're working on and in fact

01:21:39.030 --> 01:21:42.090
have just done a big survey
across all maternity services.

01:21:42.090 --> 01:21:43.710
So we should have an answer

01:21:43.710 --> 01:21:46.020
around what percentage of consultant time

01:21:46.020 --> 01:21:48.390
is actually spent on labour wards.

01:21:48.390 --> 01:21:49.390
- Donald, thank you.

01:21:51.222 --> 01:21:53.370
Jackie do you want to comment or sorry,

01:21:53.370 --> 01:21:54.930
just respond to any of the questions?

01:21:54.930 --> 01:21:58.050
- Absolutely agree and
endorse Donald's narrative.

01:21:58.050 --> 01:22:00.900
Just to pick up on the numbers,

01:22:00.900 --> 01:22:05.070
I think you mentioned the
number of midwives, Andrew,

01:22:05.070 --> 01:22:08.430
and totally understand and accept.

01:22:08.430 --> 01:22:11.190
I'm a midwife, I still work clinically

01:22:11.190 --> 01:22:16.190
and understand the challenges
that the current system has

01:22:16.650 --> 01:22:18.570
in providing safe and personal care,

01:22:18.570 --> 01:22:21.360
but that is our absolute
number one priority

01:22:21.360 --> 01:22:24.330
and it is front and centre of
everything that we're doing.

01:22:24.330 --> 01:22:28.050
Reading through and and reflecting

01:22:28.050 --> 01:22:29.970
on Morecambe Bay seven years ago,

01:22:29.970 --> 01:22:33.030
Ockenden interim, Ockenden
final, the East Kent report

01:22:33.030 --> 01:22:35.610
now, there are recurring themes

01:22:35.610 --> 01:22:39.180
and this is why right
now we are drawing a line

01:22:39.180 --> 01:22:43.890
and looking at how we can
get maternity services right

01:22:43.890 --> 01:22:48.720
and that's why we're
undertaking listening events

01:22:48.720 --> 01:22:50.790
with service users.

01:22:50.790 --> 01:22:53.310
So I know that better
births, our maternity policy

01:22:53.310 --> 01:22:55.740
five years ago listened
to some 9,000 women.

01:22:55.740 --> 01:22:58.410
We are listening again

01:22:58.410 --> 01:23:00.420
and not just listening but understanding,

01:23:00.420 --> 01:23:02.190
not just understanding but acting

01:23:02.190 --> 01:23:03.660
because that's the only way

01:23:03.660 --> 01:23:07.070
that we can inspire and
engage public confidence.

01:23:07.070 --> 01:23:10.560
So the listening events are
happening at the moment.

01:23:10.560 --> 01:23:14.070
We also have a survey that
will help service users

01:23:14.070 --> 01:23:17.850
and clinicians, midwives,
obstetricians, anaesthetists,

01:23:17.850 --> 01:23:21.720
contribute to our single delivery plan,

01:23:21.720 --> 01:23:26.010
which we hope to have
published some time next year

01:23:26.010 --> 01:23:27.660
so that they can contribute,

01:23:27.660 --> 01:23:29.940
they can have their say
about what they've heard,

01:23:29.940 --> 01:23:31.800
what they've seen, what we've done

01:23:31.800 --> 01:23:34.200
and how we absolutely get this right.

01:23:34.200 --> 01:23:36.420
And Donald alluded to the listening

01:23:36.420 --> 01:23:38.430
and the narrative that we're having

01:23:38.430 --> 01:23:40.020
with trust boards at the moment

01:23:40.020 --> 01:23:42.090
that have maternity
within their portfolio.

01:23:42.090 --> 01:23:47.090
And we did our, 103
meetings we've done so far.

01:23:48.000 --> 01:23:50.100
So we've got some 20 to go.

01:23:50.100 --> 01:23:52.530
And the focus is very much
as Donald has mentioned

01:23:52.530 --> 01:23:54.690
about culture and leadership

01:23:54.690 --> 01:23:59.280
and what a board level
responsibility looks like.

01:23:59.280 --> 01:24:01.290
But in relation to midwifery numbers,

01:24:01.290 --> 01:24:05.337
I appreciate that the numbers
aren't looking very good

01:24:05.337 --> 01:24:08.730
and I, more than anybody,
know about the challenges,

01:24:08.730 --> 01:24:10.530
about what that will actually mean.

01:24:10.530 --> 01:24:14.010
We are pressing into the
international recruitment market.

01:24:14.010 --> 01:24:17.490
We're looking at very
specifically domestic supply,

01:24:17.490 --> 01:24:21.900
but also it's really important
for us to focus on retention.

01:24:21.900 --> 01:24:24.090
Because there is no point
in getting people in

01:24:24.090 --> 01:24:25.260
if we can't keep them in.

01:24:25.260 --> 01:24:27.911
We've got our colleagues in schools

01:24:27.911 --> 01:24:30.270
encouraging people to choose midwifery

01:24:30.270 --> 01:24:31.710
as a career of choice.

01:24:31.710 --> 01:24:34.590
We've got a pastoral support offer,

01:24:34.590 --> 01:24:37.560
which means that there are
midwives that may have retired

01:24:37.560 --> 01:24:40.500
and are coming back to
do some pastoral support

01:24:40.500 --> 01:24:43.260
for the juniors that are
feeling really challenged

01:24:43.260 --> 01:24:46.480
in this current time, to encourage people

01:24:47.518 --> 01:24:49.050
to stay within maternity services.

01:24:49.050 --> 01:24:50.466
And there are lots more things,

01:24:50.466 --> 01:24:51.907
the quad underrate programme,

01:24:51.907 --> 01:24:54.720
the aspiring directs
of midwifery programme,

01:24:54.720 --> 01:24:56.460
the professional midwifery advocates,

01:24:56.460 --> 01:24:58.860
the midwifery ambassadors,
and I could go on,

01:24:58.860 --> 01:25:02.310
but we're very much focused on listening,

01:25:02.310 --> 01:25:04.320
understanding, and acting.

01:25:04.320 --> 01:25:05.320
- Jackie, thank you.

01:25:07.410 --> 01:25:11.487
We could, and I mean the
topic absolutely merits it,

01:25:11.487 --> 01:25:13.380
but I am going to move us on actually.

01:25:13.380 --> 01:25:15.600
And the recommendation clearly is

01:25:15.600 --> 01:25:18.450
you come back to us in a,
probably not the next one,

01:25:18.450 --> 01:25:20.100
but in a couple of meetings time,

01:25:20.100 --> 01:25:21.780
to understand where you are

01:25:21.780 --> 01:25:25.173
with regard to this
plan, this delivery plan.

01:25:26.940 --> 01:25:30.000
The emotion you showed
at the start there, Ruth,

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:31.830
is unsurprising.

01:25:31.830 --> 01:25:34.170
This is a really actually distressing,

01:25:34.170 --> 01:25:37.324
it's a terribly distressing report to read

01:25:37.324 --> 01:25:40.380
and without wishing to
sort of have an argument

01:25:40.380 --> 01:25:41.970
with education or other parts

01:25:41.970 --> 01:25:43.740
of sort of public service supply

01:25:43.740 --> 01:25:45.240
or other industries, actually,

01:25:46.110 --> 01:25:47.790
it's very hard to think of an industry

01:25:47.790 --> 01:25:51.360
that has a more important
contribution to society

01:25:51.360 --> 01:25:52.560
and the population than health.

01:25:52.560 --> 01:25:54.780
And within it, when we
see this in maternity

01:25:54.780 --> 01:25:56.400
and the delivery of babies,

01:25:56.400 --> 01:25:58.860
I think it is deeply distressing.

01:25:58.860 --> 01:26:01.320
So this needs to get sorted out

01:26:01.320 --> 01:26:03.237
and it's partly the
things we've talked about

01:26:03.237 --> 01:26:04.590
and the questions that occurring.

01:26:04.590 --> 01:26:06.360
It's partly how you are focusing.

01:26:06.360 --> 01:26:08.520
So it's numbers and
it's culture and so on.

01:26:08.520 --> 01:26:10.020
Do you want-
- Sorry Richard,

01:26:11.190 --> 01:26:13.500
I'm actually quiet in this subject.

01:26:13.500 --> 01:26:14.627
I'm usually very vocal on it.

01:26:14.627 --> 01:26:17.610
I like just finishes with
this one exploitation

01:26:17.610 --> 01:26:18.510
to Ruth and colleagues,

01:26:18.510 --> 01:26:20.280
which is I'm going to make Amanda

01:26:20.280 --> 01:26:22.290
and Julian's job more difficult.

01:26:22.290 --> 01:26:24.300
And that is to when you come out your plan

01:26:24.300 --> 01:26:25.680
in two board meetings time,

01:26:25.680 --> 01:26:27.850
be challenging, be direct, be clear

01:26:28.980 --> 01:26:33.360
and really push us so we sort
this out once and for all.

01:26:33.360 --> 01:26:36.270
Seven years ago is when
we had the first report

01:26:36.270 --> 01:26:38.340
and since then we've had three others,

01:26:38.340 --> 01:26:39.663
so really push us.

01:26:40.830 --> 01:26:42.987
- Well said, okay, thank you very much.

01:26:42.987 --> 01:26:45.300
And Ruth, thank you, thank you very much.

01:26:45.300 --> 01:26:48.540
So pausing only very briefly,

01:26:48.540 --> 01:26:51.159
we're going to move on I
think now to item six on the

01:26:51.159 --> 01:26:53.340
agenda, which is early diagnosis

01:26:53.340 --> 01:26:55.830
and the progress that is being made.

01:26:55.830 --> 01:26:59.760
And I think Cally, are you
leading on this and with David?

01:26:59.760 --> 01:27:01.590
- I am, I am, thanks Chairman.

01:27:01.590 --> 01:27:03.270
I'm accompanied by David Fitzgerald

01:27:03.270 --> 01:27:05.100
who's the director of policy and strategy

01:27:05.100 --> 01:27:06.540
in the National Cancer Team.

01:27:06.540 --> 01:27:09.180
So I suspect you are a
little bit of short of time.

01:27:09.180 --> 01:27:12.600
So what I'm planning to do
is just talk about the aim

01:27:12.600 --> 01:27:14.790
of our programme on early
diagnosis of cancer,

01:27:14.790 --> 01:27:17.190
where we're up to and then next steps

01:27:17.190 --> 01:27:18.660
and very happy to take questions.

01:27:18.660 --> 01:27:21.580
So the aim very importantly

01:27:22.440 --> 01:27:24.900
is to try and significantly shift the dial

01:27:24.900 --> 01:27:27.870
on early diagnosis of
cancer in this country.

01:27:27.870 --> 01:27:30.670
We know that that's a major
determinant of survival

01:27:31.950 --> 01:27:34.530
and of course first of
all you want to prevent,

01:27:34.530 --> 01:27:36.300
then you want early fast diagnosis

01:27:36.300 --> 01:27:38.910
when there are more
curative options available

01:27:38.910 --> 01:27:41.820
and then precision treatment
and good follow-up care.

01:27:41.820 --> 01:27:43.500
So having a strategy and a plan

01:27:43.500 --> 01:27:44.850
that focuses on early diagnosis

01:27:44.850 --> 01:27:46.770
I think is absolutely crucial.

01:27:46.770 --> 01:27:49.350
And of course what we're
trying to ensure that we do

01:27:49.350 --> 01:27:52.890
is move from 55% of
patients being diagnosed

01:27:52.890 --> 01:27:57.153
at stage one or stage two of
their cancer to 75% by 2028.

01:27:57.990 --> 01:28:00.330
If we do that across a range of cancers,

01:28:00.330 --> 01:28:02.520
it means saving 55,000 more lives,

01:28:02.520 --> 01:28:06.150
so people surviving
for five years or more.

01:28:06.150 --> 01:28:08.043
So very important ambition.

01:28:10.080 --> 01:28:11.283
It will also mean

01:28:11.283 --> 01:28:13.650
that we can tackle some
of the health inequalities

01:28:13.650 --> 01:28:17.880
'cause that we find in communities
that are more deprived.

01:28:17.880 --> 01:28:19.380
So we're doing quite
a lot of targeted work

01:28:19.380 --> 01:28:20.730
both in communications

01:28:20.730 --> 01:28:24.420
and in terms of trying to
encourage people to come forward.

01:28:24.420 --> 01:28:27.000
Level of social deprivation
is a major determinant

01:28:27.000 --> 01:28:31.290
of your outcome if you
are affected by cancer.

01:28:31.290 --> 01:28:33.780
So this early diagnosis initiative

01:28:33.780 --> 01:28:35.850
is very important for patient care.

01:28:35.850 --> 01:28:38.860
It's very important for the
productive use of NHS resources

01:28:39.720 --> 01:28:41.700
and it'll place us if we get this right

01:28:41.700 --> 01:28:44.820
among the best performing
countries internationally.

01:28:44.820 --> 01:28:48.270
We're very good in some
cancers internationally

01:28:48.270 --> 01:28:52.110
where we have one stop shops
and screening programmes,

01:28:52.110 --> 01:28:55.470
but for a range of cancers,
the major reason our survival

01:28:55.470 --> 01:28:57.300
isn't as good as the best internationally

01:28:57.300 --> 01:28:58.983
is about late diagnosis.

01:29:00.840 --> 01:29:05.840
So the majority of that
shift that, it's a 20% shift,

01:29:05.970 --> 01:29:09.660
14% of that is related
to known interventions

01:29:09.660 --> 01:29:10.710
that are already underway.

01:29:10.710 --> 01:29:12.840
And I'll come onto the
targeted lung health checks,

01:29:12.840 --> 01:29:15.390
that is a major part of that programme.

01:29:15.390 --> 01:29:17.730
The other 6% is innovation

01:29:17.730 --> 01:29:19.710
and cancer's a great
subject for innovation

01:29:19.710 --> 01:29:21.300
'cause there's so much
transformation going on

01:29:21.300 --> 01:29:24.420
in better diagnosis and
precision treatment.

01:29:24.420 --> 01:29:28.560
And that innovation
includes our work with GRAIL

01:29:28.560 --> 01:29:31.440
to look at whether we
can be the first country

01:29:31.440 --> 01:29:33.630
to have a blood test
to detect cancer early,

01:29:33.630 --> 01:29:35.160
particularly in some of those cancers

01:29:35.160 --> 01:29:37.320
that globally are hard to pick up early,

01:29:37.320 --> 01:29:38.790
like pancreatic cancer.

01:29:38.790 --> 01:29:42.543
So that is underway in
pilot form at this stage.

01:29:43.680 --> 01:29:46.080
We're obviously delivering
this transformation strategy

01:29:46.080 --> 01:29:49.860
at a time of enormous pressure in the NHS

01:29:49.860 --> 01:29:52.110
and we're obviously aware that some people

01:29:52.110 --> 01:29:53.790
are waiting longer for tests

01:29:53.790 --> 01:29:56.290
and for their first
treatments than we would wish.

01:29:57.180 --> 01:30:00.900
But on the upside we have
been doing a lot of work

01:30:00.900 --> 01:30:04.680
both as an NHS and with
cancer charity partners

01:30:04.680 --> 01:30:07.440
to stimulate people coming forward.

01:30:07.440 --> 01:30:10.530
We saw a dip during the
pandemic at an early stage.

01:30:10.530 --> 01:30:14.190
We've now been seeing for over,
well, nearly 18 months now,

01:30:14.190 --> 01:30:16.290
record levels of urgent cancer referrals.

01:30:16.290 --> 01:30:19.140
So they're now at 129%
of pre-pandemic levels

01:30:19.140 --> 01:30:20.190
and that's a good thing.

01:30:20.190 --> 01:30:22.040
We want those people to come forward.

01:30:22.920 --> 01:30:24.270
The key thing obviously is to make sure

01:30:24.270 --> 01:30:27.180
we have a diagnostic
and treatment capacity

01:30:27.180 --> 01:30:29.010
to match the demand.

01:30:29.010 --> 01:30:31.157
So treatment capacity is
running at about 107%.

01:30:31.157 --> 01:30:35.550
So you can see there's a
gap, but it's very, very good

01:30:35.550 --> 01:30:37.830
that we're getting so
many people coming forward

01:30:37.830 --> 01:30:40.650
and working very productively
with the cancer charities,

01:30:40.650 --> 01:30:43.260
many of whom have very
good public help lines

01:30:43.260 --> 01:30:45.393
along with the NHS to help us with that.

01:30:47.100 --> 01:30:49.080
And of course the investment
in the cancer workforce

01:30:49.080 --> 01:30:51.450
and particularly the
community diagnostic centres

01:30:51.450 --> 01:30:54.090
and direct GP referral,
crucial for cancer,

01:30:54.090 --> 01:30:56.940
again for early fast diagnosis.

01:30:56.940 --> 01:30:58.320
So that's all a good thing.

01:30:58.320 --> 01:31:00.750
And while any wait for a patient

01:31:00.750 --> 01:31:02.370
is a source of huge anxiety

01:31:02.370 --> 01:31:04.800
until they either have
a diagnosis and a plan

01:31:04.800 --> 01:31:06.630
or an exclusion of cancer,

01:31:06.630 --> 01:31:07.583
I think it's important to note

01:31:07.583 --> 01:31:10.683
that of the patients
on that 62-day pathway,

01:31:12.150 --> 01:31:14.190
the majority will not have cancer.

01:31:14.190 --> 01:31:16.620
So focusing on this
diagnostic part of the pathway

01:31:16.620 --> 01:31:17.940
is really important.

01:31:17.940 --> 01:31:20.250
6% will, the rest will not,

01:31:20.250 --> 01:31:21.990
but it's very important
that we get those patients

01:31:21.990 --> 01:31:23.580
through the diagnostic
part of their journey

01:31:23.580 --> 01:31:24.783
as quickly as possible.

01:31:26.340 --> 01:31:27.930
So as you can see from the report,

01:31:27.930 --> 01:31:29.490
the strategy on earlier diagnosis

01:31:29.490 --> 01:31:31.170
is based on six core strands.

01:31:31.170 --> 01:31:32.370
So these are the things we're doing

01:31:32.370 --> 01:31:33.750
along with the innovation theme

01:31:33.750 --> 01:31:35.340
and the use of the Galleri test

01:31:35.340 --> 01:31:38.070
or the GRAIL test that's sometimes known.

01:31:38.070 --> 01:31:40.830
And the one that I just wanted to mention,

01:31:40.830 --> 01:31:41.970
apart from the campaigns,

01:31:41.970 --> 01:31:44.580
we're running on timely presentation

01:31:44.580 --> 01:31:46.020
which are running continuously

01:31:46.020 --> 01:31:49.470
and include new campaigns
on things like fear.

01:31:49.470 --> 01:31:51.030
So not just targeting symptoms

01:31:51.030 --> 01:31:52.950
but actually trying to
target people's fear

01:31:52.950 --> 01:31:54.030
in coming forward 'cause we know

01:31:54.030 --> 01:31:56.580
that's still quite a significant factor.

01:31:56.580 --> 01:31:58.770
But the lung health checks,

01:31:58.770 --> 01:32:03.770
they're now live in 33 locations,
lung is very high volume,

01:32:04.860 --> 01:32:08.370
we diagnose very late and
if you diagnose early,

01:32:08.370 --> 01:32:09.780
you can often cure.

01:32:09.780 --> 01:32:11.880
So that's the scale of difference
we're trying to achieve.

01:32:11.880 --> 01:32:13.920
And in those lung locations

01:32:13.920 --> 01:32:17.580
where we're piloting this
lung check we started with

01:32:17.580 --> 01:32:19.950
and we're prioritising locations

01:32:19.950 --> 01:32:21.330
where there are high levels

01:32:21.330 --> 01:32:23.500
of social deprivation and smoking

01:32:24.630 --> 01:32:25.920
and we found that we are completely

01:32:25.920 --> 01:32:28.890
reversing late stage disease.

01:32:28.890 --> 01:32:33.060
So in the sort of over a
thousand cancers detected so far,

01:32:33.060 --> 01:32:35.790
we've moved from under 30%
of people being diagnosed

01:32:35.790 --> 01:32:38.820
at an early stage to 76%.

01:32:38.820 --> 01:32:40.230
So you can just see the impact of this

01:32:40.230 --> 01:32:42.420
if we roll it out across the country

01:32:42.420 --> 01:32:43.860
and you'll note from the paper,

01:32:43.860 --> 01:32:45.690
we're going tumour type by tumour type

01:32:45.690 --> 01:32:47.230
to see what else can we do

01:32:48.300 --> 01:32:50.220
to completely change up the way people

01:32:50.220 --> 01:32:53.700
are able to access services
and be risk stratified, sorry,

01:32:53.700 --> 01:32:56.010
that's a horrible expression,
not very patient friendly,

01:32:56.010 --> 01:32:58.980
but to make sure that their experience

01:32:58.980 --> 01:33:00.750
is streamlined, swift and good

01:33:00.750 --> 01:33:04.770
and we make the best use
of specialist NHS resource.

01:33:04.770 --> 01:33:08.520
So lung trucks being rolled out

01:33:08.520 --> 01:33:11.310
with the paper talks about
some work in liver disease

01:33:11.310 --> 01:33:12.360
and liver surveillance

01:33:12.360 --> 01:33:14.730
and also some work we're
just about to start

01:33:14.730 --> 01:33:16.303
supported by Jewish charity

01:33:16.303 --> 01:33:18.510
with the Ashkenazi Jewish population

01:33:18.510 --> 01:33:20.280
who have a higher risk of the BRCA gene

01:33:20.280 --> 01:33:22.923
and therefore development
of breast cancer.

01:33:24.390 --> 01:33:27.063
So Amanda sort of delivered
my best line earlier,

01:33:28.260 --> 01:33:30.180
which is apart from, you know,

01:33:30.180 --> 01:33:31.590
recovering people coming forward,

01:33:31.590 --> 01:33:33.600
which is incredibly important,

01:33:33.600 --> 01:33:34.710
but the really brilliant thing

01:33:34.710 --> 01:33:36.663
is that I know it's a small start,

01:33:37.500 --> 01:33:41.070
but the number of people,
so the percentage of people

01:33:41.070 --> 01:33:44.410
being diagnosed early
has been pretty static

01:33:45.270 --> 01:33:50.010
at sort of around 54,
55% since records began.

01:33:50.010 --> 01:33:53.670
And it's just shifting to just below 57%.

01:33:53.670 --> 01:33:55.890
And that's brilliant having
come through a pandemic as well

01:33:55.890 --> 01:33:57.000
where we did see a dip.

01:33:57.000 --> 01:34:00.120
So it gives me sort of comfort

01:34:00.120 --> 01:34:01.620
that we're going in the right direction

01:34:01.620 --> 01:34:03.180
and we need to accelerate and consolidate

01:34:03.180 --> 01:34:06.030
some of these interventions to
make a difference for people.

01:34:06.030 --> 01:34:07.320
I'm going to pause there, Chairman,

01:34:07.320 --> 01:34:08.610
but happy to take questions.

01:34:08.610 --> 01:34:09.443
- That'd be good.

01:34:09.443 --> 01:34:12.480
Can I open up for comments
or questions for Cally?

01:34:12.480 --> 01:34:13.683
So Munir, please.

01:34:15.240 --> 01:34:17.010
- Thanks very much for the paper.

01:34:17.010 --> 01:34:21.090
Just to wanting to ask about
the Galleri trial really.

01:34:21.090 --> 01:34:25.530
When is it going to read
out and if it is successful,

01:34:25.530 --> 01:34:26.880
have you thought about how often

01:34:26.880 --> 01:34:29.550
people would need the
Galleri test done, you know,

01:34:29.550 --> 01:34:32.490
in order to identify cancers

01:34:32.490 --> 01:34:33.720
as they occur as they get older?

01:34:33.720 --> 01:34:35.340
- Yes, thank you.

01:34:35.340 --> 01:34:39.720
So the next stage is to
pass certain sort of,

01:34:39.720 --> 01:34:41.790
we got some sort of health economic tests

01:34:41.790 --> 01:34:46.290
and if the early data passes those tests

01:34:46.290 --> 01:34:50.250
on sort of benefit the next stage.

01:34:50.250 --> 01:34:53.130
So we've got 140,000 people
recruited across the country.

01:34:53.130 --> 01:34:54.690
The next stage will be to roll it out

01:34:54.690 --> 01:34:57.960
to a million people from April 2024.

01:34:57.960 --> 01:35:00.720
So, there's a pipeline

01:35:00.720 --> 01:35:03.240
with health economic
checks at various points

01:35:03.240 --> 01:35:06.180
to meet our contract
conditions with GRAIL.

01:35:06.180 --> 01:35:07.530
Tim, I know, knows a lot about this

01:35:07.530 --> 01:35:09.540
and some of the other work
going on on liquid biopsies.

01:35:09.540 --> 01:35:11.130
- So sorry if you get through clinical

01:35:11.130 --> 01:35:12.750
and cost effectiveness

01:35:12.750 --> 01:35:15.060
how often would people require the test?

01:35:15.060 --> 01:35:16.768
- I think at the moment, where is it?

01:35:16.768 --> 01:35:18.750
We are talking about every
three years at the moment

01:35:18.750 --> 01:35:19.583
I think, aren't we?

01:35:19.583 --> 01:35:21.360
But we're still looking
at the operational model

01:35:21.360 --> 01:35:23.760
post full rollout, is that right?

01:35:23.760 --> 01:35:25.020
- Yeah, will depend to some extent

01:35:25.020 --> 01:35:26.250
on the outcome of the trial.

01:35:26.250 --> 01:35:29.010
I mean at the moment we are, you know,

01:35:29.010 --> 01:35:32.580
the people within the trial
are being tested annually

01:35:32.580 --> 01:35:36.090
initially and that will be the results

01:35:36.090 --> 01:35:37.320
of the second annual test

01:35:37.320 --> 01:35:39.990
that will then determine
whether or not we can proceed

01:35:39.990 --> 01:35:42.300
with the rollout in 24/25.

01:35:42.300 --> 01:35:43.133
- Thank you.

01:35:43.133 --> 01:35:44.959
- Tim?
- I just,

01:35:44.959 --> 01:35:46.590
I'm so glad you asked that question Munir,

01:35:46.590 --> 01:35:47.817
and that you raised it.

01:35:49.340 --> 01:35:52.170
It is difficult to overstate

01:35:52.170 --> 01:35:56.910
the transformative potential
of, and I'll just say

01:35:56.910 --> 01:35:59.580
being vendor agnostic for a second,

01:35:59.580 --> 01:36:03.104
there are a lot of fast
followers in this space

01:36:03.104 --> 01:36:05.940
who are looking at circulating tumour DNA

01:36:05.940 --> 01:36:08.790
and actually some really interesting

01:36:08.790 --> 01:36:10.680
fast following technology.

01:36:10.680 --> 01:36:13.920
But I just want to state really clearly

01:36:13.920 --> 01:36:16.620
what an extraordinary
transformation it would be

01:36:16.620 --> 01:36:19.650
for the delivery of all services

01:36:19.650 --> 01:36:23.820
because of the change in the
utilisation of the services

01:36:23.820 --> 01:36:27.990
that a positive trial
here would manifest as.

01:36:27.990 --> 01:36:31.110
I mean it is really, really profound

01:36:31.110 --> 01:36:32.120
and if there is a...

01:36:34.320 --> 01:36:37.050
I shouldn't overstate the potential here,

01:36:37.050 --> 01:36:42.050
but we could use a really big
win given all the pressures,

01:36:42.420 --> 01:36:45.933
this would be a really big win

01:36:45.933 --> 01:36:48.630
for the people of this country.

01:36:48.630 --> 01:36:49.800
- Thank you, and Mike?

01:36:49.800 --> 01:36:52.770
Mike, do you want to ask
a question or comment?

01:36:52.770 --> 01:36:54.120
- Yep, enjoyed the paper

01:36:54.120 --> 01:36:56.760
and thank you very much
and fantastic work.

01:36:56.760 --> 01:37:01.357
And what would be a target
for the early diagnostic rate

01:37:02.310 --> 01:37:03.810
or early diagnosis rate?

01:37:03.810 --> 01:37:06.180
What would you be shooting for?

01:37:06.180 --> 01:37:07.671
- Well, you mean the 75%?

01:37:07.671 --> 01:37:10.059
So we're shifting by 20%, yeah.

01:37:10.059 --> 01:37:15.030
So records began in 2013
of this particular metric

01:37:15.030 --> 01:37:17.280
and it's been pretty flat.

01:37:17.280 --> 01:37:20.820
So the 20% is quite ambitious

01:37:20.820 --> 01:37:22.800
and we have to make
progress on all cancers,

01:37:22.800 --> 01:37:26.610
including some of the smaller
later detected cancers

01:37:26.610 --> 01:37:27.920
to get to that 75%.

01:37:27.920 --> 01:37:30.833
So that's the real ambition by 2028.

01:37:30.833 --> 01:37:32.910
- Okay, thank you.
- Thank you.

01:37:32.910 --> 01:37:34.410
David, do you want...

01:37:34.410 --> 01:37:36.030
David, then Wol and then An.

01:37:36.030 --> 01:37:38.700
- So thank you for the report Cally.

01:37:38.700 --> 01:37:40.140
Again, very encouraging

01:37:40.140 --> 01:37:42.300
and you know there's lots of optimism

01:37:42.300 --> 01:37:45.540
and hope I think included in what you did,

01:37:45.540 --> 01:37:46.653
what you've set out.

01:37:47.550 --> 01:37:49.350
Just to add, Richard,

01:37:49.350 --> 01:37:53.010
