but just this is a really
good example for me

01:41:58.230 --> 01:42:01.500
of the power of having a
single national health service

01:42:01.500 --> 01:42:04.470
where effectively we have
made a priority of this

01:42:04.470 --> 01:42:05.970
in the long-term plan,

01:42:05.970 --> 01:42:09.030
we identified that diagnostic
capacity was a bottleneck.

01:42:09.030 --> 01:42:11.340
We've put the investment behind it

01:42:11.340 --> 01:42:13.650
and we are actually kind
of seeing the results

01:42:13.650 --> 01:42:17.280
because we've able to mobilise
extra diagnostic capacity,

01:42:17.280 --> 01:42:19.920
extra treatment capacity and also ensuring

01:42:19.920 --> 01:42:21.300
that we're mobilising primary care

01:42:21.300 --> 01:42:22.860
to kind of get the referrals

01:42:22.860 --> 01:42:24.210
and just to make the point

01:42:24.210 --> 01:42:27.000
that it's precisely because we
are a national health service

01:42:27.000 --> 01:42:28.410
and we've had the investment

01:42:28.410 --> 01:42:30.480
that we've actually been able
to make this improvement.

01:42:30.480 --> 01:42:32.040
And it's a very obvious
point but it seems to me

01:42:32.040 --> 01:42:33.960
it's really quite an
important one to point out.

01:42:33.960 --> 01:42:35.040
- Thank you.

01:42:35.040 --> 01:42:37.170
So Cally, thank you.

01:42:37.170 --> 01:42:39.960
That's very good indeed,
thank you very much.

01:42:39.960 --> 01:42:44.310
Coming on to prevention, Steve,
I think this is item seven,

01:42:44.310 --> 01:42:45.540
you're up with Matthew I think.

01:42:45.540 --> 01:42:47.790
- And I've got Matt here.
- Indeed.

01:42:47.790 --> 01:42:50.610
- So let's continue on
the theme of identifying

01:42:50.610 --> 01:42:54.690
and managing disease early is
a very, very good thing to do.

01:42:54.690 --> 01:42:56.520
Everybody is familiar with the axiom

01:42:56.520 --> 01:42:58.590
prevention is better than cure

01:42:58.590 --> 01:43:01.590
and I think it's a really
opportune time to revisit that

01:43:01.590 --> 01:43:04.230
and remind everybody just how true it is.

01:43:04.230 --> 01:43:07.860
So a few key points from me to start with,

01:43:07.860 --> 01:43:09.870
notwithstanding the comments
I might have made earlier

01:43:09.870 --> 01:43:10.703
about everybody getting older,

01:43:10.703 --> 01:43:13.620
the population getting
older and more comorbid.

01:43:13.620 --> 01:43:14.970
It is really important to remember

01:43:14.970 --> 01:43:17.880
that much of the disease
burden as we get older

01:43:17.880 --> 01:43:20.820
is preventable and/or modifiable.

01:43:20.820 --> 01:43:24.300
So that's cardiovascular
disease, it's strokes,

01:43:24.300 --> 01:43:25.830
it's diabetes.

01:43:25.830 --> 01:43:28.623
So there is an awful lot that we can do.

01:43:29.880 --> 01:43:32.520
David has mentioned that
in the last few months

01:43:32.520 --> 01:43:36.393
we've been seeing excess
mortality in the UK,

01:43:37.920 --> 01:43:39.510
and indeed in some
other countries as well.

01:43:39.510 --> 01:43:41.130
Now the reasons for that,

01:43:41.130 --> 01:43:42.900
the underlying conditions are complex

01:43:42.900 --> 01:43:45.150
and the underlying cause is complex.

01:43:45.150 --> 01:43:46.950
I'm confident the ONS and
public health colleagues

01:43:46.950 --> 01:43:48.090
are looking into that.

01:43:48.090 --> 01:43:49.860
But certainly cardiovascular disease

01:43:49.860 --> 01:43:53.580
and respiratory diseases
are part of the conditions

01:43:53.580 --> 01:43:55.830
where we have seen that excess mortality.

01:43:55.830 --> 01:43:57.270
And of course we've talked about pressure

01:43:57.270 --> 01:44:01.920
on emergency care systems,
urgent emergency care systems

01:44:01.920 --> 01:44:06.060
and much of the conveying the
attendances we see at hospital

01:44:06.060 --> 01:44:08.760
is because of conditions such
as cardiovascular disease

01:44:08.760 --> 01:44:10.140
and acute respiratory illness

01:44:10.140 --> 01:44:13.770
on the background of
chronic lung conditions.

01:44:13.770 --> 01:44:16.020
So it is absolutely in our interest

01:44:16.020 --> 01:44:18.840
and the population's interest
to focus on prevention

01:44:18.840 --> 01:44:21.450
to reduce deaths, reduce disease burden

01:44:21.450 --> 01:44:23.527
and reduce the pressure on the NHS.

01:44:24.690 --> 01:44:29.370
Now clearly a lot of
people focus on prevention,

01:44:29.370 --> 01:44:32.730
public health colleagues,
local government,

01:44:32.730 --> 01:44:35.340
but if Chris Whitty was here
and he sends his apologies

01:44:35.340 --> 01:44:37.620
'cause he would've loved to be
here but he couldn't be here,

01:44:37.620 --> 01:44:41.697
he would say that the NHS
absolutely has a key role in this.

01:44:41.697 --> 01:44:44.790
And the one thing we
mustn't do is underestimate

01:44:44.790 --> 01:44:48.000
quite just what the NHS and NHS England

01:44:48.000 --> 01:44:51.900
and its constituent bodies
can do in prevention.

01:44:51.900 --> 01:44:53.940
And in fact we recognise
that in the long-term plan

01:44:53.940 --> 01:44:54.840
a few years ago,

01:44:54.840 --> 01:44:57.840
and there are many programmes
in the long-term plan,

01:44:57.840 --> 01:45:01.710
from managing obesity, digital
weight management programmes,

01:45:01.710 --> 01:45:04.080
through to smoking cessation,

01:45:04.080 --> 01:45:06.480
through to management of blood pressure

01:45:06.480 --> 01:45:08.100
and management of cholesterol

01:45:08.100 --> 01:45:10.000
and management of atrial fibrillation,

01:45:11.490 --> 01:45:14.310
arrhythmia is responsible for stroke,

01:45:14.310 --> 01:45:15.810
that we have been developing
in the background.

01:45:15.810 --> 01:45:19.290
And to give credit to our
teams nationally and locally,

01:45:19.290 --> 01:45:21.600
that work has progressed
during the pandemic.

01:45:21.600 --> 01:45:23.820
And Matt will say a few things about it.

01:45:23.820 --> 01:45:25.860
But, for a whole host of reasons,

01:45:25.860 --> 01:45:28.440
it's really important
that we revisit that,

01:45:28.440 --> 01:45:29.700
firstly for the reasons I've said

01:45:29.700 --> 01:45:32.010
in terms of the pressures
we're under at the moment.

01:45:32.010 --> 01:45:33.180
Secondly,

01:45:33.180 --> 01:45:36.240
because with the creation
of integrated care boards,

01:45:36.240 --> 01:45:38.830
there is a particular opportunity

01:45:39.900 --> 01:45:42.120
to redouble down on this work.

01:45:42.120 --> 01:45:45.150
And of course the core purpose
of integrated care boards

01:45:45.150 --> 01:45:47.080
is to integrate health for population

01:45:47.940 --> 01:45:51.450
and what could be more in
that space than prevention.

01:45:51.450 --> 01:45:53.190
So for all those reasons, Amanda,

01:45:53.190 --> 01:45:55.020
who I know cares passionately about this,

01:45:55.020 --> 01:45:59.880
asked Chris and I, to do
some work over the summer.

01:45:59.880 --> 01:46:02.640
This is the result of the
first part of that work,

01:46:02.640 --> 01:46:04.410
in a sense a stock take of the programmes

01:46:04.410 --> 01:46:06.420
that we've got in place and a reassurance

01:46:06.420 --> 01:46:09.690
that those programmes are
functioning and they're building.

01:46:09.690 --> 01:46:11.520
Secondly, a sort of gap analysis,

01:46:11.520 --> 01:46:13.320
what more could we do?

01:46:13.320 --> 01:46:15.630
But thirdly, an opportunity to think ahead

01:46:15.630 --> 01:46:18.510
about how we move to the
next phase of the work,

01:46:18.510 --> 01:46:21.120
which is to really bring in our colleagues

01:46:21.120 --> 01:46:22.530
in the integrated care boards

01:46:22.530 --> 01:46:25.200
and ensure that they and not us

01:46:25.200 --> 01:46:28.000
are working to design and co-develop

01:46:29.850 --> 01:46:31.710
what in a sense is
needed to take this work

01:46:31.710 --> 01:46:35.610
and make the sum greater
than the sum of its parts.

01:46:35.610 --> 01:46:37.500
So with that I'm going
to hand over to Matt

01:46:37.500 --> 01:46:39.300
for a few words and then discussion.

01:46:40.530 --> 01:46:41.759
- Yeah, thank you.

01:46:41.759 --> 01:46:42.780
So to provide a bit of context.

01:46:42.780 --> 01:46:46.413
Healthy life expectancy rose
throughout the 20th century.

01:46:47.250 --> 01:46:50.880
That was driven in part by
improvements in healthcare,

01:46:50.880 --> 01:46:53.400
but the rate of improvement
stalled significantly

01:46:53.400 --> 01:46:55.710
from 2010 onwards.

01:46:55.710 --> 01:46:56.850
And that's been associated

01:46:56.850 --> 01:46:58.980
with an increase in health disparities

01:46:58.980 --> 01:46:59.910
because it's stored more

01:46:59.910 --> 01:47:02.820
amongst the more deprived
groups in the community.

01:47:02.820 --> 01:47:05.010
And when we look into
the data behind that,

01:47:05.010 --> 01:47:08.670
the trend has been driven by
CBD mortality in particular.

01:47:08.670 --> 01:47:10.860
It's a major cause of premature death,

01:47:10.860 --> 01:47:15.360
so under 75 death, along with
cancer and respiratory disease

01:47:15.360 --> 01:47:16.620
and all of those diseases

01:47:16.620 --> 01:47:18.963
are driven by modifiable risk factors.

01:47:19.890 --> 01:47:21.990
Those risk factors and the conditions

01:47:21.990 --> 01:47:23.970
that they give rise to
tend to be clustered

01:47:23.970 --> 01:47:26.217
within certain segments of the population,

01:47:26.217 --> 01:47:30.810
and that counts for about 40%
of the disparity and outcomes

01:47:30.810 --> 01:47:33.840
between the most and the least deprived.

01:47:33.840 --> 01:47:35.130
And there's really strong evidence

01:47:35.130 --> 01:47:37.110
that effective management
of those conditions

01:47:37.110 --> 01:47:40.650
in primary care can
significantly reduce harm

01:47:40.650 --> 01:47:44.220
in individual level and demand
for secondary care services.

01:47:44.220 --> 01:47:47.130
And that's why we set up
a number of prevention

01:47:47.130 --> 01:47:49.320
and long-term conditions
management initiatives

01:47:49.320 --> 01:47:51.123
on the back of the long-term plan.

01:47:52.319 --> 01:47:53.152
As Steve said,

01:47:53.152 --> 01:47:57.930
since '21 we've seen significant
non-COVID excess mortality

01:47:57.930 --> 01:48:00.090
and disruption to routine
and emergency care.

01:48:00.090 --> 01:48:02.400
And when we drill down into the drivers

01:48:02.400 --> 01:48:05.220
of non-COVID excess mortality,

01:48:05.220 --> 01:48:08.190
it's primarily CBD and diabetes.

01:48:08.190 --> 01:48:11.340
And then when we look at what's
driving urgent care demand,

01:48:11.340 --> 01:48:13.350
both respiratory and CBD,

01:48:13.350 --> 01:48:15.870
for which diabetes is a major risk factor,

01:48:15.870 --> 01:48:20.040
are both key drivers of demand.

01:48:20.040 --> 01:48:21.900
So we're arguing there are three reasons

01:48:21.900 --> 01:48:25.680
why ICBs and their partners
should prioritise prevention.

01:48:25.680 --> 01:48:27.183
So in the long run,

01:48:28.080 --> 01:48:29.820
if we want to address health inequalities,

01:48:29.820 --> 01:48:31.320
we need to do more to get upstream

01:48:31.320 --> 01:48:33.663
and tackle the causes
of health inequalities.

01:48:34.620 --> 01:48:37.770
There's the urgent emergency care crisis

01:48:37.770 --> 01:48:40.310
and while there's no
one factor drive in that

01:48:40.310 --> 01:48:42.090
it is clear that prevention

01:48:42.090 --> 01:48:43.980
could potentially help to reduce demand

01:48:43.980 --> 01:48:45.660
over the short to medium term.

01:48:45.660 --> 01:48:46.620
And then in the short term,

01:48:46.620 --> 01:48:49.500
if we want to get on
top of excess mortality,

01:48:49.500 --> 01:48:52.440
we need to rapidly recover
preventative services

01:48:52.440 --> 01:48:53.580
and activities.

01:48:53.580 --> 01:48:54.870
So the paper asked the board

01:48:54.870 --> 01:48:57.540
to approve three recommendations.

01:48:57.540 --> 01:48:58.800
Firstly, that NHS England

01:48:58.800 --> 01:49:01.560
should extensively engage with ICBs

01:49:01.560 --> 01:49:03.360
and their partners to build a consensus

01:49:03.360 --> 01:49:06.660
about the need to prioritise
secondary prevention.

01:49:06.660 --> 01:49:07.493
Secondly,

01:49:07.493 --> 01:49:10.170
the ICBs should be supported
to prioritise prevention

01:49:10.170 --> 01:49:12.240
as part of system-wide planning.

01:49:12.240 --> 01:49:15.990
And thirdly, that we
should publish information

01:49:15.990 --> 01:49:18.390
on the highest impact interventions

01:49:18.390 --> 01:49:20.070
relating to the prevention and management

01:49:20.070 --> 01:49:22.770
of CBD diabetes and respiratory.

01:49:22.770 --> 01:49:24.690
And in relation to that,

01:49:24.690 --> 01:49:26.790
we've developed a web-based resource

01:49:26.790 --> 01:49:30.360
and we'll send a link round
after the meeting to people.

01:49:30.360 --> 01:49:31.980
That's just gone live today,

01:49:31.980 --> 01:49:35.760
which is about giving
ICBs and their partners,

01:49:35.760 --> 01:49:37.500
a clear steer on where we think

01:49:37.500 --> 01:49:40.320
the greatest impact could be gained.

01:49:40.320 --> 01:49:41.400
And that's something we've done jointly

01:49:41.400 --> 01:49:42.413
with NICE and with OHED.

01:49:42.413 --> 01:49:45.840
So it's all three organisations
providing that advice.

01:49:45.840 --> 01:49:46.673
- Thank you very much.

01:49:46.673 --> 01:49:48.860
I mean Amanda, I know you want to come in,

01:49:49.939 --> 01:49:50.775
I think Matt's...

01:49:50.775 --> 01:49:51.909
- Is that okay?
- Yep.

01:49:51.909 --> 01:49:53.850
- Thank you very much indeed.

01:49:53.850 --> 01:49:57.450
So just to thank particularly
Steve and Chris for their work

01:49:57.450 --> 01:50:00.450
on this over the summer
and Matt for his support.

01:50:00.450 --> 01:50:02.010
But I think,

01:50:02.010 --> 01:50:04.950
so my honest sense is
nobody needs persuading

01:50:04.950 --> 01:50:07.560
about prevention, it's not a sell,

01:50:07.560 --> 01:50:10.560
everybody understands the
importance of this agenda

01:50:10.560 --> 01:50:12.269
and in fact it's probably, you know,

01:50:12.269 --> 01:50:14.370
one of the areas where if you
talk to colleagues in ICBs

01:50:14.370 --> 01:50:18.750
you get the most engagement
and energy and enthusiasm.

01:50:18.750 --> 01:50:21.090
The challenge for us
exactly as Steve says,

01:50:21.090 --> 01:50:23.460
I think is now that this
first part of the work

01:50:23.460 --> 01:50:25.530
has been done, I'm delighted
to hear it's got live today.

01:50:25.530 --> 01:50:27.360
Look forward to getting the link.

01:50:27.360 --> 01:50:29.010
You know you've said to me many times

01:50:29.010 --> 01:50:31.440
the what is a knowable thing.

01:50:31.440 --> 01:50:34.050
The evidence about what the
critical interventions are,

01:50:34.050 --> 01:50:35.700
you know, it's blood pressure monitoring,

01:50:35.700 --> 01:50:39.330
it's lipid testing, it's AF
identification, et cetera.

01:50:39.330 --> 01:50:41.070
And we know the what,

01:50:41.070 --> 01:50:44.160
but we have got a real
opportunity to build,

01:50:44.160 --> 01:50:45.600
I think particularly on the success

01:50:45.600 --> 01:50:47.610
of the COVID vaccine programme

01:50:47.610 --> 01:50:50.460
to think much more
radically about the how.

01:50:50.460 --> 01:50:53.700
So that's why I really
welcome this next step,

01:50:53.700 --> 01:50:56.790
which is engagement
with ICBs in particular

01:50:56.790 --> 01:50:59.010
because one of the questions
for me in all of this

01:50:59.010 --> 01:51:01.980
is let's be clear what is
the national bit of this

01:51:01.980 --> 01:51:04.170
and then what is absolutely the local bit?

01:51:04.170 --> 01:51:06.750
Nationally it's things like the app,

01:51:06.750 --> 01:51:08.160
you know, what could we be in differently

01:51:08.160 --> 01:51:09.780
using technology to support this,

01:51:09.780 --> 01:51:13.770
to give patients information
about their own risk factors so

01:51:13.770 --> 01:51:16.440
that they can self direct to some of this.

01:51:16.440 --> 01:51:19.290
But the other bit that we do
well nationally, is we share,

01:51:19.290 --> 01:51:21.540
so if you've got some great
practise going on locally,

01:51:21.540 --> 01:51:23.520
a bit like the COVID
vaccination programme, you know,

01:51:23.520 --> 01:51:25.189
oh look we're doing it
in a football stadium,

01:51:25.189 --> 01:51:28.350
we're doing it in a supermarket,
we're doing it in a mosque.

01:51:28.350 --> 01:51:29.583
You know, it's that kind of thing

01:51:29.583 --> 01:51:31.980
that we can capture and share for others

01:51:31.980 --> 01:51:33.180
to then think about

01:51:33.180 --> 01:51:35.280
how they might want to
implement it locally.

01:51:35.280 --> 01:51:36.390
But then there are other things

01:51:36.390 --> 01:51:38.280
where I think we have a national role

01:51:38.280 --> 01:51:39.660
and providing that data,

01:51:39.660 --> 01:51:42.000
particularly the ability
for people to understand

01:51:42.000 --> 01:51:43.440
that own local populations

01:51:43.440 --> 01:51:45.090
and then what the biggest needs are.

01:51:45.090 --> 01:51:47.776
And then they need to, as
we've discovered in COVID,

01:51:47.776 --> 01:51:50.640
you know, work in partnership
with local government,

01:51:50.640 --> 01:51:51.750
with local voluntary sector,

01:51:51.750 --> 01:51:53.430
with patients themselves of course

01:51:53.430 --> 01:51:55.740
to think about how you then deliver.

01:51:55.740 --> 01:51:59.130
Some of that will always be
core component of primary care

01:51:59.130 --> 01:52:00.990
and you know, we need to really respect

01:52:00.990 --> 01:52:02.070
and acknowledge that.

01:52:02.070 --> 01:52:02.903
But some of it,

01:52:02.903 --> 01:52:05.370
I think we can also build on
some of those opportunities

01:52:05.370 --> 01:52:08.430
to think differently about
how we do some of this

01:52:08.430 --> 01:52:09.510
as indeed we are with things

01:52:09.510 --> 01:52:11.490
like community pharmacy right now.

01:52:11.490 --> 01:52:15.540
So for me this is a hugely
exciting opportunity for us

01:52:15.540 --> 01:52:18.000
to again, live what we've described

01:52:18.000 --> 01:52:19.612
in our NHS operating framework,

01:52:19.612 --> 01:52:22.530
which is the working
with the co-production,

01:52:22.530 --> 01:52:25.590
the partnership to take
something that we all recognise,

01:52:25.590 --> 01:52:29.160
I think is a hugely important
element of our work,

01:52:29.160 --> 01:52:31.140
not just for now but for the long term,

01:52:31.140 --> 01:52:33.930
I think really, hopefully
creatively and differently

01:52:33.930 --> 01:52:38.250
about how we can do this in the future.

01:52:38.250 --> 01:52:39.720
- So you clearly have an advocate

01:52:39.720 --> 01:52:42.530
for the approvals you want on the board.

01:52:43.688 --> 01:52:45.630
And I'm going to just
get a couple of comments

01:52:45.630 --> 01:52:46.710
and then give them time.

01:52:46.710 --> 01:52:48.390
So let me go to Wol and then David.

01:52:48.390 --> 01:52:51.783
- So can I just be clear what
being actually asked to do,

01:52:51.783 --> 01:52:54.270
because I can put my
merger committee hat on

01:52:54.270 --> 01:52:55.103
for a minute here

01:52:55.103 --> 01:52:58.890
and go, I think this is
existing programme of work.

01:52:58.890 --> 01:53:01.980
I think that it's suggesting
it needs to then develop

01:53:01.980 --> 01:53:05.280
and grow beyond the merger day.

01:53:05.280 --> 01:53:06.330
So first question,

01:53:06.330 --> 01:53:07.680
is it going to go through the process

01:53:07.680 --> 01:53:09.300
that we have with the design authority

01:53:09.300 --> 01:53:11.670
and understand how that's going to impact?

01:53:11.670 --> 01:53:13.620
So I'm just want to reassure
that's going to happen

01:53:13.620 --> 01:53:15.210
because I can't see how
we're going to prove it

01:53:15.210 --> 01:53:17.850
without the structural making sense.

01:53:17.850 --> 01:53:20.400
The second thing I would
say is that, and Amanda,

01:53:20.400 --> 01:53:22.350
you'll remember at Guys
and Tommy's Foundation,

01:53:22.350 --> 01:53:26.130
we did a lot of this work,
seven or eight years ago

01:53:26.130 --> 01:53:29.640
and I'm delighted the NHS at the centre

01:53:29.640 --> 01:53:32.910
is finally thinking
how can we scale stuff?

01:53:32.910 --> 01:53:34.516
I think that's the hard-
- Scale and spread.

01:53:34.516 --> 01:53:35.580
It's scale and spread.
- I think that's the hard way

01:53:35.580 --> 01:53:37.200
to say, the great ideas out there.

01:53:37.200 --> 01:53:38.940
How can we scale and make that happen?

01:53:38.940 --> 01:53:42.660
My plea would be that we do
this in a really subtle way.

01:53:42.660 --> 01:53:46.408
Sorry, subtle, sensitive
way, out from a co-creation.

01:53:46.408 --> 01:53:48.630
- Exactly.
- Rather than us saying

01:53:48.630 --> 01:53:49.920
this is what you're going to do.
- Exactly.

01:53:49.920 --> 01:53:52.170
- Or us demanding information
as to what you're doing

01:53:52.170 --> 01:53:53.640
and making it really sort of clunky

01:53:53.640 --> 01:53:55.470
and sort of unsustainable approach.

01:53:55.470 --> 01:53:56.710
So I'm really, you know,
'cause there are people

01:53:56.710 --> 01:53:59.490
doing really great work out there already.

01:53:59.490 --> 01:54:03.300
Our role may just be to
coordinate and spread that.

01:54:03.300 --> 01:54:04.713
- Thank you. David?

01:54:04.713 --> 01:54:06.210
- So I really welcome this paper,

01:54:06.210 --> 01:54:08.730
so thank you Matthew and Steve.

01:54:08.730 --> 01:54:11.280
And the reason that I do,

01:54:11.280 --> 01:54:13.800
I think we've done this on a
number of occasions recently

01:54:13.800 --> 01:54:15.560
is take a much more strategic approach

01:54:15.560 --> 01:54:16.890
to the challenges we've got.

01:54:16.890 --> 01:54:19.980
I think the work on
urgent and emergency care,

01:54:19.980 --> 01:54:21.327
I think the stuff we've just had on cancer

01:54:21.327 --> 01:54:24.060
and early diagnosis, are
two examples of that.

01:54:24.060 --> 01:54:25.260
So it's good.

01:54:25.260 --> 01:54:26.700
I think we're doing this.

01:54:26.700 --> 01:54:28.320
If I could just connect it to the report

01:54:28.320 --> 01:54:30.390
that David presented on performance

01:54:30.390 --> 01:54:33.600
where we talk a lot
about flow in hospitals

01:54:33.600 --> 01:54:35.007
and actually flow at the back end.

01:54:35.007 --> 01:54:37.290
And it seems to me that this is about flow

01:54:37.290 --> 01:54:40.530
right at the front end and
about secondary prevention.

01:54:40.530 --> 01:54:41.820
So not primary prevention,

01:54:41.820 --> 01:54:46.820
this is not about the levers
that education and housing

01:54:47.250 --> 01:54:48.900
and employment have on this work,

01:54:48.900 --> 01:54:52.020
but it's about the hands that
we've got on these levers.

01:54:52.020 --> 01:54:54.807
So I just want to commend the work

01:54:54.807 --> 01:54:58.950
and I can pick up the comments
that colleagues are making

01:54:58.950 --> 01:55:01.050
about more details, et cetera.

01:55:01.050 --> 01:55:05.580
But I'm down on Amanda's side
with this about enthusiasm,

01:55:05.580 --> 01:55:07.497
for this is the right thing to do.

01:55:07.497 --> 01:55:10.560
And I don't think we've had
this in our narrative as well

01:55:10.560 --> 01:55:14.700
when we're talking about how
we see services developing.

01:55:14.700 --> 01:55:16.290
I think developing a narrative

01:55:16.290 --> 01:55:17.820
which includes what we're doing

01:55:17.820 --> 01:55:21.903
to prevent secondary prevention, sorry,

01:55:22.980 --> 01:55:24.930
I think is really important.

01:55:24.930 --> 01:55:26.490
And from some other work I've been doing,

01:55:26.490 --> 01:55:28.270
I know that this is a big debate

01:55:30.349 --> 01:55:32.520
in Europe at the moment at
the World Health Organisation

01:55:32.520 --> 01:55:35.340
about how do you get in and
do the secondary prevention.

01:55:35.340 --> 01:55:37.830
So it's preventing things developing

01:55:37.830 --> 01:55:40.260
when people are within the system.

01:55:40.260 --> 01:55:43.497
So I think I really encourage this

01:55:43.497 --> 01:55:44.580
and I don't know what this will mean

01:55:44.580 --> 01:55:45.990
or how you developing in a programme,

01:55:45.990 --> 01:55:47.460
but I just think strategically

01:55:47.460 --> 01:55:49.050
this is absolutely the right thing to do.

01:55:49.050 --> 01:55:50.364
- Thank you. Munir?

01:55:50.364 --> 01:55:52.200
- Yeah, be a really,
really important area,

01:55:52.200 --> 01:55:54.300
I completely agree with the approach,

01:55:54.300 --> 01:55:55.530
locally driven, particularly

01:55:55.530 --> 01:55:58.203
if you want to reach the
hard to reach populations,

01:55:59.070 --> 01:56:00.930
but I think nationally technology

01:56:00.930 --> 01:56:02.100
is really important as well.

01:56:02.100 --> 01:56:04.710
Many of us actually wear smart watches.

01:56:04.710 --> 01:56:06.447
- [Richard] I just
checking my ECG actually.

01:56:06.447 --> 01:56:07.920
- But with consent and so on,

01:56:07.920 --> 01:56:10.337
we could actually link it to the NHS app

01:56:11.460 --> 01:56:13.590
and provide a way of being
able to identify people

01:56:13.590 --> 01:56:15.180
with atrial fibrillation.

01:56:15.180 --> 01:56:16.140
- Yeah, very good.

01:56:16.140 --> 01:56:18.240
Navina, and this is the
last comment and then...

01:56:18.240 --> 01:56:20.190
- I thank you, Chair.

01:56:20.190 --> 01:56:22.610
I think that it is mentioned in the paper,

01:56:22.610 --> 01:56:26.280
but I think we ought to stress
how crucial this will be

01:56:26.280 --> 01:56:28.950
for reducing health
inequalities for our population.

01:56:28.950 --> 01:56:32.370
And that is a big priority
for us and for ICBs.

01:56:32.370 --> 01:56:34.440
- So, I mean, not subject to it.

01:56:34.440 --> 01:56:36.390
I think what you just have to be...

01:56:36.390 --> 01:56:37.443
Yes, Amanda?

01:56:39.467 --> 01:56:42.120
- I get the privilege of
having, thank you Richard.

01:56:42.120 --> 01:56:43.740
No, its just because of what Wol said,

01:56:43.740 --> 01:56:44.573
I thought was probably quite important

01:56:44.573 --> 01:56:45.810
just to close that one down.

01:56:45.810 --> 01:56:47.640
'Cause I think sort of
the whole point for me

01:56:47.640 --> 01:56:49.800
about the work that Steve's
led on this with Chris

01:56:49.800 --> 01:56:53.730
is it is not about necessarily
a big national programme

01:56:53.730 --> 01:56:56.250
that works in a particular way,

01:56:56.250 --> 01:56:58.080
sort of performance
managing local delivery.

01:56:58.080 --> 01:56:59.909
I think that's why I
was trying to call out

01:56:59.909 --> 01:57:02.250
I think some of the things,
that we haven't tested it,

01:57:02.250 --> 01:57:04.770
but I would say probably
would lend themselves

01:57:04.770 --> 01:57:06.720
to a national, so tech, it's the data,

01:57:06.720 --> 01:57:08.400
it's the sharing of best practise.

01:57:08.400 --> 01:57:12.360
But it's I think a really big opportunity

01:57:12.360 --> 01:57:16.560
for us to use this as a sort
of worked example almost

01:57:16.560 --> 01:57:19.320
of a different way of working,
working with local systems.

01:57:19.320 --> 01:57:21.270
So it's not about us
having lots of, you know,

01:57:21.270 --> 01:57:22.410
pulling things to the centre.

01:57:22.410 --> 01:57:24.900
Actually this one will be
much more about actually

01:57:24.900 --> 01:57:29.430
our supporting local systems
to do things for themselves

01:57:29.430 --> 01:57:31.830
and exactly the way that we
did with COVID vaccination,

01:57:31.830 --> 01:57:33.600
which doesn't mean we don't
need to have some structure

01:57:33.600 --> 01:57:36.780
in the centre, of course, and
that I know is part, Mark,

01:57:36.780 --> 01:57:40.593
of what you've been working
through with the teams on this.

01:57:41.550 --> 01:57:42.383
- Just to be clear,

01:57:42.383 --> 01:57:45.030
we are taking the
opportunity as part of this

01:57:45.030 --> 01:57:46.350
to rearrange our programmes

01:57:46.350 --> 01:57:47.787
as part of the NH then being approved.

01:57:47.787 --> 01:57:49.713
And the scale thing is interesting
because one of the things

01:57:49.713 --> 01:57:52.410
that I think we can also do nationally

01:57:52.410 --> 01:57:54.450
is provide scale on things
such as procurement.

01:57:54.450 --> 01:57:56.100
We can procure some of these programmes

01:57:56.100 --> 01:57:58.200
that are much better cost nationally

01:57:58.200 --> 01:57:59.970
and then offer them out for local systems

01:57:59.970 --> 01:58:02.119
to use in a variety of ways.

01:58:02.119 --> 01:58:05.190
So getting that sweet spot
between what we can do nationally

01:58:05.190 --> 01:58:07.260
and what should be done locally

01:58:07.260 --> 01:58:08.490
is a really important .

01:58:08.490 --> 01:58:09.780
- So one of the most fascinating things

01:58:09.780 --> 01:58:11.730
about looking at multi conditions

01:58:11.730 --> 01:58:14.070
and particularly going from,
I think the report we did

01:58:14.070 --> 01:58:16.800
seven years ago from one to many.

01:58:16.800 --> 01:58:20.703
You know, that sort of, yeah,
is it's actually hyperlocal.

01:58:21.780 --> 01:58:23.380
And actually national, you know,

01:58:24.596 --> 01:58:25.500
if you are Lambert and Suffolk,

01:58:25.500 --> 01:58:27.860
it's completely different if
you are Birmingham or whatever.

01:58:27.860 --> 01:58:31.020
So actually national may not
have a significant role to play

01:58:31.020 --> 01:58:32.730
apart from rightly called out,

01:58:32.730 --> 01:58:35.774
data tech and that sort of
thing, that I completely buy.

01:58:35.774 --> 01:58:37.832
- So I'm going-
- population

01:58:37.832 --> 01:58:39.300
of health management at large, isn't it?

01:58:39.300 --> 01:58:41.280
It needs to be built
around local populations,

01:58:41.280 --> 01:58:44.160
but nationally you do
products, innovation tools.

01:58:44.160 --> 01:58:46.350
That look at.

01:58:46.350 --> 01:58:48.063
- So completely lost any control.

01:58:50.160 --> 01:58:52.863
I'm there, I did twice.

01:58:53.790 --> 01:58:54.827
So look, thank you.

01:58:54.827 --> 01:58:58.350
Steve, thank you, and
Matt, thank you very much.

01:58:58.350 --> 01:58:59.183
Very good.

01:58:59.183 --> 01:59:01.500
I actually think those last
two items we've discussed,

01:59:01.500 --> 01:59:02.910
whether it was on early diagnosis

01:59:02.910 --> 01:59:05.853
and now actually on prevention,

01:59:07.080 --> 01:59:10.020
imagine making a real step
change in these two areas

01:59:10.020 --> 01:59:12.820
and how actually
transformational it would be

01:59:14.310 --> 01:59:15.300
and they both actually,

01:59:15.300 --> 01:59:17.760
but the thought came to my head

01:59:17.760 --> 01:59:20.190
as we had this second
conversation around prevention

01:59:20.190 --> 01:59:23.550
is clearly actually then as
we think forward to 24/25

01:59:23.550 --> 01:59:26.250
and the primary care and the
relationship and the contract

01:59:26.250 --> 01:59:29.220
and how that should be thought about

01:59:29.220 --> 01:59:32.610
in turns then of actually
incentivizing and so on

01:59:32.610 --> 01:59:36.150
via the ICSs or ICBs into primary.

01:59:36.150 --> 01:59:37.980
This aspect is really, really important.

01:59:37.980 --> 01:59:39.360
So we've got that mechanism

01:59:39.360 --> 01:59:42.330
which will be looming on us quite soon.

01:59:42.330 --> 01:59:44.820
So let me move on.

01:59:44.820 --> 01:59:46.620
So it's item eight I think,

01:59:46.620 --> 01:59:49.020
which is medicine's access and uptake.

01:59:49.020 --> 01:59:51.270
And John, I think you're up.

01:59:51.270 --> 01:59:52.830
- Thank you, Chair.

01:59:52.830 --> 01:59:56.370
So I guess, you know,
where Steve, you know,

01:59:56.370 --> 01:59:58.860
can't prevent and Cally can't diagnose

01:59:58.860 --> 02:00:01.200
early specialised commissioning,
I guess, we'll try.

02:00:01.200 --> 02:00:02.463
It's best to cure.

02:00:04.590 --> 02:00:06.960
The paper you've got before
you updates for board

02:00:06.960 --> 02:00:09.450
on the substantial progress being made

02:00:09.450 --> 02:00:11.700
to support access and
uptake to new medicines

02:00:11.700 --> 02:00:14.550
while securing value
for money for taxpayers.

02:00:14.550 --> 02:00:16.080
And most importantly,

02:00:16.080 --> 02:00:18.930
it highlights the impact
that this focused activity

02:00:18.930 --> 02:00:21.180
is having on patient outcomes.

02:00:21.180 --> 02:00:23.550
And I think it's worth just
acknowledging that this progress

02:00:23.550 --> 02:00:27.060
has been achieved over a
relatively short timeframe.

02:00:27.060 --> 02:00:28.620
For the first four to five years

02:00:28.620 --> 02:00:30.840
following NHS England's establishment,

02:00:30.840 --> 02:00:33.450
we were very much passive recipients

02:00:33.450 --> 02:00:35.040
of NICE recommendations,

02:00:35.040 --> 02:00:37.920
whether they be positive or negative.

02:00:37.920 --> 02:00:40.320
Patient access schemes,
which were the mechanism

02:00:40.320 --> 02:00:42.240
by which pharmaceutical companies

02:00:42.240 --> 02:00:44.970
could submit value propositions to NICE

02:00:44.970 --> 02:00:47.550
with the responsibility of
the Department of Health,

02:00:47.550 --> 02:00:49.410
as was for commercial medicines unit,

02:00:49.410 --> 02:00:51.300
which supports for supply and procurement

02:00:51.300 --> 02:00:53.040
of hospital medicines.

02:00:53.040 --> 02:00:54.960
Responsibility for both those areas

02:00:54.960 --> 02:00:59.220
transferred to NHS England only in 2018.

02:00:59.220 --> 02:01:00.390
And following that,

02:01:00.390 --> 02:01:03.510
we rapidly established a new
commercial medicine's function.

02:01:03.510 --> 02:01:06.607
And I think since then,
overall access and uptake

02:01:06.607 --> 02:01:09.360
has been transformed to such an extent.

02:01:09.360 --> 02:01:12.600
But it is now not
uncommon for NHS patients

02:01:12.600 --> 02:01:15.630
to be amongst the first in
Europe and even the world

02:01:15.630 --> 02:01:20.130
to access many new and innovative drugs.

02:01:20.130 --> 02:01:23.940
The infographic provided
in the paper at annex A

02:01:23.940 --> 02:01:26.250
highlights I think just
some of the successes

02:01:26.250 --> 02:01:27.873
in the last 12 months.

02:01:29.010 --> 02:01:32.100
Importantly, patient impact is being felt

02:01:32.100 --> 02:01:34.680
in a number of areas across cancer,

02:01:34.680 --> 02:01:36.420
rare and less common conditions

02:01:36.420 --> 02:01:39.120
as well as in much larger
patient populations.

02:01:39.120 --> 02:01:41.070
And just to draw out a few examples,

02:01:41.070 --> 02:01:43.830
so in cancer, over 80,000 patients

02:01:43.830 --> 02:01:47.940
have benefited from rapid access
to promising new treatments

02:01:47.940 --> 02:01:50.630
following the reforms we made
to the Cancer Drugs Fund,

02:01:50.630 --> 02:01:53.760
18,000 patients this year alone

02:01:53.760 --> 02:01:55.860
accessing some of those treatments.

02:01:55.860 --> 02:01:59.100
And you'll see in annex B further detail

02:01:59.100 --> 02:02:02.490
on how that is actually
impacting on survival rates.

02:02:02.490 --> 02:02:04.350
In rare and less common conditions,

02:02:04.350 --> 02:02:07.530
we are seeing some dramatic
improvements in outcomes

02:02:07.530 --> 02:02:09.450
as a result of a commercial access deals

02:02:09.450 --> 02:02:11.130
we have put in place.

02:02:11.130 --> 02:02:14.760
For example, in cystic
fibrosis, over 7,000 people

02:02:14.760 --> 02:02:19.260
are now accessing life-changing
CFTR modulator therapies.

02:02:19.260 --> 02:02:21.750
This is the fastest and highest uptake

02:02:21.750 --> 02:02:23.580
of any country in the world.

02:02:23.580 --> 02:02:26.280
And these treatments have
led to dramatic improvements

02:02:26.280 --> 02:02:30.090
in patients lung function,
reduced hospital admissions.

02:02:30.090 --> 02:02:32.910
And recent data actually
from the CF registry

02:02:32.910 --> 02:02:35.370
has shown that the last 18 months

02:02:35.370 --> 02:02:37.320
we've seen a doubling
in the pregnancy rate

02:02:37.320 --> 02:02:39.796
amongst people with CF, which is I think,

02:02:39.796 --> 02:02:41.610
you know, really remarkable.

02:02:41.610 --> 02:02:44.340
In other areas like
spinal muscular atrophy,

02:02:44.340 --> 02:02:46.260
very devastating condition,

02:02:46.260 --> 02:02:48.720
we've put in place three
managed access agreements

02:02:48.720 --> 02:02:50.580
for three new treatments,

02:02:50.580 --> 02:02:52.500
which has enabled hundreds of patients

02:02:52.500 --> 02:02:54.750
to benefit for the first ever treatment,

02:02:54.750 --> 02:02:57.780
a breakthrough gene therapy
as well as an oral drug

02:02:57.780 --> 02:02:59.103
that can be taken at home.

02:03:00.150 --> 02:03:01.250
The launch earlier this year

02:03:01.250 --> 02:03:03.000
of the Innovative Medicines Fund

02:03:03.000 --> 02:03:04.950
will further strengthen our ability

02:03:04.950 --> 02:03:09.540
to bring promising but uncertain
treatments to patients.

02:03:09.540 --> 02:03:12.870
And finally, through a
series of smart procurements,

02:03:12.870 --> 02:03:14.730
we've expanded access to treatments

02:03:14.730 --> 02:03:16.530
in much larger patient populations,

02:03:16.530 --> 02:03:19.680
particularly in areas of
cardiovascular disease,

02:03:19.680 --> 02:03:22.320
HIV and hepatitis C.

02:03:22.320 --> 02:03:26.070
Maybe if I focus on HIV and hepatitis C.

02:03:26.070 --> 02:03:28.860
So firstly in HIV and
of course worth noting

02:03:28.860 --> 02:03:31.770
that it is World AIDS Day today,

02:03:31.770 --> 02:03:33.540
we are making huge progress

02:03:33.540 --> 02:03:36.810
towards making England the
first country in the world

02:03:36.810 --> 02:03:39.990
to have no new HIV transmissions.

02:03:39.990 --> 02:03:43.260
A series of national deals
struck by NHS England

02:03:43.260 --> 02:03:46.680
is enabling 87,000 people with HIV

02:03:46.680 --> 02:03:48.420
and over 60,000 people

02:03:48.420 --> 02:03:51.510
considered at high risk
of contracting HIV,

02:03:51.510 --> 02:03:54.630
with access to the latest
preventative treatment.

02:03:54.630 --> 02:03:56.490
And in relation to hepatitis C,

02:03:56.490 --> 02:03:58.860
we remain on track to eliminate for virus

02:03:58.860 --> 02:04:01.743
well ahead of the World
Health Organisation goals.

02:04:03.000 --> 02:04:06.180
The latest data in fact
shows that 70,000 people

02:04:06.180 --> 02:04:09.360
have now been cured from the
potentially fatal disease

02:04:09.360 --> 02:04:13.743
with deaths falling by 35% since 2019.

02:04:14.580 --> 02:04:17.765
Finally, the paper sets out just six areas

02:04:17.765 --> 02:04:19.020
that I think is really important

02:04:19.020 --> 02:04:21.300
for us to focus on going forward.

02:04:21.300 --> 02:04:24.120
Firstly, maintaining
the central role of NICE

02:04:24.120 --> 02:04:26.460
and determining the clinical
and cost effectiveness

02:04:26.460 --> 02:04:29.850
of new medicines, critically important.

02:04:29.850 --> 02:04:34.500
Secondly, we need to
carefully manage overall spend

02:04:34.500 --> 02:04:38.070
in the branded medicines
bill to ensure affordability

02:04:38.070 --> 02:04:40.980
and sustainability for NHS finances.

02:04:40.980 --> 02:04:42.870
Thirdly, we need to drive greater value

02:04:42.870 --> 02:04:45.030
from existing drug spend.

02:04:45.030 --> 02:04:47.160
Fourthly, we need to be supporting

02:04:47.160 --> 02:04:49.380
and embedding medicines optimization

02:04:49.380 --> 02:04:50.973
at all levels in the system.

02:04:51.840 --> 02:04:54.750
Fifthly, we need to take
a proactive approach

02:04:54.750 --> 02:04:57.690
to ensuring clinical services are ready,

02:04:57.690 --> 02:05:01.110
willing and able to adopt
new high impact treatments.

02:05:01.110 --> 02:05:03.000
And of course finally, we need to continue

02:05:03.000 --> 02:05:06.330
to partner with industry
on all of the above,

02:05:06.330 --> 02:05:08.310
recognising that those partnerships

02:05:08.310 --> 02:05:10.140
can go much, much deeper

02:05:10.140 --> 02:05:12.837
than just kind of securing
access to the latest drugs.

02:05:12.837 --> 02:05:14.940
And as Cally said, the GRAIL deal

02:05:14.940 --> 02:05:17.480
is probably a really good example of that.

02:05:17.480 --> 02:05:18.480
So I will stop there.

02:05:18.480 --> 02:05:19.350
I'm happy to take-
- Thank you.

02:05:19.350 --> 02:05:22.440
So pausing to see comments or questions.

02:05:22.440 --> 02:05:24.330
Tim, then Munir I think, yep.

02:05:24.330 --> 02:05:26.809
- Just really quickly to point out,

02:05:26.809 --> 02:05:29.010
and I think you'll agree with me,

02:05:29.010 --> 02:05:33.213
the critical role of patient
advocacy groups in that,

02:05:34.500 --> 02:05:38.269
in their positioning on these
high-impact medications,

02:05:38.269 --> 02:05:40.020
particularly in the CF community,

02:05:40.020 --> 02:05:42.450
I would just say they
had a really big role

02:05:42.450 --> 02:05:44.400
that they played in the whole process

02:05:44.400 --> 02:05:46.710
and shout out to them,

02:05:46.710 --> 02:05:51.710
that's a very important, that
pressure is a good thing.

02:05:51.810 --> 02:05:54.622
- Absolutely, vital role they played.

02:05:54.622 --> 02:05:55.973
- So patient advocacy groups.

02:05:55.973 --> 02:05:57.189
Munir?

02:05:57.189 --> 02:05:59.010
- Thanks, a really great
paper, thank you very much.

02:05:59.010 --> 02:06:01.650
And good to see medicines
being discussed to the board.

02:06:01.650 --> 02:06:03.314
- I knew you would like that.
- For a change.

02:06:04.620 --> 02:06:07.770
- Especially for you Munir.
- Yeah, absolutely.

02:06:07.770 --> 02:06:10.590
So it highlights two things.

02:06:10.590 --> 02:06:12.270
The first is the power of the NHS

02:06:12.270 --> 02:06:15.180
to provide access to novel
therapies to patients.

02:06:15.180 --> 02:06:16.620
Really, really important.

02:06:16.620 --> 02:06:18.900
The second is that it's an exciting time

02:06:18.900 --> 02:06:21.720
that we are moving away
from symptom control

02:06:21.720 --> 02:06:24.210
to cure of diseases with some
of these novel therapies,

02:06:24.210 --> 02:06:27.453
which hasn't happened
before for infections.

02:06:28.320 --> 02:06:30.180
But I'm sure as part of what you're doing,

02:06:30.180 --> 02:06:33.270
you have got a horizon
scanning function and you know,

02:06:33.270 --> 02:06:34.980
you mentioned affordability

02:06:34.980 --> 02:06:37.650
but there are new drugs
coming through all the time

02:06:37.650 --> 02:06:41.040
and you know, a drug
which costs $3.5 million

02:06:41.040 --> 02:06:42.540
has just been announced.

02:06:42.540 --> 02:06:44.580
So can you just give us an idea

02:06:44.580 --> 02:06:46.800
of how you are assessing
affordability and so on

02:06:46.800 --> 02:06:47.790
as you move forward

02:06:47.790 --> 02:06:50.670
as more and more new novel
therapies come through

02:06:50.670 --> 02:06:51.960
which are going to cost more and more.

02:06:51.960 --> 02:06:53.310
Innovation is not cheap.

02:06:53.310 --> 02:06:54.143
- No, absolutely.

02:06:54.143 --> 02:06:57.540
I think there are probably
three important aspects to that.

02:06:57.540 --> 02:06:58.680
So firstly, as I said,

02:06:58.680 --> 02:07:00.990
I think making sure that we're focusing

02:07:00.990 --> 02:07:02.610
on whether these treatments have value

02:07:02.610 --> 02:07:04.290
and by that I mean
whether they're clinically

02:07:04.290 --> 02:07:07.230
and cost effective is
I think the first step

02:07:07.230 --> 02:07:10.410
and that's a critical
role for NICE to play.

02:07:10.410 --> 02:07:14.280
But NHS England has been
playing a really key part

02:07:14.280 --> 02:07:15.780
in supporting companies

02:07:15.780 --> 02:07:18.150
in putting forward value propositions.

02:07:18.150 --> 02:07:20.130
But what I would say on that

02:07:20.130 --> 02:07:23.520
is that drugs do hold a kind of unique

02:07:23.520 --> 02:07:26.160
and privileged position in our system

02:07:26.160 --> 02:07:29.940
in that every positive
recommendation that comes from NICE

02:07:29.940 --> 02:07:31.890
comes with a legal requirement

02:07:31.890 --> 02:07:33.900
on commissioners to fund that.

02:07:33.900 --> 02:07:35.850
And so that makes it, you know,

02:07:35.850 --> 02:07:39.300
really important that we
manage affordability as well.

02:07:39.300 --> 02:07:42.240
Maybe I could just illustrate
why just focusing on clinical

02:07:42.240 --> 02:07:45.630
and cost effectiveness
alone is not enough.

02:07:45.630 --> 02:07:50.630
So the hepatitis C drugs,
direct antiviral drugs,

02:07:51.150 --> 02:07:53.700
probably some of the
most cost effective drugs

02:07:53.700 --> 02:07:55.563
we have seen in decades,

02:07:56.400 --> 02:07:59.490
yet with a phenomenal budget impact.

02:07:59.490 --> 02:08:01.890
So we put in place a deal over five years,

02:08:01.890 --> 02:08:05.400
which is worth in the
region of a billion pounds

02:08:05.400 --> 02:08:08.910
and that has secured very,
very substantial discounts.

02:08:08.910 --> 02:08:11.130
But that just shows you the scale

02:08:11.130 --> 02:08:13.490
of some of your affordability challenges.

02:08:13.490 --> 02:08:15.720
So the second bit of this is, as I said,

02:08:15.720 --> 02:08:19.350
managing overall spend on
the branded medicines bill.

02:08:19.350 --> 02:08:20.940
So underpinning our success

02:08:20.940 --> 02:08:23.760
in building this positive
access environment

02:08:23.760 --> 02:08:26.760
has been the partnership
we forged with industry

02:08:26.760 --> 02:08:28.380
through the Voluntary Scheme

02:08:28.380 --> 02:08:30.960
for Branded Medicines Pricing and Access.

02:08:30.960 --> 02:08:33.810
You may have heard this
referred to as VPAS

02:08:33.810 --> 02:08:36.540
and that current scheme which
was agreed between industry,

02:08:36.540 --> 02:08:41.540
government and NHS England looks
to manage the overall spend

02:08:42.120 --> 02:08:46.560
in branded medicines at the
moment by placing a cap of 2%

02:08:46.560 --> 02:08:51.000
on growth in the medicines
branded spend annually.

02:08:51.000 --> 02:08:54.090
That scheme comes to an
end of the end of next year

02:08:54.090 --> 02:08:55.920
and it will be really important for us

02:08:55.920 --> 02:08:59.490
to make sure that we work with government

02:08:59.490 --> 02:09:02.580
as we look to agree a new
voluntary scheme with industry.

02:09:02.580 --> 02:09:04.770
The final thing to say is obviously

02:09:04.770 --> 02:09:07.620
the opportunities we
have with kind of generic

02:09:07.620 --> 02:09:09.900
and biosimilar entry to the market

02:09:09.900 --> 02:09:12.000
where we got, you know, real opportunities

02:09:12.000 --> 02:09:13.050
to bring down prices.

02:09:13.050 --> 02:09:16.290
I think we reported for
over the last three years

02:09:16.290 --> 02:09:18.237
we had reduced, you know,

02:09:18.237 --> 02:09:21.750
the cost of some of those
drugs by 1.2 billion pounds.

02:09:21.750 --> 02:09:24.327
So I think important progress
and foundations to build on.

02:09:24.327 --> 02:09:25.800
- I'm very conscious of time

02:09:25.800 --> 02:09:27.780
and Steve is sort of desperate

02:09:27.780 --> 02:09:28.613
to make one comment.
- I know

02:09:28.613 --> 02:09:29.446
you're very short of time

02:09:29.446 --> 02:09:30.769
but this is too good an opportunity.

02:09:30.769 --> 02:09:31.920
- Go on.
- You should not be shy

02:09:31.920 --> 02:09:34.257
about just how brilliantly this works.

02:09:34.257 --> 02:09:37.350
And the evolution that we
now got to with a combination

02:09:37.350 --> 02:09:41.130
of MHRA approving NICE
opposing the value very quickly

02:09:41.130 --> 02:09:43.800
and then us being able
to negotiate a price,

02:09:43.800 --> 02:09:46.260
works fantastically well,
so two quick points.

02:09:46.260 --> 02:09:49.680
One, you need to think about
how we take the best of that

02:09:49.680 --> 02:09:52.050
and move it into devices, consumables

02:09:52.050 --> 02:09:53.400
and digital technology,

02:09:53.400 --> 02:09:55.830
not directly transferable
but lots of lessons.

02:09:55.830 --> 02:09:58.980
And secondly, just want to
point out we are trail blazing

02:09:58.980 --> 02:10:01.980
in paragraph 25 on our
approach to innovation

02:10:01.980 --> 02:10:03.750
in antimicrobials.
- Yeah.

02:10:03.750 --> 02:10:06.150
- Other countries are using
slightly different models

02:10:06.150 --> 02:10:08.850
but the fact that we are driving this

02:10:08.850 --> 02:10:11.550
is actually leading
other countries to follow

02:10:11.550 --> 02:10:13.200
and it doesn't really
matter what the model is,

02:10:13.200 --> 02:10:15.300
the fact is that people
are focusing on it.

02:10:15.300 --> 02:10:16.133
- Thank you.

02:10:16.133 --> 02:10:18.870
I find myself sitting corrected again,

02:10:18.870 --> 02:10:22.275
actually, this afternoon on
trying to close that down

02:10:22.275 --> 02:10:25.350
and they were very good points.

02:10:25.350 --> 02:10:27.360
I am going to move us on though,

02:10:27.360 --> 02:10:30.030
and I have heard Julian on the
topic in various discussions

02:10:30.030 --> 02:10:35.030
on VPAS, and particularly as
we head into the new agreements

02:10:36.360 --> 02:10:39.380
that will need to include
in a year or so's time.

02:10:39.380 --> 02:10:41.580
So I'm going to move to the last item,

02:10:41.580 --> 02:10:42.780
which is a really important item

02:10:42.780 --> 02:10:44.940
and I realise we're
running up against time,

02:10:44.940 --> 02:10:47.260
but we've got 10 minutes or so for this

02:10:48.600 --> 02:10:50.250
and it's an update on the integration,

02:10:50.250 --> 02:10:53.430
Mark, of NHS England and
Health Education England

02:10:53.430 --> 02:10:56.160
and NHS Digital and X.

02:10:56.160 --> 02:10:58.320
So if you can give us the highlights

02:10:58.320 --> 02:10:59.790
we discussed it in the
private board already,

02:10:59.790 --> 02:11:02.340
so if you can give us the
highlights that would be great.

02:11:02.340 --> 02:11:03.173
- Chair, thank you.

02:11:03.173 --> 02:11:06.210
So the update I'll give, it will be brief,

02:11:06.210 --> 02:11:07.830
but I'll cover three main elements.

02:11:07.830 --> 02:11:10.500
So the first is as we've been
dividing the programme up,

02:11:10.500 --> 02:11:12.510
there's a legal process that's running

02:11:12.510 --> 02:11:14.820
in order to do the legal mergers

02:11:14.820 --> 02:11:18.270
as we bring NHS Digital into NHS England

02:11:18.270 --> 02:11:22.080
and then Health Education
England into NHS England.

02:11:22.080 --> 02:11:24.660
They're running at two
different time points now

02:11:24.660 --> 02:11:25.740
following the request that's made

02:11:25.740 --> 02:11:29.370
from previous Secretary of
State for an earlier merger.

02:11:29.370 --> 02:11:31.546
And that merger will
happen between NHS Digital

02:11:31.546 --> 02:11:33.900
and NHS England by the 31st of January.

02:11:33.900 --> 02:11:35.520
But we're sticking with the same date

02:11:35.520 --> 02:11:37.743
as planned for Health Education England.

02:11:38.709 --> 02:11:40.290
From a legal perspective,

02:11:40.290 --> 02:11:43.500
all of the work's going on
now and it's in parliament

02:11:43.500 --> 02:11:45.060
about the regulations continuing.

02:11:45.060 --> 02:11:46.260
We're doing huge amounts of work

02:11:46.260 --> 02:11:48.120
around making sure the governance
arrangements are right,

02:11:48.120 --> 02:11:52.890
around how data is overseen
with the data Safe Haven.

02:11:52.890 --> 02:11:55.020
So huge amounts of work
and collaboration's gone on

02:11:55.020 --> 02:11:56.880
between the Department of Health

02:11:56.880 --> 02:12:00.390
but also with NHS Digital
and NHS England team.

02:12:00.390 --> 02:12:02.780
So we've seen some really
good work that's been done

02:12:02.780 --> 02:12:05.610
in the past weeks and months.

02:12:05.610 --> 02:12:07.560
The second element is
about what we're designing

02:12:07.560 --> 02:12:08.393
in the new organisation

02:12:08.393 --> 02:12:10.830
and we're designing a new organisation

02:12:10.830 --> 02:12:13.890
that's going to be not
just focusing on the scale

02:12:13.890 --> 02:12:15.240
and size of the organisation

02:12:15.240 --> 02:12:17.910
but what it's going to be intending to do.

02:12:17.910 --> 02:12:20.370
And that is to make sure
that we are, you know,

02:12:20.370 --> 02:12:22.890
being a really effective system leader,

02:12:22.890 --> 02:12:23.880
speaking with one voice,

02:12:23.880 --> 02:12:25.590
maximising the expertise and talent

02:12:25.590 --> 02:12:29.370
we've got across the board,
simplifying how we work

02:12:29.370 --> 02:12:32.310
and really getting behind
this move to system working

02:12:32.310 --> 02:12:34.590
in a way that's really meaningful

02:12:34.590 --> 02:12:37.920
and uniquely focusing on
the things that we should do

02:12:37.920 --> 02:12:39.360
as the new NHS England,

02:12:39.360 --> 02:12:43.800
not doing a whole breadth of
other aspects of our work.

02:12:43.800 --> 02:12:44.633
So how do we do that?

02:12:44.633 --> 02:12:45.930
We've known from the outset

02:12:45.930 --> 02:12:47.640
we needed to be a smaller organisation.

02:12:47.640 --> 02:12:49.890
So our starting point,
just as a quick reminder,

02:12:49.890 --> 02:12:53.040
so we started off with
the number of funded posts

02:12:53.040 --> 02:12:54.750
across the three organisations,

02:12:54.750 --> 02:12:57.900
being 21 and a half thousand.

02:12:57.900 --> 02:12:59.970
At the time, we gave to the board in July

02:12:59.970 --> 02:13:01.740
to ask for a sign off for the programme.

02:13:01.740 --> 02:13:05.520
We had 19,200 members of staff in post

02:13:05.520 --> 02:13:07.680
and we're moving to a much smaller scale.

02:13:07.680 --> 02:13:11.520
So we will have 30% fewer posts

02:13:11.520 --> 02:13:16.520
to ensure that we are a
smaller, less bureaucratic,

02:13:16.800 --> 02:13:19.230
simplified way of working
across the organisation.

02:13:19.230 --> 02:13:23.940
So we have 30% fewer
posts, so 15,800 posts,

02:13:23.940 --> 02:13:27.810
the time the change completes
and there'll be a further 10%

02:13:27.810 --> 02:13:30.840
where we've got staff
that's moving out into ICBs

02:13:30.840 --> 02:13:32.760
to support some of the delegated functions

02:13:32.760 --> 02:13:35.760
that we'll be transferring
out to the next two years.

02:13:35.760 --> 02:13:37.020
So we're doing huge amounts of work

02:13:37.020 --> 02:13:38.820
through the vertical designs

02:13:38.820 --> 02:13:40.890
to make sure that the directorates

02:13:40.890 --> 02:13:42.720
underpin the structure
of working effectively,

02:13:42.720 --> 02:13:45.810
but importantly how the
horizontal aspects work.

02:13:45.810 --> 02:13:47.970
So we move this silo working

02:13:47.970 --> 02:13:50.400
that we've seen I think historically

02:13:50.400 --> 02:13:53.940
and start to look at how we
coordinate a lot greater,

02:13:53.940 --> 02:13:55.440
the activities that we do inside

02:13:55.440 --> 02:13:57.000
and the activities that we do outside

02:13:57.000 --> 02:13:58.550
to support the rest of the NHS.

02:13:59.427 --> 02:14:01.650
The people change
element's really important

02:14:01.650 --> 02:14:04.080
trying to support our
leadership teams important,

02:14:04.080 --> 02:14:06.090
every member of staff who's
going through this change.

02:14:06.090 --> 02:14:07.740
So when does it all start?

02:14:07.740 --> 02:14:08.970
And I think there are three key things

02:14:08.970 --> 02:14:11.010
that we hear from our staff
about the change, you know,

02:14:11.010 --> 02:14:13.590
three questions that they
really want us to answer.

02:14:13.590 --> 02:14:15.396
First is, when's the change starting?

02:14:15.396 --> 02:14:16.912
You know, when am I going to know

02:14:16.912 --> 02:14:17.988
what's the outcome is for me?

02:14:17.988 --> 02:14:19.290
And the third is, you know,

02:14:19.290 --> 02:14:21.540
we're talking about
voluntary redundancy scheme,

02:14:21.540 --> 02:14:23.310
is that something that
we're going to put in place?

02:14:23.310 --> 02:14:25.380
So we're getting close to the point now

02:14:25.380 --> 02:14:27.120
where we can start to
answer those questions

02:14:27.120 --> 02:14:29.580
for every member of staff.

02:14:29.580 --> 02:14:33.840
So as of February we will
be starting the first wave

02:14:33.840 --> 02:14:36.268
of consultations for our staff

02:14:36.268 --> 02:14:37.350
that are going through the change process.

02:14:37.350 --> 02:14:40.080
We've published the timetable,

02:14:40.080 --> 02:14:42.810
which will ensure that all of our staff

02:14:42.810 --> 02:14:45.180
know the outcome for them personally

02:14:45.180 --> 02:14:47.730
by the end of September next year.

02:14:47.730 --> 02:14:51.742
The whole change programme will
conclude by the end of 23/24

02:14:51.742 --> 02:14:54.197
in order to design an organisation

02:14:54.197 --> 02:14:55.917
that's going to do what we set out

02:14:55.917 --> 02:14:57.240
and what I've just referred to,

02:14:57.240 --> 02:15:00.723
but also to deliver a
smaller organisation as well.

02:15:02.430 --> 02:15:04.530
In February we'll have
11,000 members of staff

02:15:04.530 --> 02:15:05.570
starting the change process.

02:15:05.570 --> 02:15:06.720
So that's significant

02:15:06.720 --> 02:15:08.670
and this is what we're building up to do

02:15:08.670 --> 02:15:11.670
again with a great support
from our colleagues

02:15:11.670 --> 02:15:14.220
and also teams right
across the organisation.

02:15:14.220 --> 02:15:16.470
We're doing huge amounts of work

02:15:16.470 --> 02:15:17.550
in the background at the moment

02:15:17.550 --> 02:15:18.780
on the culture change elements

02:15:18.780 --> 02:15:21.150
that we want to see designed
into the new organisation,

02:15:21.150 --> 02:15:23.335
this new way of work that's
going to underpin the

02:15:23.335 --> 02:15:25.110
effectiveness of how we operate.

02:15:25.110 --> 02:15:26.550
And I'm really grateful for the support

02:15:26.550 --> 02:15:28.980
that we've had from our staff networks

02:15:28.980 --> 02:15:29.813
and also from the trade union colleagues

02:15:29.813 --> 02:15:32.430
who been working collaboratively with us

02:15:32.430 --> 02:15:34.500
throughout all of this.

02:15:34.500 --> 02:15:35.850
The voluntary redundancy scheme's

02:15:35.850 --> 02:15:36.900
really important for us to land.

02:15:36.900 --> 02:15:39.540
You know, it gives better choices,

02:15:39.540 --> 02:15:42.120
you know, for our staff that are affected,

02:15:42.120 --> 02:15:43.560
better value for money for the taxpayer

02:15:43.560 --> 02:15:46.680
and greater opportunity from
a timeliness perspective

02:15:46.680 --> 02:15:48.923
to enact the change and
move to this new structure.

02:15:48.923 --> 02:15:49.870
- [Richard] We need that very soon.

02:15:49.870 --> 02:15:52.080
- Yeah, well, we are in discussions

02:15:52.080 --> 02:15:54.330
with the Department of
Health and Social Care,

02:15:54.330 --> 02:15:55.949
so we've been in discussions
with it for a little while

02:15:55.949 --> 02:15:57.810
and we're just waiting
for the outcome of that

02:15:57.810 --> 02:15:59.670
to be shared with us.

02:15:59.670 --> 02:16:02.220
So that's the broad update, Chair,

02:16:02.220 --> 02:16:04.800
and I'm happy to take any
questions on anything I've said.

02:16:04.800 --> 02:16:07.289
- I'm not going to allow,
I'm going to now stand.

02:16:07.289 --> 02:16:08.550
No, no, I'm not going
to allow any questions.

02:16:08.550 --> 02:16:10.527
I'm going to ask two people to comment.

02:16:10.527 --> 02:16:13.020
And I'm not going to look at
anybody else who's waving at me

02:16:14.010 --> 02:16:15.240
trying to establish authority.

02:16:15.240 --> 02:16:16.717
I'm going to ask Wol and Laura to comment.

02:16:16.717 --> 02:16:18.690
I'm going to actually,
Laura to comment first.

02:16:18.690 --> 02:16:21.780
I'd just like to thank Laura
for her leadership in the board

02:16:21.780 --> 02:16:24.660
on NHS Digital because
that's now coming first

02:16:24.660 --> 02:16:27.390
and it was accelerated into the programme.

02:16:27.390 --> 02:16:29.820
So thank you for your
chairing of that board

02:16:29.820 --> 02:16:31.620
and leadership of it and
engagement in this programme.

02:16:31.620 --> 02:16:33.150
Do you want to make your
comment or ask your question?

02:16:33.150 --> 02:16:35.460
Then I'm going to get Wol in a moment.

02:16:35.460 --> 02:16:38.700
- Well, by the time we meet
again as a public board,

02:16:38.700 --> 02:16:41.310
I will only be wearing one hat.

02:16:41.310 --> 02:16:44.400
So I will no longer be
the chair of NHS Digital

02:16:44.400 --> 02:16:47.730
because it will have ceased to exist.

02:16:47.730 --> 02:16:51.183
And in that context I want
to kind of say three things.

02:16:52.050 --> 02:16:54.000
The first is thank you,

02:16:54.000 --> 02:16:57.810
so thank you to the teams who
are working incredibly well,

02:16:57.810 --> 02:17:00.210
as you've said, Mark, to make that happen,

02:17:00.210 --> 02:17:02.913
particularly to the
accelerated time scale.

02:17:03.930 --> 02:17:06.990
Thank you to my colleagues
on the NHS Digital Board

02:17:06.990 --> 02:17:09.930
and my every single colleague indeed

02:17:09.930 --> 02:17:14.280
for trusting that this
is the right way forward

02:17:14.280 --> 02:17:16.200
and for going with it.

02:17:16.200 --> 02:17:18.090
And thank you to Simon Bolton

02:17:18.090 --> 02:17:21.990
who has led the team through this process.

02:17:21.990 --> 02:17:25.173
So those are my hugely
important thank yous.

02:17:26.190 --> 02:17:28.960
The second thing is I'll
now put my NHSE hat on

02:17:30.030 --> 02:17:34.950
and I want to say to those
people, you are really welcome.

02:17:34.950 --> 02:17:36.690
You are really welcome.

02:17:36.690 --> 02:17:38.010
This organisation,

02:17:38.010 --> 02:17:42.720
this new NHS England
needs you and your skills.

02:17:42.720 --> 02:17:45.150
And that was the heart of
what I was recommending

02:17:45.150 --> 02:17:47.490
in my review, is come join the party

02:17:47.490 --> 02:17:51.033
because we have to transform
the NHS using that capability.

02:17:52.200 --> 02:17:53.910
And then the third thing I want to say

02:17:53.910 --> 02:17:58.910
is the journey starts now,
perhaps on the 31st of January,

02:17:59.007 --> 02:18:00.930
1st of February, which is that actually

02:18:00.930 --> 02:18:03.600
sort of bringing the two
organisations together

02:18:03.600 --> 02:18:05.670
may feel like the hard work right now,

02:18:05.670 --> 02:18:08.430
but actually it's the
beginning of hopefully

02:18:08.430 --> 02:18:11.460
a whole new world where
it's much more automatic

02:18:11.460 --> 02:18:12.900
as Amanda said, that we think

02:18:12.900 --> 02:18:16.080
about how will the app
play into prevention

02:18:16.080 --> 02:18:17.550
at a national scale.

02:18:17.550 --> 02:18:20.160
It's much more automatic
about how we think data

02:18:20.160 --> 02:18:22.650
can play in the elective recovery.

02:18:22.650 --> 02:18:25.770
So those are my three kind of points.

02:18:25.770 --> 02:18:29.460
A thank you, a welcome and whoa,

02:18:29.460 --> 02:18:31.290
let's get going on that journey.

02:18:31.290 --> 02:18:32.123
- Thank you, Laura.

02:18:32.123 --> 02:18:34.043
And if I could just get Wol
to make the final comments

02:18:34.043 --> 02:18:36.720
'cause Mark's led for the executives

02:18:36.720 --> 02:18:38.250
and you've led for the board on this, Wol.

02:18:38.250 --> 02:18:40.890
- Indeed, Laura, I completely
echo your comments,

02:18:40.890 --> 02:18:44.520
the thanks you've given to
people across NHS Digital.

02:18:44.520 --> 02:18:46.890
I felt faintly embarrassed in
being thanked by both Richard

02:18:46.890 --> 02:18:48.300
and Amanda for leading this programme,

02:18:48.300 --> 02:18:51.035
knowing that most of the work
is done by Mark, his team,

02:18:51.035 --> 02:18:53.280
HEE and the people at NHSD.

02:18:53.280 --> 02:18:54.130
But I'll take it.

02:18:55.260 --> 02:18:59.130
No look, I think this is
a highly complex merger.

02:18:59.130 --> 02:19:01.110
It's been ongoing for a long time.

02:19:01.110 --> 02:19:03.780
I think it's important
that we all really focus

02:19:03.780 --> 02:19:04.613
on the thing that matters,

02:19:04.613 --> 02:19:06.180
which is what we're trying to create.

02:19:06.180 --> 02:19:08.340
I know in the press and
various different other forums,

02:19:08.340 --> 02:19:09.960
people focus on the numbers.

02:19:09.960 --> 02:19:11.580
Actually the numbers are the outcome

02:19:11.580 --> 02:19:13.380
of the what we're trying to create,

02:19:13.380 --> 02:19:17.790
a better, leaner deprioritized
NHS England centre

02:19:17.790 --> 02:19:19.920
able to add value to
a system and you know,

02:19:19.920 --> 02:19:23.190
at the end of the day achieve
better outcomes for patients.

02:19:23.190 --> 02:19:25.890
Having said all that, we
are at a critical juncture.

02:19:25.890 --> 02:19:28.110
There are a number of
decisions that need to be taken

02:19:28.110 --> 02:19:31.260
by people outside this organisation,

02:19:31.260 --> 02:19:32.910
which need to happen in a timely fashion

02:19:32.910 --> 02:19:35.910
in order to land all the
different elements properly.

02:19:35.910 --> 02:19:37.500
So I think that it's important

02:19:37.500 --> 02:19:38.790
we just call that out as a board,

02:19:38.790 --> 02:19:40.980
because basically if one or two

02:19:40.980 --> 02:19:43.020
don't quite go the we expect or want

02:19:43.020 --> 02:19:44.520
or would like in the time we'd like,

02:19:44.520 --> 02:19:46.680
then it's going to cause
some interesting issues.

02:19:46.680 --> 02:19:48.360
I'm confident as Mark is,

02:19:48.360 --> 02:19:50.010
and Andrew's very optimistic as well.

02:19:50.010 --> 02:19:52.050
So it's all going to be fine,

02:19:52.050 --> 02:19:53.760
but it is a merger at the end of the day

02:19:53.760 --> 02:19:55.950
and things will shift and change.

02:19:55.950 --> 02:19:58.770
Laura, that you going first as you know

02:19:58.770 --> 02:20:01.830
has caused an interesting
shift in our priorities.

02:20:01.830 --> 02:20:03.900
But again, you know the
work that your team has done

02:20:03.900 --> 02:20:07.200
with Mark and people like Miranda as well

02:20:07.200 --> 02:20:09.150
who's been leading the Day
One Readiness programme

02:20:09.150 --> 02:20:11.850
has been phenomenal in just shifting

02:20:11.850 --> 02:20:13.290
and focusing on getting that right.

02:20:13.290 --> 02:20:16.473
And it is again complex, but
we have not forgotten HEE.

02:20:17.400 --> 02:20:19.950
I'm regularly reminded
this merger was announced

02:20:19.950 --> 02:20:21.959
nearly a year ago, I think, is that right?

02:20:21.959 --> 02:20:23.513
- Over a year.
- Over a year ago,

02:20:23.513 --> 02:20:25.440
and has still got some way to go.

02:20:25.440 --> 02:20:28.170
But it is complex and
in the end of the day,

02:20:28.170 --> 02:20:30.600
I think the focus for
people and the messaging,

02:20:30.600 --> 02:20:32.488
I know that Amanda and
the team are giving,

02:20:32.488 --> 02:20:35.504
is this is one of the most
important things they do.

02:20:35.504 --> 02:20:38.430
And let's not forget,
we've talked about winter,

02:20:38.430 --> 02:20:39.990
we've talked about a
range of things going on

02:20:39.990 --> 02:20:42.393
that are all quite difficult in BAU.

02:20:43.315 --> 02:20:45.090
And at the same time
we're trying to deliver

02:20:45.090 --> 02:20:47.490
a very complex transformation programme

02:20:47.490 --> 02:20:50.400
called the New NHS England Committee,

02:20:50.400 --> 02:20:53.490
sorry, New NHS England
Entity or Organisation.

02:20:53.490 --> 02:20:54.390
It's important to remember

02:20:54.390 --> 02:20:56.040
that both things happen at the same time

02:20:56.040 --> 02:20:58.680
and I really want to thank
everyone and colleagues

02:20:58.680 --> 02:21:03.680
in the NHS England's Organisation
as well as the others

02:21:03.780 --> 02:21:05.640
for all the work they're
doing to make this happen.

02:21:05.640 --> 02:21:07.047
Thank you.
- Well said.

02:21:07.047 --> 02:21:12.047
So I think that is a very good
close and I'm going to pause

02:21:13.380 --> 02:21:16.290
to see if there's any
other business from anyone,

02:21:16.290 --> 02:21:19.260
attendees to the board
or members of the board.

02:21:19.260 --> 02:21:20.093
And if not,

02:21:20.093 --> 02:21:22.110
I'd just like to endorse
what Wol has just said,

02:21:22.110 --> 02:21:24.000
so my thanks to everybody,

02:21:24.000 --> 02:21:26.790
non-exec colleagues and
clearly executive colleague and

02:21:26.790 --> 02:21:29.940
and then all the way down
through the organisation

02:21:29.940 --> 02:21:32.220
and particularly, as
Wol has used that word,

02:21:32.220 --> 02:21:35.070
which is winter, particularly
as we head into winter,

02:21:35.070 --> 02:21:37.950
1st of December today I'm told
it is the first day of winter

02:21:37.950 --> 02:21:41.746
and so you know, we're going
to fight the good fight.

02:21:41.746 --> 02:21:43.320
Thank you very much everybody.

02:21:43.320 --> 02:21:44.153
Very good.

